noi_dung:
{align:justify|The three volumes of Karmic Retribution in the Present Life have been very popular with readers and have been reprinted continuously. Many friends urge me to keep writing. But I think that no matter how much I write, it is still about cause and effect; intelligent readers with bright minds only need to read one or two volumes to clearly understand the Buddha's principles and know how to practice in order to create happiness for themselves and others... As for those without good roots, no matter how much they read they will not practice accordingly — they only admire spiritual powers and crave to know past and future lives, while the essential points — being vegetarian, doing good and refraining from evil, purifying body, speech and mind — receive none of their attention. Not to mention those who read but find it hard to accept, who do not believe and refuse to take any of it in...}
{align:justify|Nowadays there are a great many people who are infatuated with spiritual powers and like to seek outwardly... Most of them run everywhere looking for Master Miao Fa and me. There are even people in Jiangsu, Shijiazhuang, Hebei, Mount Wutai and elsewhere impersonating others and claiming to be Master Miao Fa. (So readers should not go looking to meet him!)}
{align:justify|If you read books without knowing how to turn the light inward (self-reflection), arouse the resolve to repent, and practice according to the teachings, then even if you were to meet Avalokiteshvara Bodhisattva or Maitreya Bodhisattva... it would be of no use, because you would not know how to correct your faults and would refuse to turn back toward goodness...}
{align:justify|To reduce the problem of fraud and impersonation described above, I invited the laywoman Qua Hong and asked her to compile her stories of cultivation and the beneficial things she has seen and heard into a book, to help readers deeply believe in cause and effect, abandon evil and joyfully practice good. It is also meant to remind everyone that the nature of every person already possesses spiritual powers and wisdom (just like laywoman Qua Hong) — there is no need to seek them outside...}
{align:justify|If everyone is willing to follow the Buddha's Dharma, develop moral discipline, meditation and wisdom, and truly cultivate... then they will have sufficient wisdom to guide others. Such "clear-eyed" teachers exist everywhere, a great many of them. You only need to be steadfast: take the precepts as your teacher, and resolve to "cut off all evil, cultivate all good, and practice Chan and Pure Land together" — then in the end you will unlock your own wisdom, and when you "see that the five aggregates are empty, you will pass beyond all suffering and hardship"...}
{align:justify|Written in shame by Qua Khanh}
{align:justify|July 20, 2008}

{align:justify|As a tiny child, I often stood before the mirror wondering: "Who is this person in the mirror? Is it me?"... yet it changes from birth to death following impermanence — so where am I really? This question haunted me and consumed my thoughts for many years, until I came to know Buddhism and finally understood clearly: "The me in the mirror is only a physical body, an outer shell (like a house), while the master of the house is the real me, which from the beginning has always been fully endowed with Buddha nature."}
{align:justify|From then on, from the depths of my heart, I thirsted to discover the eternal, unchanging truth of the universe and human life within the Buddha Dharma.}
{align:justify|When I was 18, a craze for qigong swept the country, praised as the ideal way to "train the body and cultivate health." I joined in and learned qigong, and among the schools I found "Stillness Qigong" the best and most suitable, because it taught the recitation: "Nam mô Quan Thế Âm Bồ-tát". Perhaps this was a condition from a past life, for as soon as I heard this name my body and mind felt at ease and my spirit lifted.}
{align:justify|So every day, morning and evening, I recited His name and diligently practiced sitting meditation. The empowering support of Avalokiteshvara Bodhisattva is truly inconceivable — "in a thousand places sought, in a thousand places answered." He helped me pass through difficult trials in life several times, which only strengthened my firm faith in reciting His name.}
{align:justify|From then on, wherever I went and whatever I did, as soon as I stepped out the door I would recite the name of Avalokiteshvara Buddha (silently or aloud), and I also quietly resolved to study and emulate the great compassion of Avalokiteshvara Bodhisattva (cultivating diligently to benefit sentient beings).}
{align:justify|The person who guided me in qigong and set my feet on the true path of Buddhist study was my benevolent teacher, the layman Qua Khanh.}
{align:justify|The first time I recited the Diamond Sutra, when I read these lines:}
{align:justify|All conditioned phenomena}
{align:justify|Are like dreams, illusions, bubbles and shadows,}
{align:justify|Like dew and lightning —}
{align:justify|Contemplate them thus...}
{align:justify|my heart was shaken to the core; a hundred emotions tangled together and I burst into tears. I wept because for so many years I had been lost in the cycle of the six realms, forgetting my original face, like a lost child with no one to help — no matter how hard I tried, I could not find the way home. Now, through a wholesome affinity, I had encountered the Buddha Dharma, and my mind lit up as though a dense forest of weeds had been cleared away and I had found the true direction for this human life...}
{align:justify|I told myself I must seize this moment, turn my steps homeward, and cultivate with all my strength to soon reach awakening...}
{align:justify|Under the guidance of my benevolent teacher Qua Khanh, I began reciting the "Four Clear and Unalterable Instructions on Purity" in the Shurangama Sutra, reciting the Shurangama Mantra, and prostrating to Great Master Hsuan Hua. Although he had already entered nirvana, he once declared: "Those who have never met me but aspire to become my disciples may sincerely bow to me ten thousand times, and they can become my disciples." And so I bowed with complete sincerity... my vow fulfilled, I became a disciple of Master Hsuan Hua.}
{align:justify|Master Hsuan Hua is the eminent monk I revere most — a teacher who always sacrificed himself for others, led by personal example, and strictly upheld the precepts. The six principles of cultivation he laid down — "no fighting, no greed, no seeking, no selfishness, no pursuing personal advantage, no lying"... became the standard rules of my practice, helping me watch over every arising thought of my own mind.}
{align:justify|The Avatamsaka Sutra, the Buddha's Pratyutpanna Samadhi Sutra, the Mahayana sutras and so on... all remind us to regard our teacher as we would the Buddha. While prostrating to Master Hsuan Hua, I dreamed many times of receiving the support of Avalokiteshvara Bodhisattva, just as the Universal Gate Chapter describes Avalokiteshvara Bodhisattva; I felt my mind utterly pure, and so I have always regarded Master Hsuan Hua as a manifestation of Avalokiteshvara Bodhisattva.}
{align:justify|Reading Master Hsuan Hua's explanation of the "Four Clear and Unalterable Instructions on Purity" helped me understand that the four foundations of cultivation are to cut off lust, killing, stealing and lying... These four are closely interrelated. If you break the precept against lust, you will easily break the precept against killing, and just as easily break the precepts against stealing and lying... To transcend the three realms, you must cultivate within every arising thought and strictly uphold the precepts...}
{align:justify|The sutras record a great deal about the vital importance of the precepts. More than 2,500 years ago, before Shakyamuni Buddha entered nirvana, Ananda asked him:}
{align:justify|"While the Buddha is in the world, everyone takes the Buddha as teacher. After the Buddha enters nirvana, whom shall we take as teacher?"}
{align:justify|The Buddha gently answered: "After I enter nirvana, take the precepts as your teacher!"...}
{align:justify|This shows how important the precepts are. Therefore we must keep ourselves under tight control, maintain proper knowledge and views, break through layers of delusion, recover our pure original face, and realize unsurpassed Buddhahood.}
{align:justify|Buddhism says: "To know the causes of past lives, look at what you experience in this life. To know the results of future lives, look at what you plant in the present."}
{align:justify|Today morals decline by the day and people's hearts are not what they once were; this is an age of materialism and money, when for fame and self-interest people pile up evil karma such as killing, sexual misconduct, stealing, lying and cheating... and so karmic retribution in the present life follows. Because people live recklessly and wrongly, they meet with calamities: car crashes, plane crashes, earthquakes, tsunamis, fires, floods, storms and so on... leaving all who witness them trembling with fear...}
{align:justify|Before such terrifying disasters, we should reflect: "Who is destroying the ecological balance and ravaging the natural environment? Why is it that in an age of ever-advancing science and technology, we are so helpless before nature's punishment?"...}
{align:justify|We need to wake up: this is nature's warning to us. If humanity keeps living wildly without restraint, in the end it will only... bury itself.}
{align:justify|How can we build a harmonious homeland where humanity lives in happiness? How can we avoid natural and man-made disasters and live and work in peace? All of these answers can be found in the Buddha Dharma.}
{align:justify|The Buddha always reminds us: "Cut off evil and cultivate good; no killing, stealing, sexual misconduct or lying; cut off self-serving thoughts; reform the habitual behaviors that create faults"... only then can we be happy and peaceful, and people will naturally live together in harmony and all of society can develop well.}
{align:justify|Because in recent years I have gained such benefit in Buddhist study, my fellow lay practitioners and I all deeply feel how wondrous and inconceivable the Buddha Dharma is, and that karmic retribution never errs by even a hair. So we have written down the simple things we have seen and heard in a collection, hoping that through it everyone will deeply believe in cause and effect and abandon evil for good.}
{align:justify|This book is written in the hope that fellow practitioners who read it will firm up their faith in Buddhist study, constantly examine and control themselves, and practice according to the Buddha Dharma. Use your own "true cultivation and real attainment" to transform your destiny, leave suffering behind for joy, accomplish the Buddha Way — and no longer "seek the Dharma outside the mind," with no need to rush about everywhere chasing after someone who understands cause and effect or possesses spiritual powers.}
{align:justify|We must remember well and engrave on our bones the teaching of the Diamond Sutra: "All that has form is illusory." All of the Buddha's teachings exist to cure the sicknesses of sentient beings; they were established because of the delusions of sentient beings, which is why it is said: "Even the Dharma must be let go of, how much more what is not the Dharma."}
{align:justify|For the people, places and names connected with the characters in these articles... we have all used pseudonyms, aliases or anonymity. Moreover, owing to my own limited ability, the language and writing in this book surely still have many shortcomings. I earnestly beg all good and wise fellow practitioners for forgiveness and guidance.}
{align:justify|Respectfully written by the junior student Qua Hong}

{align:justify|As Qua Khanh always reminds us: readers should not seek outwardly or rush about trying to meet Master Miao Fa and him at all costs (it is far more helpful to practice what Master Miao Fa teaches). But amusingly, there are still readers so confused as to think that I could help them meet Master Miao Fa. I am only the translator of this work, conveying stories of cause and effect for you to read.}
{align:justify|Reading "Karmic Retribution in the Present Life, volumes 1 and 2," we see what a transcendent and wonderful figure Master Miao Fa is (through Qua Khanh's descriptive pen). At that time the author Qua Khanh was only a beginner newly come to the faith, without much experience.}
{align:justify|By now (through the works I have read) I know that after studying under Master Miao Fa, the layman Qua Khanh — though cultivating as a layman — practices the Buddha Dharma with great care, has gained very deep spiritual experience and an extremely powerful ability to inspire others... to the point that many people, after meeting him just once, resolved to cut off evil and practice good, strictly uphold the Buddha's precepts, and vow to become vegetarian for life... He is praised by all as a most kind and humble sage of profound cultivation...}
{align:justify|As for the laywoman Qua Hong, who first studied under the layman Qua Khanh and was guided and led by him... she single-mindedly practiced the Buddha Dharma and became a disciple of Master Hsuan Hua. Though a young laywoman cultivating at home, thanks to her strict observance of the precepts and extremely diligent practice, she has opened the wisdom eye and attained knowledge of past lives, with the ability to see through the three periods of time. Though she possesses spiritual powers, she does not use them to show off, but helps untie people's knots as conditions allow, helping them cut off bad habits and advance in cultivation.}
{align:justify|In her personal account, Qua Hong says little about her own abilities. But as I understand it, whenever someone comes to her with questions... whatever they did in the past or in many previous lives appears before her eyes like a film reel... And she clearly sees how their past or present wrongdoings will cause them to suffer retribution in the future... At times, when something needs to be corrected at once for fuller perfection but the person does not know or recognize it... she will point out what to fix... That is why her guidance is usually solemn, frank and decisive. She never flatters, but aims to expose faults so that the person corrects them... while Qua Khanh also has the ability to see through the past, though his constructive style is gentler.}
{align:justify|Though Qua Hong's ability is strong and surpasses Qua Khanh's, she deeply reveres Qua Khanh and always follows his direction. Sometimes only when Qua Khanh himself encourages and urges her will she speak the truth. If readers wonder why Qua Hong, as a laywoman, developed spiritual powers so early, one may say: it depends on the depth of each individual's cultivation, on their moral discipline, meditation and wisdom. Master Hsuan Hua once said: "Everyone has spiritual powers; when the mind is pure, their wondrous functions naturally appear"... But spiritual powers become dangerous to their owner (if pride, greed, anger and delusion still remain in the heart, for such a person will use them to feed the ego, and will very easily stray into the deviant). The highest spiritual power praised by the Buddha is the "extinction of outflows" — the complete cleansing of afflictions. So instead of craving spiritual powers, we should cultivate a pure mind. When the lake of the mind is clear, all phenomena appear of themselves and wondrous functions unfold...}
{align:justify|In the Ksitigarbha Sutra, first chapter, the Brahman woman is advised by the Buddha "Flower of Meditation and King of Self-Mastery Thus Come One" to sincerely recite His name, and she will then know where her mother was reborn... She did exactly that day and night, and suddenly saw her own body descend into the hells... She reached the hells through the power of concentration — while Bright Eyes (at the end of the first chapter of the Ksitigarbha Sutra), who saw the Buddha at midnight, did so through a dream rather than through concentration. Thus Qua Hong's ability to see through the three periods belongs to the power of concentration. The Buddha once said that he and the Bodhisattvas often manifest hundreds of thousands of kotis of bodies in every form: gods, humans, dragons, monks, nuns, laymen, laywomen... even transforming into grasses, trees, mountains and rivers... in order to guide sentient beings into the Way according to conditions...}
{align:justify|Though Qua Khanh and Qua Hong are only laypeople, the scope of their salvation work commands our reverence, for they can help people transform powerfully and reform themselves extremely well. This inspiring power is not something just anyone can achieve, and what is precious is that they never promote or boast about spiritual powers or themselves.}
{align:justify|Readers may find the stories told in this book outlandish, hard to believe, or may not believe them at all. I too once found them hard to believe...}
{align:justify|The Buddha reminded us: do not hastily believe anything, even the words of saints, the words of the sutras, or what has been handed down since ancient times, unless we have examined and tested it with wisdom...}
{align:justify|The Buddha does not advise us to blind faith (superstition), but to look at things with wisdom and follow this principle: what benefits others and oneself and harms no one should be done. What benefits oneself or others but harms someone else should not be done.}
{align:justify|All the stories here were written and translated for the happiness of readers — whether to believe them and how to receive them is up to you.}
{align:justify|When traveling on the road, if you do not know the traffic laws and do not obey them, you are liable to have an accident.}
{align:justify|In a nation, if you do not know the law and violate national law, you are liable to be punished or even imprisoned...}
{align:justify|In a human life, if you do not understand or master the law of cause and effect, you will unknowingly commit faults, live wrongly, and fall into the three evil paths...}
{align:justify|What deserves attention is... that disciples of the Buddha who genuinely practice the Buddha Dharma will never lie, so the stories told here are always worthy of respect and trust (by the power of Buddhists' truthful speech). Moreover their contents hold many precious lessons well worth our reflection...}
{align:justify|Since monastics already know the Buddha Dharma, understand the law of cause and effect, and know how to become vegetarian and cultivate, they can enjoy the bliss of the Dharma even without this book... But laypeople with hair, who do not yet know the faith, are not versed in the law of cause and effect and understand little... (they can so easily do things that harm themselves without knowing it)... Since the cautionary, knot-untying stories here mostly concern laypeople with hair... To remind them, Qua Hong often quotes the Buddha Dharma (necessary for those who do not yet know, though those who already know too much Dharma may find it tedious reading)... That is why I often joke that I translate for laypeople with hair to read, while monastics only "read along"!}
{align:justify|Note: the phrase "studying Buddhism" often used by the authors in this book does not simply mean studying doctrine or researching the Dharma; it includes actual practice and cultivation — becoming vegetarian, reciting sutras, following the Buddha's example, practicing the Buddha's conduct, arousing vast resolve, and benefiting sentient beings...}
{align:justify|This translation is divided into two parts:}
{align:justify|Part 1 consists of stories written by Qua Hong; only the "Further explanation" at the end of each story is by Qua Khanh.}
{align:justify|Part 2 consists of experiences related by monastics or laypeople in their own words, shared with readers.}
{align:justify|I wish to send my gratitude to Hong Minh Chau, Co Nguyen Tam, Dieu An, Duong Bich Thuy, Lieu Thong... and to so many who quietly supported me. Thanks to Ut Chien and Chanh Dao, who enthusiastically helped fix up my computer so I could complete this work well.}
{align:justify|I dedicate the merit of this work to all benefactors, to the soul of Carol Chi Phan... and to everyone present in this world.}
{align:justify|I also thank readers for their sincere sharing. If "Karmic Retribution in the Present Life 1 and 2" helped you understand cause and effect, then this volume 3 has helped readers strengthen their faith, awaken, and more strongly resolve to become vegetarian. This is truly boundless encouragement for the translator.}
{align:justify|I wish you lasting peace and happiness, and may your wisdom and compassion always shine so that together we build a beautiful human realm.}
{align:justify|Finally, please forgive this translator's errors.}
{align:justify|Hanh Doan — March 29, 2013}
{align:justify|Part 1}

{align:justify|In the Long Agama Sutra, the Buddha teaches:}
{align:justify|"In this world a great many people do not know that the law of cause and effect and the six realms of rebirth exist. They do not believe that ghosts and spirits exist in the world, nor do they believe in Buddhas and Bodhisattvas, and still less do they believe in karmic retribution.}
{align:justify|Because these ordinary beings lack the wisdom and spiritual powers of a Buddha. Though they have fleshly eyes, they are completely blind, unable to see through the true nature of the universe and all things"...}
{align:justify|Even so, we can still rely on the perfect, ultimate wisdom of the Buddha preserved in the sutras to gain a rough understanding of a few things. Here I present a passage of the Buddha's teaching from the Long Agama Sutra concerning ghosts and spirits; after reading it you will clearly understand the realms, states and conditions of the various ghosts and spirits, and will also understand why the circumstances of evil people and good people differ so greatly.}
{align:justify|"The Buddha told the monks:}
{align:justify|- All houses where people dwell have ghosts and spirits; they are everywhere. Every alley, avenue, slaughterhouse, shop, market and mound... all have ghosts and spirits; no place is empty... Ghosts and spirits usually take their names from the places they lodge in: lodging with people they take human names, lodging in a village they take the village's name, lodging in a city they take the city's name, lodging in a country they take the country's name, lodging on land they take the land's name, lodging on a mountain they take the mountain's name, lodging by a river they take the river's name...}
{align:justify|The Buddha told the monks:}
{align:justify|- Every tree, even one as small as a cart axle, has ghosts and spirits attached to it; there is nowhere they are not. Whenever a boy or girl is born, ghosts and spirits follow to guard them. When their death comes, those guardian ghosts gather in their vital energies, and so they must die.}
{align:justify|The Buddha told the monks: "Suppose a non-Buddhist Brahman asks: 'Venerable sir, if whenever a boy or girl is born ghosts and spirits follow to guard them, and when they must die the guardian ghosts gather in their vital energies so they die at once — then why is it that in this life some people are harassed by ghosts and spirits, while others are not?'... Then, O monks, answer them thus:}
{align:justify|- People of the world constantly break the law, do many things contrary to the Way and the Dharma, create the ten evil deeds, and hold perverse, upside-down views... It is like people of this world: though there be a hundred or a thousand people... only one spirit watches over them (like a flock of sheep: though there be hundreds of thousands, one shepherd suffices).}
{align:justify|But as for practitioners who always uphold wholesome dharmas, see correctly and truly, believe in and practice all ten good deeds... though such a person be but one alone, as many as hundreds of thousands of spirits follow to guard them (just as kings and great ministers are guarded by hundreds of thousands of followers... and those who practice good enjoy the same). For this reason, some people of the world are harassed by ghosts and spirits while others never are... thanks to the protection of good spirits!}
{align:justify|Having read this sutra passage of the Buddha's explanation, we can thoroughly understand "the relationship between ghosts and spirits, human beings, mountains, rivers, the earth, trees, flowers and grasses... in nature." It also clearly explains why evil people are often harassed by ghosts and spirits, while good people are usually protected by ghosts and spirits and kept from harm...}
{align:justify|This volume of "Karmic Retribution in the Present Life" records and lists a whole series of real cause-and-effect experiences that we and many others have undergone, which also prove that what the Buddha taught is true speech. Stories such as "The Kindness of the Xiangba Tree" and "The Young Gentleman in Blue"... (see Karmic Retribution in the Present Life, volume 3) are all true accounts proving that "spirits lodge in trees, grasses and flowers and manifest in all kinds of forms"... exactly as the Buddha once said in the sutras: "ghosts and spirits lodge in grasses and trees, taking their names from them"...}
{align:justify|Once we have understood this principle clearly, we must know that ghosts and spirits see and know all of our deeds and actions! (How much more so the exalted ones of profound wisdom such as Buddhas and Bodhisattvas?)... Then why do people of the world not act with caution and follow the Buddha's words in practicing according to the teachings?}

{align:justify|One evening around 8 p.m. in 1997, I had just gotten off work and hurried onto the late bus back to the inner city. There were very few people on the bus then; halfway along, four dead-drunk young men boarded. Each held a bottle of liquor, their clothes disheveled — plainly "drunk as skunks." One of them, seeing me sitting alone, staggered over and sat down beside me, casually draping an arm over the back of my seat; the other three drunks rushed up and sat down surrounding me.}
{align:justify|At that moment I felt extremely uneasy, a little queasy inside, but I forced myself to stay calm and sternly told the young man sitting next to me to take his hand down...}
{align:justify|As a result, not only did he refuse to listen, he reacted even more brazenly (by throwing his arm around my shoulder and laughing loudly). He was unmistakably a hoodlum! I wanted to change seats but he would not let me out.}
{align:justify|I called out to the ticket seller for help, shouting at the top of my voice: "Ticket seller, hey!"...}
{align:justify|The ticket seller slowly walked over; seeing this, the hoodlum darkened his face, hastily whipped out money to buy five tickets, and pointing at me, shamelessly declared:}
{align:justify|"This young lady is with us — we're all together!"}
{align:justify|Hearing that, I truly... had a mouth but could hardly speak, yet I still strained to explain:}
{align:justify|"I don't know them... don't take their money — let me buy my own ticket!"}
{align:justify|With that, I glanced at the ticket seller, silently begging for help... but he seemed too frightened of these thugs; he gave me a quick look, retreated to his own spot at once, and just played dumb, acting as if he had heard nothing!}
{align:justify|Left alone with the hoodlums... with no one to protect me, in that moment of extreme danger I could only pray that the Bodhisattva would save me. I deeply believe that Avalokiteshvara Bodhisattva "answers every sincere plea" and in times of peril bestows inconceivable shelter and protection!}
{align:justify|Alone in the dark of night with no one to help, I folded my palms and silently recited: "Nam mô Quan Thế Âm Bồ-tát"... I single-mindedly pleaded with Him to shield me and rescue me from danger. Gradually my mind grew calm; I was no longer tense, nor afraid at all, for in that very moment mind and the Buddha's name had become one.}
{align:justify|About fifteen minutes later, while I was concentrating wholeheartedly on reciting the name of Avalokiteshvara Bodhisattva, the drunks who had been aggressively closing in on me... suddenly bellowed at the top of their lungs:}
{align:justify|"DRIVER! STOP THE BUS!..."}
{align:justify|"What's the matter? What a nuisance! The bus is now on the highway — it can't stop!" the driver replied irritably.}
{align:justify|"We really can't hold it, can't hold it any longer!... Stop the bus! STOP RIGHT NOW!"}
{align:justify|The one sitting next to me spoke with an utterly miserable look...}
{align:justify|At this point the remaining drunks also jumped up in a fluster, all shouting in a panic:}
{align:justify|"We need to pee so badly, can't hold it any more — stop the bus! WE WANT TO GET OFF!"}
{align:justify|As soon as the driver stopped, the four drunks tumbled off onto the road and vanished into the night...}
{align:justify|The driver cursed under his breath:}
{align:justify|"Nutcases!"}
{align:justify|And drove on.}
{align:justify|I breathed a sigh of relief. The air on the bus was quiet again. I thanked Avalokiteshvara Bodhisattva with all my heart for His unseen support; it was His compassionate vow-power that helped me "turn danger into safety." Just as the Universal Gate Chapter says: "Sentient beings beset by hardship, boundless suffering pressing upon their bodies — Guanyin's wondrous wisdom-power can save the world from suffering"...}
{align:justify|Having received such grace in my hour of calamity, I venture to share it with those who have an affinity: I want to say that if you are in danger, lonely and helpless with no one to shelter you... then sincerely recite the name of Avalokiteshvara Bodhisattva. As long as you are wholehearted and sincere, you will surely receive His inconceivable empowering support.}

{align:justify|Layman Truong studies Buddhism with great diligence — for over a decade, day after day, strictly upholding the precepts, reciting sutras and repenting, organizing life releases, printing sutras regularly... Whatever benefits sentient beings, he wholeheartedly devotes himself to; he is a genuine lay Buddhist practitioner.}
{align:justify|In recent years, thanks to wholeheartedly giving and broadly planting fields of merit, his "household appliances" business has prospered extremely well, accumulating great wealth and a rich, comfortable life.}
{align:justify|Early this year, a friend suggested he play the stock market to grow his assets further. Hearing this, Layman Truong was delighted: if he could earn more money through the stock market and use it for the benefit of sentient beings, would that not kill two birds with one stone? The moment this thought arose, that very night in a dream a Dharma-protecting spirit at once admonished him: "A practitioner should not play the stock market"...}
{align:justify|Upon waking, the Dharma protector's words kept ringing in his ears and set Layman Truong thinking deeply; then it suddenly dawned on him: "To cultivate for liberation from the three realms, a true practitioner should not play stocks! For stocks constantly rise and plunge, and the stock market is full of risks (individuals and groups alike harbor a gambler's mentality, thirsting to make big money in it)... unwittingly feeding one's own desires and bottomless greed. Such a greedy mind can gravely harm one's spiritual health, so for a practitioner it does more harm than good... Playing stocks forces you to worry about a great many things, and afflictions arise accordingly; depending on whether the stock market shakes or slides, the mind grows uneasy, sinking into calculations about money and getting stuck in them. Once the mind is constantly fearful and tangled, it is hard to maintain concentration and hard to keep peace and joy.}
{align:justify|Just consider: if a practitioner's mind is always agitated, always calculating about money (fretting over the tide of stocks), enslaved by gain and loss like that, how could they possibly "go beyond distorted dream-thinking and reach ultimate nirvana?" Of course, if you have already smashed all attachments, there is nothing to say.}
{align:justify|Therefore I sincerely warn all Dharma friends here: as genuine practitioners, you should not play the stock market.}
{align:justify|Further explanation: Conditions arise and conditions cease; this body of ours holds ten thousand sufferings, and for food, clothing and shelter we give rise to piled-up greedy thoughts every hour of every day. A disciple of the Buddha needs only pure wealth and honest means... to nourish this physical body. Impure money should neither be used nor hoarded... Deep faith in cause and effect is the layperson's path of livelihood. If you wish to cultivate truly, I urge you to be cautious of speculative trades like playing stocks and stay far away from them...}

{align:justify|Many readers may feel it is too strict to say: "Playing the stock market hinders cultivation"...? Speaking as a monastic, I agree with this story. For if investing in stocks leaves your heart fretting day and night without peace, then for the sake of material things we have bartered away the purity and tranquility of our original mind.}
{align:justify|In (Karmic Retribution in the Present Life, volume 3, page 231) there is the story of the general director of a real estate company, a billionaire many times over... He played stocks, and once lost 4.7 million in fifteen minutes; the shock gave him a cerebral hemorrhage and he died suddenly.}
{align:justify|So this is where playing stocks causes hindrance. But if you play, and whether stocks rise or fall... whether you win big or lose heavily, your heart remains calm and peaceful... then there is nothing to say — you have every right to decide as you please.}

{align:justify|Each of us, through past ignorance, has created countless evil deeds, staining our precious self-nature and soiling it with worldly dust. Because ignorance veils the mind, we cannot see the radiance of light. Therefore we must sweep away the dust. According to Buddhism, the best way to wash away defilements is repentance (one of the wholesome practices such as reciting sutras, bowing to the Buddha, reciting the Buddha's name, holding mantras and so on...)}
{align:justify|General Director Tran is a businesswoman who owns a highly successful enterprise. She has studied Buddhism for over two years, and under her influence many employees of her company have followed her example in becoming vegetarian and practicing. As a result their lives have changed, their spirits are high, the company grows ever stronger, and product quality is more stable. Compared with other enterprises, Mrs. Tran is a famous woman leader whom everyone warmly admires and praises.}
{align:justify|One day Mrs. Tran came to my house and told me about her health. She complained that lately she often suffered violent headaches of unknown origin, an illness that gravely affected her work, her daily life and her practice.}
{align:justify|While Mrs. Tran was describing her headaches, I suddenly saw an enormous crocodile appear from her left shoulder up to her neck, ceaselessly biting at her nape so that the left side of her head no longer received enough blood for the brain. I could not hold back and hastily asked:}
{align:justify|"Have you ever eaten crocodile meat or any wild animals?"}
{align:justify|"Oh dear! I have eaten a great deal of animal meat," Mrs. Tran said gloomily. "Back when I still ate meat, having to constantly wine and dine clients, I ate almost every kind of wild game: ostrich, turtle, wild rabbit, snow chicken, poisonous snakes, even crocodile!... But I only ate crocodile meat once, and besides it was frozen meat — the animal had already been killed beforehand..."}
{align:justify|I explained:}
{align:justify|"Although you ate only one piece of meat, you yourself hosted the guests and paid for this evil deed out of your own pocket! You must know that wild animals (like crocodiles)... are highly sentient and should never be killed casually. Do not eat their flesh out of craving for tasty food and a full belly... Master Hsuan Hua once explained why we should not kill. The reason is this: 'When we eat the flesh of sentient beings, we absorb the animals' qi and blood into our own bodies, and their consciousnesses follow along to torment us'... It was that crocodile that took revenge, keeping your body constantly sick and depleted! Right now its consciousness is on your left side (from shoulder to nape), ceaselessly biting your neck so that the left brain no longer receives enough blood — that is why you have headaches..."}
{align:justify|Mrs. Tran came to her senses:}
{align:justify|"Then my offenses are grave indeed, for I have eaten so many wild animals. I must repent, recite sutras, make vows... first of all dedicating the merit to this crocodile..."}
{align:justify|That evening, after bathing, Mrs. Tran knelt before the Buddha and recited the Ksitigarbha Sutra, dedicating it to the murdered crocodile. While reciting, she endured with patience, persevering through two full recitations of the Ksitigarbha Sutra and overcoming the dizziness and headaches...}
{align:justify|The next day when we met again, I saw that her spirits and appearance had changed for the better... Her face was beginning to glow pink and rosy, and the dark patches had faded by more than half...}
{align:justify|That afternoon several fellow practitioners came to visit Mrs. Tran, and before them all this imposing general director cast off every trace of her aloof official pride, earnestly confessing the faults she had committed in the past, sincerely repenting the offense of having eaten and killed so many living beings, repenting that as a leader she had once harbored arrogance, hot temper and narrowness, repenting the heaps of evil karma she had created before studying Buddhism...}
{align:justify|Even as she repented, the dark patches still lingering on her face gradually dissolved completely, replaced by a radiant, rosy freshness...}
{align:justify|At that moment I saw the beings she had harmed flickering in and out of view; under the power of her sincere repentance, they forgave with joyful equanimity, then rose up in group after group and in an instant were reborn into wholesome destinies.}
{align:justify|Such supreme and immense power of repentance moved everyone deeply; the whole hall buzzed as people confessed together: "you a sentence, me a sentence"... each sincerely admitting faults, noisily examining the evil karma and the past errors and wrongs they had created...}
{align:justify|The power of repentance is exactly as the Buddha taught in the "Sutra on the Differences in Karmic Retribution": "If one who has created grave offenses knows remorse and self-reproach, repents past faults and vows never to repeat them, one can surely pull out every evil karma by the roots"...}

{align:justify|With a "CRASH," the car Tien Khon was driving slammed into the median strip. It was night, but the streetlights were blazing, and the car was not going fast. Yet for some reason Tien Khon's vision clouded over; the windshield before his eyes seemed full of dust, and he simply could not see the median strip ahead. In keeping with the car's speed, it struck the median with matching force. Hearing the car grind with an abnormal "screech, screech," Tien Khon hurriedly hit the brakes, only to discover the brakes had failed. Just then his daughter, sitting behind him, cried out tensely:}
{align:justify|"Dad, stop! Or we'll hit the car ahead!"}
{align:justify|But the car no longer obeyed his control; it plowed straight into the median (and just then, at the bend, a truck appeared), struck the truck as well, and only then stopped. The dangerous crash unfolding before their eyes quickly drew a crowd of onlookers.}
{align:justify|It was nine at night. Everyone assumed the driver must be drunk to have caused such an accident. After the oversized "ram-and-crash," Tien Khon's car was completely deformed; a piece of the median's iron sheeting was twisted, torn and peeled back, jutting out... stopping ten centimeters from Tien Khon's right thigh — which is why his leg was unhurt. Apart from a scratched, bleeding nose... and a red swollen thigh, Tien Khon himself had nothing to worry about, and his daughter suffered only light scrapes...}
{align:justify|After a while, a beautiful middle-aged woman with a dazed look came running to the scene of the accident.}
{align:justify|Seeing her, the daughter named Linh Linh quickly waved and shouted:}
{align:justify|"Mom!..."}
{align:justify|Seeing father and daughter safe and sound, she calmed down again.}
{align:justify|This middle-aged woman was in fact a devout Buddhist. She was vegetarian, recited the Buddha's name, strictly upheld the precepts — an exemplary lay practitioner whom many fellow Buddhists admired and emulated; everyone called her "Professor Vuong."}
{align:justify|Since studying Buddhism, her family had changed greatly. Her daughter, once willful and unruly, had begun to honor her parents and had likewise become vegetarian and started reciting the Buddha's name. "When the whole family cultivates together, wisdom surely appears"... Her husband devoted himself all the more to vegetarianism and life releases. Having studied Buddhism for less than four years, they had become a Buddhist family, so exemplary and happy that many who saw them could only envy and wish...}
{align:justify|Why did this Buddhist family meet with such a sudden accident? The affair began a month earlier.}
{align:justify|That morning, Mrs. Vuong went to the market, and the vegetable seller kindly gave her some beans to try.}
{align:justify|These beans are not safe eaten raw. Mrs. Vuong, having no time to spare, ate them raw, and as a result suffered vomiting and diarrhea that left her weakened and frail. With insufficient blood, her back pain flared up again.}
{align:justify|Hearing this, I — being a physician — felt duty-bound to help, and hurried to her house to treat her. Her back pain eased. By then it was late at night, and as I took my leave, Mrs. Vuong asked her husband and daughter to escort me home. After seeing me home, on their way back they met with danger as described. That night, upon hearing they had been in a crash, I tossed and worried all night long and could not fall asleep. The next day, feeling guilty, I visited them, bringing a home-cooked dinner.}
{align:justify|What astonished me was this: after enduring such a terrifying calamity, the entire Vuong family were in excellent spirits. Each of them repented together for the killing karma created in the past, all grateful for the Buddha's empowering protection that let grave offenses be repaid lightly so they stayed safe. The whole Vuong family deeply understood that this painful retribution must surely stem from the evil causes they themselves had planted in past lives — impossible to condone.}
{align:justify|The Great Jewel-Heap Sutra says: "Suppose a hundred thousand eons pass — karma once created is never lost; when conditions come together, its retribution is at once received"...}
{align:justify|The Buddha proclaimed for us the eternal, unchanging truth of the universe. The cause of this sudden car accident traces back to a grievous past fault, and now I recount this karmic story so that everyone may firm up their resolve in the Way and together cut off evil and practice good.}
{align:justify|Tracing to its roots, it turns out this unwholesome seed was planted in the Qing dynasty:}
{align:justify|"Long ago, Mrs. Vuong, Tien Khon and their daughter Linh Linh — were all men, sworn brothers as close as flesh and blood. At the time all three served the imperial court as officials; they dealt uprightly with others and tolerated no fakery... and thereby offended those of petty minds...}
{align:justify|These wicked-hearted officials secretly conspired, using the power in their hands to scheme again and again to eliminate the three (seeing them as thorns in the eye)... But every treacherous plot failed.}
{align:justify|Finally, in a major case of 'seizing power and usurping the throne,' these evil officials deliberately framed all three as 'fellow rebel conspirators,' fabricating complete material and human evidence...}
{align:justify|As a result, the court at once issued warrants for their arrest... The three were secretly tipped off beforehand and fled in time into the deep mountains.}
{align:justify|But the petty officials did not give up. They jointly memorialized the throne to arrest the friends and relatives closely connected with the three, punishing them severely — some dying, some wounded — spreading panic so that no one dared help the three any longer...}
{align:justify|Hiding in the desolate mountains without a soul in sight, the three wronged men knew nothing of this. Later, when they received the terrible news that their close friends had been implicated and had suffered tragic calamities, they all misunderstood, believing that Magistrate Truong (the judge of the case) had deliberately ruined them...}
{align:justify|In truth Magistrate Truong was by no means a bad man; he was forced to try this case, but the moment he accepted it he secretly sent someone to warn the three beforehand so they could escape in time. Not knowing they had been quietly saved by him, all three swore to avenge their friends and relatives!}
{align:justify|After consulting together, the eldest brother (Linh Linh) drafted the scheme to lure Magistrate Truong out. They sent word inviting him to meet them in the forest outside the city, with the second brother (Tien Khon) lying in ambush to strike unexpectedly from behind and cruelly murder Magistrate Truong, while the third brother (Mrs. Vuong in this life) stood watch...}
{align:justify|In that life I was one of their relatives. When I entered the forest and witnessed what these three sworn brothers did, not only did I fail to stop them, I even brought food to support them... I gave them steamed buns; the three ate their fill, then hastily burned Magistrate Truong's body and fled... As for me, I pretended to know nothing and moved far away (back to my native village). From then on Magistrate Truong vanished from the human world.}
{align:justify|That is why in this life I too must receive retribution. That very evening, while carrying food into the kitchen for laywoman Vuong, I had just picked up a cup to pour tea when I suddenly slipped, sprawled on the floor, and the cup flew from my hand, clattering onto the tiles with a 'clang' — yet it did not break. This made everyone quietly savor the workings of karmic cause and effect...}
{align:justify|We all clearly understand: had we not all resolved to follow the Buddha Dharma in cultivation, the terrifying retribution can well be imagined — for such causes must surely bring such results!...}
{align:justify|After returning home, I nursed endless remorse, silently repenting in my heart for having joined forces in a past life to harm the good-hearted Magistrate Truong.}
{align:justify|Before the Buddha's altar I knelt and recited seven full recitations of the Ksitigarbha Sutra, earnestly dedicating the merit to deliver Magistrate Truong. On the third day, just as I finished the dedication, I suddenly heard a soft voice at my ear: 'I'm leaving now!'}
{align:justify|I told him at once:}
{align:justify|'May you take refuge in the Triple Gem, recite the Buddha's name often, cut off evil and practice good, skillfully arouse the great Bodhi resolve, benefit sentient beings, and pray that here and now you be reborn in a wholesome destiny, hear the Dharma, cultivate well, and soon reach the shore of awakening...'}
{align:justify|From this we can know that everything we meet in life is no accident, but retribution drawn forth by causes planted long ago — only that sentient beings are deluded and heavy with karma and cannot see through... Hence the saying: 'Bodhisattvas fear causes; sentient beings fear results'...}
{align:justify|Therefore, when facing thorny, adverse circumstances, accept the retribution calmly, strive to create good karma, correct faults, practice good, and cultivate with all your strength to redeem past wrongs.}

{align:justify|Then X dreamed of a past life in which she entered someone's house and found a large sum of money left carelessly about. X gave rise to greed and stole it, but before she could hide it, several people entered the house. In fright, X rushed into the bathroom, dropped the money into the toilet and flushed it away to destroy the evidence, so no one would discover the theft... though the money was never spent, the offense was already complete — which is why in this life X must repay debts without end...}
{align:justify|Waking up, X came to her senses and vowed to reform properly from then on, no longer wailing over "unreasonable debts crashing down."}
{align:justify|Having told this story, X said:}
{align:justify|"I did it, I bear it — whom is there to blame? Plant evil causes and you must reap unwholesome fruits!"}

{align:justify|At first, reading the title of this story, I was indifferent and did not want to translate it. But then I forced myself to read what the author had to say, and the story moved me. Let me share it with readers, especially lay Buddhists at home and young people about to become parents.}
{align:justify|Tiet Dinh is a musician. When she became pregnant she developed true faith in Buddhism, vowing to strictly uphold the five precepts, practice according to the Buddha Dharma, and educate her unborn child in a Buddhist way.}
{align:justify|Let us hear Tiet Dinh relate the miraculous spiritual communion between mother and child:}
{align:justify|"After marrying, wanting the child to be born healthy and intelligent, I paid great attention to diet, exercise, and thorough preparation in every way.}
{align:justify|I saw in the 'Ksitigarbha Sutra' that: 'Sentient beings of Jambudvipa in future ages who are about to bear children, if within seven days they recite this inconceivable Ksitigarbha Sutra and recite the name of Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva ten thousand times, then whether the child is boy or girl, whatever ill retribution from past lives... will at once be liberated; the child will be peaceful, happy, easy to raise, and long-lived'...}
{align:justify|This sutra passage gave me precious guidance. I believed it and felt greatly encouraged. So I resolved to nurture and raise a child 'pure as ice and jade,' of lofty character, determined to forge its wisdom and merit in the Buddha Dharma.}
{align:justify|In the early months of pregnancy, I began reciting the 'Ksitigarbha Sutra' for my child. My body was still unaccustomed and uncomfortable, so I only recited reverently on the ten fasting days (the 1st, 8th, 14th, 15th, 18th, 23rd, 24th, 28th, 29th and 30th).}
{align:justify|At first, I simply thought this could let the tiny life just taking shape in my womb hear the Buddha's immortal sutra. I was determined to give my baby the very best prenatal education, and my heart felt so proud and happy — a joy truly beyond words. I always prayed that the greatly compassionate Buddhas and Bodhisattvas would grant me a child as I wished.}
{align:justify|Four months pregnant, thanks to a wise teacher's guidance and with my family's full support and care, I completely trusted the Buddha's words and became entirely vegetarian. Vegetarianism not only gave me good, clean nutrition without fatigue or discomfort; on the contrary, my complexion stayed rosy and fresh, my body and mind were peaceful and joyful, and my energy was full.}
{align:justify|Later I progressed further: each day I could recite one full 'Ksitigarbha Sutra' for my child, diligently and without laziness. I held unwavering faith in everything the Buddha taught in the sutra. I recited in a clear voice, keeping daily company with the fetus growing within me, my heart full of a peace and joy never before experienced in this practice. My heart constantly thirsted to see my child's face soon.}
{align:justify|Time slipped by, and summer arrived with its stifling, oppressive heat. But I did not slacken in the least, still striving in effort, purifying my mind and restraining desires — that is: 'ears hearing no evil sounds, eyes seeing no evil things, diligently unrelenting, always guarding the mind with right mindfulness' — each day reciting sutras for the fetus, striving to be a mother in the true sense.}
{align:justify|One day, having finished reciting the 'Ksitigarbha Sutra,' I felt my throat dry and sore, so I walked to the refrigerator to grab an ice cream as a treat, a reward for my 'hard' recitation just now. But the instant I opened the refrigerator door, a voice suddenly rang out in my head:}
{align:justify|'Mom, please don't eat ice cream — I'll get so cold!'}
{align:justify|This was truly startling; I thought I must have misheard, feeling it very strange, and mused to myself: 'This is probably just my own muddled imagination, or some kind of autosuggestion!' So I paid it no mind and decided: 'Rest a little, eat it later.'}
{align:justify|After resting, I went to open the refrigerator again. To my amazement, the same sensation returned:}
{align:justify|'Mom, don't eat ice cream — I'll get so cold!'}
{align:justify|This time the words rang out powerfully in my mind and finally caught my attention. Clearly the fetus in my womb was using 'mind to speak' with me, 'telling' me of its own sensations of pain and pleasure... This wondrous communion made me deeply understand that my unborn child and I were 'heart to heart, mind knowing mind.' The 'Ksitigarbha Sutra' had connected my child and me in a wondrous, intimate bond. Mother and child understood each other through 'inner language,' sharing sensations and forming an extraordinarily profound and mysterious bond of mother and child in this life!}
{align:justify|I once recited with a sincere and reverent heart the 'Sutra Spoken by the Buddha on Longevity, Extinguishing Offenses, and Protecting Children.' The sutra says: 'When the child is in the womb, if the mother eats hot food, the child suffers as though in burning hell. If the mother eats cold food, the child suffers as though in freezing hell, in pain all day long...' Measured against this passage, the moment of my child's and my 'psychic resonance' confirmed and illuminated it. This moved me beyond measure — I shall never forget it. The Buddha's words are indeed true speech, real speech! This also matches what the medical classic 'Essential Formulas Worth a Thousand Gold' says: 'While pregnant, eating extremely hot or extremely cold food is forbidden.'}
{align:justify|From then on, for the sake of the fetus, no matter how sweltering the weather, I never touched cold food again.}
{align:justify|When my darling was two weeks old, out of habit I fed the baby huang lian — a Chinese medicinal herb — which gave the baby ceaseless diarrhea. Gazing at my baby, thin and drained of strength, my heart burned like fire and I was frantic with worry... I pressed my cheek to my child's pale forehead, my mind churning with a hundred cares, my heart tangled like thread... when suddenly a sentence appeared in my head:}
{align:justify|'Mom, stop using chicken eggs! Eating chicken eggs is very bad for me!'}
{align:justify|I woke with a start — it was my darling speaking to me. I was fully vegetarian exactly as the Ksitigarbha Sutra teaches, but to stimulate milk flow my sister-in-law had been making me chicken-egg wine every day.}
{align:justify|This time, I began to savor a delightful spiritual dialogue with my child. I raised a thought in my mind:}
{align:justify|'Darling, what should Mom do so you'll be well?'}
{align:justify|The baby answered at once:}
{align:justify|'Mom, quickly recite seven Ksitigarbha Sutras and dedicate them to my karmic creditors...'}
{align:justify|Without a moment's delay, I set about reciting the seven sutras. Afterward, I 'asked' the baby:}
{align:justify|'Mom has finished the seven — is there anything else you want Mom to do for you?'}
{align:justify|The baby 'replied':}
{align:justify|'From now on, recite one Ksitigarbha Sutra for me every day.'}
{align:justify|I asked again:}
{align:justify|'Until when should I recite?'}
{align:justify|The baby 'answered':}
{align:justify|'Until I can recite sutras myself.'}
{align:justify|'When will you be able to recite sutras?'}
{align:justify|'At seven years old!'}
{align:justify|I suddenly understood. From then on, I recited every day without fail. By the second day, the baby's diarrhea had stopped completely.}
{align:justify|As I write these lines, my baby is just over two months old, healthy, peaceful and easy to raise. And to fulfill my promise to my child, every day I recite the Ksitigarbha Sutra for the baby — seven years will make 2,555 recitations. My husband and I firmly believe our baby can use these conditions to inspire us to diligent cultivation.}
{align:justify|I deeply understand that our whole family is joining forces to complete the work of the Way. As our child grows day by day, our work of the Way grows ever more diligent... and our wholesome deeds and virtues grow along with it. For our entire family, what peace and happiness this is.}
{align:justify|(Respectfully related by laywoman Tiet Dinh.)}
{align:justify|Explanation by layman Qua Khanh: Mother and child are drawn together by extremely deep conditions into profound love. During pregnancy, mother and child truly depend on each other, connected in blood, bone and flesh, in spirit... down to the body itself.}
{align:justify|Reciting Mahayana sutras for the fetus is the very best prenatal education. I have met many women who, while pregnant, recited the Ksitigarbha Sutra, the Liang Huang Repentance or other Mahayana sutras... and the children born were truly beautiful, intelligent, healthy, peaceful and easy to raise.}
{align:justify|This is the sincere personal account of a mother in Shanghai. When her child was forty-five days old, I met all three members of the family and personally verified it. The contents of this story are entirely true and most trustworthy.}

{align:justify|Nowadays abortion in society grows ever more serious, and the retribution for abortion is truly dreadful — harming not only the love between husband and wife but also causing immense suffering to women's minds and health. What is more, in the future one must still endure bitter retribution in the hells.}
{align:justify|We must do our utmost to cherish life and to stop and forbid abortion. Lay disciples of the Buddha should live faithfully, restrain desire, and guard and treasure their own health. As for those who have already had abortions, repentance alone is not enough — they must also perform life releases and recite sutras dedicated to the aborted fetuses before the bonds of enmity can be untied...}
{align:justify|One day a Miss Kim came to our clinic for treatment. She looked very refined, her hair in a maiden's bun, but her face was mournful and dark... she looked utterly worn out. As soon as she entered, Miss Kim said in a small, anguished voice:}
{align:justify|"Doctor, please help me, save me. My whole body aches..."}
{align:justify|I took her pulse and found it exhausted and forceless, as after a grave illness. I asked at once:}
{align:justify|"Have you had an abortion recently?"}
{align:justify|Miss Kim burst into tears and sobbed out her story:}
{align:justify|I am 35 this year, and only with great difficulty did I conceive a baby. Seven months pregnant, a hospital checkup showed the doctor that the baby's heart was not developing well, and it would be best to terminate the pregnancy. But while I was still hesitating, undecided, I was forced to obey my family and abort the seven-month fetus... Ever since the abortion, my spirit has utterly collapsed; to this day I constantly wish to kill myself and suffer terribly. Doctor, please save me...}
{align:justify|Miss Kim had originally enjoyed a successful career; married for three years, she longed intensely for a child, and this year her wish was finally fulfilled. But in early pregnancy she often had to travel on business, on top of social wining and dining with clients, leading to unbroken acts of killing. She did not know how to protect her health while pregnant, nor did she ever have the chance to hear teachings or learn of the Buddha Dharma; through seven months of pregnancy she never met a single Buddhist friend, and not knowing how to safeguard the fetus well, she brought about this tragedy. Seeing Miss Kim's suffering, I advised her:}
{align:justify|"You must not be agitated — abortion is very dangerous for women. Aborting a seven-month child is more dangerous still. The killing karma of abortion is extremely grave. It not only harms your own health but also diminishes happiness and affects the future fortunes of your family and career."}
{align:justify|Now, besides medical treatment, you must sincerely repent the killing karma you have created. Recite for the aborted fetus the "Sutra Spoken by the Buddha on Longevity, Extinguishing Offenses and Protecting Children Dharani" at least seven times, reciting steadily one or two times a day (you may kneel while reciting, then dedicate the merit to the aborted fetus), helping it hear the Buddha Dharma and soon be reborn in a wholesome destiny.}
{align:justify|Miss Kim nodded when she heard this. A month and a half later she returned. I saw her face had changed — her complexion was fresh and rosy, her spirits high — and she cheerfully told me:}
{align:justify|My health is now ninety percent recovered, my mind at ease... I no longer think of suicide. I am so grateful to you, Doctor. Seeing her "come back to life" and find faith, I said:}
{align:justify|You need not thank me. Thank the Buddha Dharma. All sentient beings have Buddha nature, so we must extend compassion to every being and do them no harm...}
{align:justify|Since beginningless eons, never hearing or knowing the Buddha Dharma, we have created boundless offenses and harmed countless sentient beings.}
{align:justify|Therefore we must honestly recognize our own faults, blame neither heaven nor others, repent with complete sincerity, and broadly perform meritorious deeds dedicated to those we have harmed... Only thus may we hope for their forgiveness...}
{align:justify|When you go home, you should often read and study the Buddhist sutras; try reading Master Hsuan Hua's commentary on the Diamond Sutra to understand the vital meaning of life... Once you have read the sutra you will know what to do, and right in the course of practical Buddhist study and cultivation you will find the path from suffering to joy, taste great liberation, live a life of wisdom — and the aborted fetus, hearing the sutras, will rely on them to untie its grievances...}

{align:justify|Ma Thanh is a young, very successful businessman whose family was once perfectly happy — but because his work demanded heavy socializing, things happened that left them... helpless.}
{align:justify|Ma Thanh has loved quiet sitting and cultivation since childhood; after marrying, busy with household and company affairs, he gradually grew lax in practice and became stained by worldly bad habits. During that time he created countless killing offenses — killing, stealing, lust, lying... he violated them all... And so the bond between husband and wife steadily soured. At first there were quarrels, then it came to blows, and the marriage stood on the brink of collapse.}
{align:justify|Ma Thanh wanted to save his marriage and hoped to escape affliction, so he came to see me. At our first meeting, Ma Thanh made a poor impression on me: his face looked dismally dark and gloomy, and I said:}
{align:justify|"Your mind has been in great anguish lately, and your heart aches unbearably, doesn't it?"}
{align:justify|"That's right!" Ma Thanh nodded in admission.}
{align:justify|"Your wife has had four abortions, and among them was an exceptionally fine baby boy, wasn't there?"}
{align:justify|Ma Thanh's ordinarily composed face suddenly turned pale. He said tensely:}
{align:justify|"It's true my wife has had four abortions, but we never knew whether they were boys or girls. Only once, before an abortion, my wife dreamed of a little boy — how could you possibly know that?"}
{align:justify|Nothing any person does fails to leave traces behind; all is revealed plainly. Everything we do is silently "recorded" by heaven and earth in the unseen, yet people themselves never know, firmly imagining no one will ever know what they did — when in fact everything is "recorded" in full, without missing a hair!}
{align:justify|All the troubles arising in your family stem from killing karma, and among these the offense of abortion is the gravest! You must repent with complete sincerity, recite sutras, become vegetarian, and...}
{align:justify|For each murdered fetus recite at least seven full recitations of the "Sutra Spoken by the Buddha on Longevity, Extinguishing Offenses and Protecting Children Dharani," unfailingly repenting and reforming with a sincere heart... From now on you must no longer beat your wife — can you do that?}
{align:justify|I can, I surely can, Ma Thanh said, shaken and resolute.}
{align:justify|A month later Ma Thanh brought his wife, Ms. Truong, to see me; by then the dark gloom on his face had vanished, and his appearance was positively bright and cheerful, full of confidence. The moment he saw me, Ma Thanh said with feeling:}
{align:justify|"It is truly inconceivable — I distinctly felt every word and sentence of the 'Sutra Spoken by the Buddha on Longevity, Extinguishing Offenses and Protecting Children Dharani.' I had just finished reading it once when the anguish and pain in my heart dissolved away, so I hurried to the hospital for a checkup — and the ECG showed my heart condition had returned to normal. Even the doctor was amazed."}
{align:justify|I vow that from now on I will firmly study Buddhism, recite sutras and repent, be a true Buddhist, and live to benefit sentient beings. Our marriage has now improved a great deal. My wife and daughter have also begun to enter the study of Buddhism...}
{align:justify|Thanks to Ma Thanh's genuine repentance and true practice of reciting the "Sutra Spoken by the Buddha on Longevity, Extinguishing Offenses and Protecting Children Dharani," the hatred of the aborted fetuses was dissolved, helping the babies soon be reborn, while his own illness recovered fully; the whole family cultivates together in the Buddha Dharma and reaps much benefit.}
{align:justify|Further explanation: Abortion ranks among the gravest killing offenses. Women who have had abortions need to repent sincerely and must kneel before the Buddha and recite the "Sutra Spoken by the Buddha on Longevity, Extinguishing Offenses and Protecting Children Dharani," or the "Ksitigarbha Sutra"... dedicating the merit to the murdered fetuses — only thus can the offenses be extinguished.}
{align:justify|Best of all is for husband and wife to repent and recite together, which works even better. For driving a woman from conception to abortion is a responsibility the man can never evade.}

{align:justify|This is laywoman Ly's own lived experience. Let us hear her tell it:}
{align:justify|My name is Ly Man Hue. Though only 30 years old, my life has been so full of painful calamities that I felt helpless and utterly abandoned, sinking ever deeper into dejection and despair. Not until I met the Buddha Dharma did my life undergo changes rare indeed (never before experienced).}
{align:justify|My mother had ten children, so life was a hard struggle. I am the third; my eighth sibling, a boy, was given away to be raised by others. My father died early, and I was forced to leave school after third grade. After that I worked as a nanny, a laborer...}
{align:justify|Tempered by the "furnace of life," my naturally tough, unyielding, never-say-die character only gained further resolve.}
{align:justify|I run a company with a good income; at 19 I met my present husband. Because our two families' circumstances differed so widely, and because I still had to care for my younger siblings (brothers and sisters alike), my husband had to live with us for eight years for many reasons — both parents opposed it, so we could not formally register our marriage (during this time I did something I regret to my core: I once had an abortion. That abortion left lasting harm, so that for many years after I could hardly conceive. Only in 2002 did we formally marry.}
{align:justify|Once married, we wanted a child with all our hearts; we saw countless doctors and tried every way, but to no avail. Later my husband and I adopted two baby girls (blood sisters) as our daughters.}
{align:justify|Before knowing the Buddha Dharma, I was often vexed, always wrapped in fog, directionless. Every day after work I never knew where to go (was it better to go home or not?) — so I just kept driving in circles, hands on the wheel, not knowing where I should go or return to. Unable to fill the hollow, empty feeling in my heart, in five years I replaced five brand-new cars... I spent wastefully while my mind churned in endless turmoil. I thought I was a good person — why did I receive no good reward? Instead I faced so many entanglements and sorrows...}
{align:justify|My family stood on the brink of collapse; I strained myself to exhaustion running the business, yet was schemed out of my property, while my younger sisters were ungrateful, constantly picking fights and causing trouble...}
{align:justify|In short, my life was a botched, tangled mess, my mind daily seared by suffering.}
{align:justify|Once, riding in a friend's car, I happened to read "Karmic Retribution in the Present Life" and was awakened by its stories of cause and effect as from a dream. From then on my life began to have direction...}
{align:justify|So I at once gave up eating meat — I even felt nauseous at the very sight of fish or flesh... Before, I had run everywhere seeing doctors and taking medicine... after giving up meat I needed no medicine at all... On the contrary, my whole body felt wonderfully light and at ease. I no longer spent wildly and wastefully, but learned to save and to use the money to help others...}
{align:justify|In October 2007 I was fortunate to meet layman Qua Khanh, an unassuming man, very approachable. The moment we met I felt an extraordinary familiarity, like a reunion of deep affection. I poured out all the sufferings I had endured; when he began teaching the assembly, I quietly sat in to listen. Little did I know something strange was about to happen...}
{align:justify|While I was sitting cross-legged on the sofa, I suddenly felt as though "I was... falling into a chicken pit! Full of chickens, filthy and stinking! As if I too were... one of the chickens? Just then my whole body shook, and I kept vomiting without knowing anything at all"...}
{align:justify|At this, several friends present panicked and rushed to help, deftly steadying me and cleaning up the mess. When I came to, I had no idea anything had just happened.}
{align:justify|Having only just encountered Buddhism, I experienced a most marvelous sensation — which also strengthened the faith of the friends present.}
{align:justify|I suddenly remembered: during that pregnancy, wanting to nourish my body, for a long stretch I ate one chicken every day — yet in the end I could not keep the baby. "Truly karmic retribution follows like shadow follows form."}
{align:justify|Since that unimaginable event, I began reciting the Ksitigarbha Sutra, bowing to the Buddha and sitting in meditation... once sitting for eight hours straight... My life gradually began to change. Work went ever more smoothly; my sister who had run away from home has returned. I am the pillar for my brothers and sisters. Besides the company, I also own a fashion shop, though before I only ran it half-heartedly.}
{align:justify|After meeting the Buddha Dharma, I reorganized and renovated the fashion shop and business began to thrive... but I realized the greatest harvest was spiritual peace, for my heart no longer clings. In the past I clutched tightly at things never mine, exhausting my heart... Once I understood the Way, I let go easily; what was lost no longer weighs perpetually on my mind. And so my heart is far more relaxed and comfortable, and my marriage has improved. I was even planning to go to the hospital at the end of December 2007 for in-vitro fertilization. Having understood cause and effect, whenever afflictions arise I dissolve them myself.}
{align:justify|Then something strange happened. In early spring 2008, I dreamed an auspicious dream: in my house there was a figure in a yellow robe, head shaven, his whole body blazing with light...}
{align:justify|I saw myself as one of two ordinary people kneeling before him; the other, who knows what was said, was sent out? Only I remained, and the radiant figure said to me:}
{align:justify|"What would you ask of me?"}
{align:justify|I replied: "I ask for a child..."}
{align:justify|He said:}
{align:justify|"Granted — may your wish be fulfilled..."}
{align:justify|Then I woke up. Afterward I felt strange changes in my body (I had stopped taking medicine half a year before, and had been vegetarian for over half a year), and the miracle appeared: at last I was pregnant! It was so strange I could hardly believe it. So again and again I went to the hospital to be examined, to make absolutely sure I was pregnant...}
{align:justify|After I became pregnant, though my body's reactions were strong, my heart was filled with inexpressible joy... I thank the Buddha Dharma; I thank the Buddhas and Bodhisattvas for their support in guiding me onto the path of right faith and Buddhist practice. The Buddha Dharma has brought me boundless benefit.}

{align:justify|Tieu Dien's is a Buddhist family — not rich, but living very happily and at ease. Tieu Dien's resolve to study Buddhism began with her father's strange story:}
{align:justify|Tieu Dien's father (henceforth called Old Dien) is a typical southern farmer, fully endowed with the diligence and hard work of a farming household. Every day he worked from sunrise to sunset. And so the years passed...}
{align:justify|One day, the farmhouse busy with the harvest season, Old Dien had to toil until eleven at night. Finished, he dragged his weary steps along, wiping sweat as he walked the familiar narrow lane home. Hurrying along, at a bend he suddenly saw several dark shapes rush straight at him, shouting in hoarse voices:}
{align:justify|"Stop! You cannot pass!..."}
{align:justify|At first glance Old Dien took them for robbers, so he swung his hoe and struck wildly, "splitting" one of them... only a "CLANG!" rang out, and the dark shapes were gone...}
{align:justify|By then Old Dien, carried away, swung his hoe a few more times at the air around him... but in a flash, he struck... the top of his own head! What happened after that he cannot remember at all...}
{align:justify|In fact those dark shapes were beings of the ghost realm — karmic creditors (in a past life on the battlefield Old Dien had killed them; they nursed undying hatred and waited always for a chance at revenge).}
{align:justify|According to Old Dien's wife, who recalls what followed:}
{align:justify|""That evening near midnight, I was asleep when suddenly I heard sounds from the kitchen: 'I'll kill you! I'll kill you!'...}
{align:justify|When I ran into the kitchen, the sight before my eyes turned my legs to jelly: my own handsome husband was raving mad, slashing wildly at empty air while screaming at the top of his lungs..."}
{align:justify|At the time I was so frightened I trembled all over, then called for neighbors to help take my husband to the hospital."}
{align:justify|After this, the neighbors buzzed with gossip, saying:}
{align:justify|"Old Dien ran into demons and went mad!"...}
{align:justify|(Right here I wish to remind everyone: when walking at night in the dark and you see dark shapes, or when passing a cemetery or an abandoned house, if you feel afraid... it is best to recite the Buddha's name or hold a mantra. This not only dispels all fear in the mind but also greatly benefits beings unseen by fleshly eyes)...}
{align:justify|Old Dien's madness lasted a full thirty years, until recent years when he began listening to the Buddha Dharma... After taking the Three Refuges, his condition gradually improved.}
{align:justify|Out of concern for her father, Tieu Dien vowed to become vegetarian, often joining others in life releases and sutra printings, persevering in reciting the Ksitigarbha Sutra dedicated to her father, hoping he might soon recover.}
{align:justify|Thanks to his daughter's efforts, Old Dien's illness grew ever more stable and his thinking gradually returned to normal.}
{align:justify|Nowadays Old Dien steadfastly recites the Buddha's name every hour of every day, his mind free of stray thoughts... though he still cannot fully care for himself, he lives very happily and peacefully.}
{align:justify|If you cultivate skillfully and persevere without tiring, in the end the day will come when you escape the bitter sea of birth and death.}
{align:justify|The Buddha Dharma is like the sun: it not only shatters delusion and darkness but also warms the human world. The question is whether you will open your heart to receive it.}
{align:justify|May all sentient beings believe and uphold the Buddha's teachings, unlock wisdom, and soon bask in sunlight that melts away delusion. }

{align:justify|Human destiny can be changed — people can use the right methods to reform their fate and determine their own future! Lieu Pham, a Ming-dynasty scholar, wrote down his own practical experience of changing destiny in the "Four Lessons of Liao-Fan," inspiring countless later generations to use Buddhist methods to change their own fate! This alone proves: "Destiny is established by oneself; karmic retribution never lies."}
{align:justify|Below is a true story proving that destiny can be reformed:}
{align:justify|Phung Lap Khiem is a well-known talented man... a university graduate. As a student he loved studying the Book of Changes and divination... and often used this knowledge to read his own fortune. Every application proved remarkably accurate. So he believed: human destiny is predestined!}
{align:justify|Once he visited a famous Book of Changes master, who predicted many things about his future, firmly asserting that his future marriage would last only fifteen years before breaking apart (the cause lying with his wife). Hearing this, Phung grew uneasy, hoping to find a remedy...}
{align:justify|Sure enough, everything unfolded exactly as the master had predicted. Phung entered graduate school with ease, graduated, worked... then was promoted again and again, finally hung up his own company sign and achieved glorious success...}
{align:justify|Three years later he married a gentle, beautiful girl... family life was happy, his career smooth... and Phung gradually forgot the prophecy that had once filled him with anxious dread...}
{align:justify|Husband and wife lived warmly together, and in the blink of an eye came the end of... the fourteenth year. One day Phung's beloved wife suddenly found a small hard lump beneath her right breast (the size of a chicken egg), painfully swollen... she went to the hospital for tests and learned she had end-stage "mammary gland cancer." The doctor pronounced: Phung's wife had "at most one more year to live!".}
{align:justify|Before this monstrous illness, the whole family sank into inconsolable grief. Only then did Phung recall the old prophecy, sighing to himself: "Truly, the appointed number is fixed"...}
{align:justify|On the brink of despair, Phung chanced upon Qua Khanh's "Karmic Retribution in the Present Life," in which every story is vivid and true, leaving him deeply educated and gradually understanding: "only deep faith in cause and effect, cutting off evil and practicing good, cultivating according to the Buddha Dharma" can transform destiny!}
{align:justify|From then on, husband and wife began to kindle hope for a new life. With friends' help, he met layman Qua Khanh. At one glance at Mrs. Phung's pale-green complexion, Qua Khanh gently pointed out the evil causes, the heaped-up sinful karma she had created in the past... he urged her to become vegetarian, sincerely repent her evil deeds, properly recite the Ksitigarbha Sutra, and dedicate it to all the beings she had ever harmed...}
{align:justify|Hearing this, Mrs. Phung went home and began at once... her longing to live blazing so fiercely that she dared not harbor a shred of pride; day after day she persevered in kneeling recitation of the Ksitigarbha Sutra, repentance, life releases, printing sutras and wholesome books...}
{align:justify|Half a year later, a miracle occurred: the vicious illness was completely healed.}
{align:justify|Today Phung and his wife have walked hand in hand into the sixteenth year of their marriage (a family once about to break apart, transformed through Buddhist study and practice into a Buddhist family living happily)... This practical experience, personally lived through by husband and wife alike, convinces them all the more that "destiny lies in one's own hands!"...}
{align:justify|If everyone can deeply believe in cause and effect, cut off evil and practice good, and follow the Buddha's words — strictly upholding the precepts, becoming vegetarian, cultivating correctly and properly... then in this very life you can not only change your destiny but also transcend birth and death, leave suffering for joy, unlock wisdom, and live free and at ease...}
{align:justify|Further explanation: The Buddha Dharma is like sunlight, while the killing, stealing, lust and lying... people create daily pile up like mountains of ice, growing daily, deep, thick and hard — like a frozen hell, forever frigid and cruel... Only those with affinity can hear the Buddha Dharma and believe and accept it, correct faults once known, and practice once understood... This is like the sun rising amid solid ice: before long the ice melts into water, and this water can benefit all things. It contends with none, and not only sustains the lives of sentient beings but also washes their defilements away until they are pure. This is precisely the foundation of a practitioner's path.}

{align:justify|Many Buddhists who have studied Buddhism for years have given up meat, fish and flesh, yet still eat chicken and duck eggs. So should a Buddhist disciple eat eggs or not?}
{align:justify|First, let me tell a true story:}
{align:justify|Once at a Dharma assembly, laywoman Tran brought along a female colleague named Nha Lam, 35 years old. She was thin, her face sallow — looking very worn out. Meeting Professor Qua Khanh for the first time, her eyes shone with joy and longing.}
{align:justify|During the teaching she listened with rapt sincerity. When Professor Qua Khanh asked what troubled her, Miss Lam's face flushed slightly; she looked tense, stammering without forming a sentence, until laywoman Tran finally explained on her behalf and everyone understood.}
{align:justify|It turned out she had been married ten years and thirsted for a child, yet no matter which doctors she begged for medicine, she never conceived. Today, fortunate to meet Professor Qua Khanh, she pleaded with him to clear up her confusion...}
{align:justify|Professor Qua Khanh deeply sympathized with her suffering of "unfulfilled longing" (wishing for a child but having none)... He sighed, turned to me and said:}
{align:justify|"Qua Hong, please observe why Nha Lam has no children."}
{align:justify|I turned to look at Nha Lam, met her longing gaze, and could not hold back an exclamation:}
{align:justify|"Are you very fond of eating eggs? Especially quail eggs?"}
{align:justify|"Yes, yes, I love them. I've eaten a great many quail eggs... so could it be that..."}
{align:justify|The second half carried a doubtful tone. Laywoman Tran beside her, quick-witted, promptly asked a question for her:}
{align:justify|"Everyone says eggs are nutritious, and before knowing the Buddha Dharma we all ate eggs. But why is it that only Nha Lam cannot conceive?"}
{align:justify|I looked at Professor Qua Khanh standing beside me (seeing his eyes full of urging and encouragement), and explained at once:}
{align:justify|"The Buddha said that eating the eggs of birds, water creatures and so on... shows no compassion. Venerable Master Hsuan Hua also taught about eating eggs; he once joked: 'If you are not afraid of becoming an egg for others to eat in a future life, you may eat whatever eggs you like'... Because cause and effect interweave in tangled complexity, and each person's merit, virtue and conditions differ, although many people eat eggs, their retributions are not the same... If you want a child, you must cut off all meat (which of course includes eggs). Moreover, you must perform many life releases and recite the 'Ksitigarbha Sutra,' dedicating it to all the beings you have ever eaten..."}
{align:justify|When I finished, I glanced at Nha Lam and saw she looked disbelieving and unaccepting. Plainly she had been waiting to hear... a karmic tale with gripping twists about her infertility (difficulty conceiving)... only to have her "bubble burst" by my blunt, all-too-plain words. Perhaps she felt what I said was too "ordinary," unimportant, nothing worth noting — her face showed utter disappointment.}
{align:justify|I felt truly sorry for her, for she believed nothing at all; I had no choice but to say it straight out:}
{align:justify|"Right now you only want a child for yourself — but have you spared a thought for the eggs you ate?... They too had mothers... and their mothers suffered hardship and toil to bear their young."}
{align:justify|At this, Professor Qua Khanh gently told Nha Lam:}
{align:justify|"Qua Hong has explained the principle of cause and effect to you quite correctly and accurately. Treasure it, receive it, and practice as she instructs, and you will attract a good child. Best of all is for husband and wife to reform together."}
{align:justify|On the occasion, layman Qua Khanh told a true story as proof:}
{align:justify|"There was a woman head of a biology research department whose daughter had been married four years without conceiving. So she used her expertise to guide her son-in-law in concocting medicine..."}
{align:justify|But no matter what they brewed or did... the results only deepened her doubts about her own expertise. And so this department head went about gloomy and dejected, moving all to sigh: 'How pitiful, the heart of a parent!'."}
{align:justify|At the time I told her:}
{align:justify|"Your daughter and her husband are both crazy about eggs, and love quail eggs most of all (this is the shared karma that leaves them childless). To have a child, both must become vegetarian, release life, recite sutras, recite the Buddha's name, and do many good deeds..."}
{align:justify|The department head's daughter and her husband practiced as advised. As a result, barely half a year later the daughter was pregnant..."}
{align:justify|When Qua Khanh finished, laywoman Ly (a probationary woman doctor) asked me at once:}
{align:justify|"Why are there so many patients nowadays who (once pregnant but aborted) now want children and cannot...?"}
{align:justify|I told everyone:}
{align:justify|"Every child who comes to be reborn shares profoundly deep conditions with its parents... And within dense transmigration, the chance to be reborn human even once is extremely rare! Yet parents, for many reasons, abort recklessly, destroying the aborted infant's chance to become human. In doing so, how would they know they have created an offense as grave as killing an Arhat (hinting that if the child, once born, cultivated and realized fruition, cutting it off beforehand would equal the offense of killing an Arhat)? And worse, never repenting, wantonly killing and eating flesh, piling killing karma deep upon deep, they must surely attract the evil retribution of 'a severed line, no descendants'..."}
{align:justify|I saw Nha Lam and the others nodding in agreement — how glad I was that she could wake up. I hope she receives the Buddhas' empowering support, reforms her faults toward the good, upholds the precepts in cultivation, and soon bears a noble child...}
{align:justify|Half a year passed, and laywoman Tran — who had first brought Nha Lam — telephoned me with the news: 'Since returning home, Nha Lam has done exactly as we instructed, and now she is pregnant. She is so grateful to everyone.'"}
{align:justify|Further explanation: I urge everyone not to eat any kind of eggs out of craving for tasty food and pampering the palate. In twenty years on the Buddhist path I have met many egg-eaters (including eaters of bird, snake and fish eggs... down to eggs still unformed inside the hen)... who became women unable to conceive, or who constantly miscarried... At their wits' end, they followed my instructions: repent before the Buddha (vowing to give up meat and all eggs). Each day recite one or two full 'Ksitigarbha Sutras,' dedicating them to the eggs and to the mothers that laid the eggs they ate... As a result, the great majority conceived within about three months, and their bodies changed, growing healthy...}
{align:justify|May those who are infertile and thirst for children, whose family fortunes go poorly or whose health is weak, practice according to this method — within a few months inconceivable things will appear in your own bodies. You need not spend a cent to have a child, and you will save a great deal of money besides — so good a deal, why not do it? (But... will you have the courage to stay vegetarian, practice good, release life, recite the Buddha's name...? Can you do everything exactly as we instruct — that all depends on you?)...}

{align:justify|On the question of eating meat, the Buddha spoke very clearly in many Mahayana sutras. In the "Brahma Net Sutra" it is written: "No meat at all should be eaten. If a Buddhist disciple deliberately eats meat, he cuts off the seed of great compassion, causing all sentient beings to flee at the mere sight of him. A Bodhisattva should not eat the flesh of sentient beings, for eating meat incurs boundless offenses!"... And in the "Lankavatara Sutra" the Buddha teaches: "I regard all sentient beings as my children; hearing meat taken as food, I do not even approve — how much less would I eat it myself! Mahamati, all kinds of pungent plants — onions, leeks, garlic, chives... constantly reek with foul impurity, obstructing the holy path and the pure dwellings of gods and humans in the world... how much more the pure lands of the Buddhas? Liquor is the same: it obstructs the holy path, damages wholesome karma, and constantly breeds many faults... Mahamati! Therefore those who walk the holy path should use none of these pungent, meaty, wine, meat, onion, garlic... foul-smelling things"...}
{align:justify|There are a great many scriptures in the Great Canon concerning giving up meat. I myself have lived through an experience with eating meat.}
{align:justify|One day, having finished my evening recitation, I began to sit in meditation. What I went through that night is an experience I can never forget!}
{align:justify|At the time my back ached dully, yet I gritted my teeth and kept sitting — when a thought suddenly rose in my mind: "I am 26 this year and have been vegetarian for two years — but before age 26, how many sentient beings did I harm?"...}
{align:justify|In an instant, a screen appeared before my eyes:}
{align:justify|There was a very wide river, filled with shrimp, fish, turtles, snakes... on the banks every kind of land animal: buffalo, cows, goats, pigs, dogs, chickens, ducks and so on... gathered in herds and flocks... There were even animals I had never seen... Strangely, I had never met these unfamiliar creatures — so how could I have harmed them?}
{align:justify|Just as I was silently puzzling over this, like a film reel rolling, I clearly saw scenes of myself feasting guests, and a voice rang out: "In the past, every birthday you held a grand feast and invited people over; though you yourself ate very little, because you entertained guests — or when you married... these banquet feasts took countless animal lives! Though you ate little, so many beings were killed on your account... Nor is that all: you also recommended good restaurants, urging people to go eat seafood, shrimp, crabs of every kind... These people ate, then recommended others in turn, so the killing karma you committed naturally piled up, multiplying ten thousandfold"...}
{align:justify|A vivid scene of wedding banquets — killing, eating meat, drinking wine... appeared before my eyes in sharp detail, lifelike in every stroke... Just like a film projector, replaying the earliest images, fresh and alive.}
{align:justify|Realizing I had created countless evil deeds, I hastily repented, recited the Buddha's name... and prayed for the deliverance of the beings I had killed.}
{align:justify|Just then I saw a great many fish, shrimp... (water creatures of every kind) ride the empty air away together by the power of Buddha-recitation. But a large number of animals (cows, goats, pigs, dogs and so on) still refused to leave. On my back appeared several soft-shelled turtles and snakes, stubbornly clinging and refusing to go. At once I clearly understood the cause of the pain in my back — and so I resolved that I must repent with complete sincerity, recite sutras and mantras for the beings I had harmed, that they might soon leave suffering behind for peace...}
{align:justify|That very night I began reciting the Diamond Sutra and the Shurangama Mantra, sincerely repenting my ancient evil deeds, dedicating the merit to the beings I had killed...}
{align:justify|Two months later, one day while reciting the dedication verse, I suddenly felt my right thigh go numb, swollen and aching, the pain spreading down to my ankle and foot... then vanishing. For three days running this kept recurring... after which my back fully healed. So I believe those beings, relying on the power of sutra recitation and dedication, were reborn into wholesome realms.}
{align:justify|This made me profoundly realize: a follower of the Buddha Dharma should not only refrain from eating the flesh of sentient beings, but should also never invite, instigate, or recommend meat-serving places to others — still less urge others to eat meat. Moreover, in every arising thought one must be utterly free of even a trace of killing intent.}
{align:justify|Today a great many people in the world do not believe in cause and effect; they care only about eating for the palate, indulging freely in so-called delicacies from land and sea — there have even appeared monstrous scenes of eating humans (eating newborn or miscarried fetuses). Such fearless, unrestrained killing and karma-creating will attract all manner of strange epidemics breaking out and spreading unchecked...}
{align:justify|Therefore I respectfully urge everyone: turn back soon, be a true Buddhist, strictly uphold the precepts, and above all do not eat the flesh of sentient beings.}
{align:justify|Further explanation:}
{align:justify|People of old, seeing anything amiss or unseemly... would shut their doors and reflect, examining themselves — so illnesses often healed without medicine. Modern people, by contrast, always see themselves as faultless, often saying "I've done nothing evil" — never knowing that killing and eating flesh is itself a grave offense!}
{align:justify|Today natural and man-made disasters — earthquakes, tsunamis, floods, fires, car crashes, plane crashes... — strike unceasingly, and all kinds of strange illnesses arise, mostly rooted in eating meat. If you can do as laywoman Qua Hong did — repent and reform, recite sutras and cultivate good — you will surely be healthy and auspicious.}
{align:justify|Moreover, the Mahayana sutras always urge people to give up meat. In the "Great Nirvana Sutra," Bodhisattva Kashyapa asks: "World-Honored One, I see that meat should not be given to meat-eating people. Why? Because not eating meat brings very great merit."}
{align:justify|The Buddha praised him: "Excellent! You understand my meaning well; a Dharma-protecting Bodhisattva must be just so. From this day on, the shravaka disciples are no longer permitted to eat meat, for eating meat severs the seed of great compassion (when receiving offerings from donors, one should contemplate it as eating the flesh of one's own child)..."}
{align:justify|Bodhisattva Kashyapa asked again: "World-Honored One! Why did I previously hear that monks may eat the three kinds of pure meat?"}
{align:justify|"The three pure meats were a temporary expedient... From today on I decree for my disciples: they may eat no meat at all. One who eats meat makes all sentient beings panic at his very scent... They will tell one another: This man is my enemy!"}
{align:justify|Therefore a Bodhisattva should give up eating meat; a true disciple of the Buddha must practice according to the teachings and cut off meat-eating entirely!}

{align:justify|Slandering the Buddha Dharma — especially the Mahayana Dharma — brings very heavy retribution. The "Sutra Spoken by the Buddha on the Certainty of Karma from Slandering the Wondrous Dharma" records: "If sentient beings hear the Mahayana taught and, their minds displeased, give rise to slander, know that they are kin of demons. By arousing thoughts that slander the Mahayana sutras, after death they fall into Avici and suffer boundless pain. Then they are reborn among hungry ghosts eating fire, excrement, mucus and filth... enduring suffering for boundless eons, after which they are born human as lepers, blind, deaf, mute, stammering, with deficient faculties... These sentient beings pass through boundless eons of rebirth... and only at the very end meet a Buddha to serve and make offerings to... Therefore, hearing the Buddha teach the Mahayana sutras, keep the mind pure and free of stray thoughts"...}
{align:justify|Tieu Quach is a tall, handsome university student, freshly graduated and preparing to study in America. But he suffered a mental affliction most hard to cure, so he came to me for help.}
{align:justify|The moment we met, Tieu Quach spoke with an anguished look:}
{align:justify|"Alas! I have committed a grave fault. I know I am wrong, yet I simply cannot control myself. What should I do?"}
{align:justify|Hearing this, I asked:}
{align:justify|"What grave fault? Knowing it is wrong yet unable to control it? Drug addiction or sexual misconduct?..."}
{align:justify|Tieu Quach answered:}
{align:justify|"None of those — something more serious still! I have studied Buddhism these several years; I can now be vegetarian, recite sutras, release life... but I suffer one great sickness: whenever I read in the sutras that 'one must revere the Triple Gem — Buddha, Dharma, Sangha — and must not wildly slander, lest retribution follow'..."}
{align:justify|I at once give rise to doubt and disbelief. At times I even deliberately slander the Buddha Dharma, to see whether any retribution really comes... Once I read in the Shurangama Sutra: 'In cultivating samadhi to escape the dust, if you do not eliminate killing, you cannot escape the dust. Even if you are learned and meditative concentration manifests, if you do not cut off killing, you are certain to fall among spirits'..."}
{align:justify|Having read that passage, I grew doubtful, and even told my family to buy fish to kill and eat, to see whether any retribution would come. So I broke the precepts and killed... Afterward I was deeply sorry, knowing what I did was wrong — yet somehow I cannot control myself...}
{align:justify|"Since you recognize it as a fault and know you act wrongly, you must resolve to repent."}
{align:justify|"Though I know it is wrong, at times I still want to dig out passages in the Buddhist sutras to slander... I know this is a fault, yet I cannot reform — and that frightens me. What should I do?"}
{align:justify|Seeing Tieu Quach as stubborn and hard as a rock, I thought "only the vajra method can shatter such arrogance." I put on a stern face and said sharply:}
{align:justify|"Do you know that the offense of reviling the Buddha and the Dharma condemns you to the Uninterrupted Hell? If you do not earnestly resolve to repent, you are throwing your life away, for that offense means falling into hell... In truth, in past lives you cultivated for several lifetimes, sitting in bitter ascetic meditation — but you held wrong views (just like the fifty skandha-demons the Buddha describes in the Shurangama Sutra). During meditation you saw all kinds of strange states: meeting Buddhas, seeing the lands of Buddhas, Buddha halos and so on... and at once claimed you had realized sagehood, had awakened... giving rise to immense pride, arrogance and conceit... The Buddha warned: 'Seeing strange states without craving or clinging is wholesome; but claiming sagehood for yourself means falling into the deviant!'... And so demons seized the opening to possess and prod you into wrongdoing, sending you straight down into the ghost path..."}
{align:justify|A human body is hard to obtain, the Buddha Dharma hard to hear; in this life, thanks to ripened wholesome conditions, you are blessed with a man's body and handsome features and have encountered the Buddha Dharma... But if you do not seize this rare chance to cultivate diligently and smash your wrong views and ego-clinging, you will waste every wholesome condition!..."}
{align:justify|Tieu Quach looked alarmed and asked:}
{align:justify|"Then what must I do?"}
{align:justify|"When you return home, take the Buddhist sutras you once slandered, respectfully place them on your Buddha altar or in some pure place, then kneel and sincerely repent your past offenses (reviling the Buddha and the Dharma) and vow never to repeat them... then bow in repentance with a sincere heart..."}
{align:justify|You must do this every day until your mind is truly pure, without a trace of slanderous thought. You must study the Shurangama Sutra, memorize the fifty skandha-demons to tell right from wrong clearly, and memorize the Shurangama Mantra by heart, dedicating... praying for the Buddhas' empowering support to grant you proper knowledge and views and a Bodhi resolve firm and irreversible... can you do it?"}
{align:justify|"I can, I can! I understand now! At last I have found the medicine to root out this incurable sickness... How fortunate! My sickness has hope of cure!"}
{align:justify|Seeing Tieu Quach look remorseful and joyful, I sincerely pray he soon becomes a true disciple of the Buddha and one day contributes his share to propagating the Buddha's teaching.}
{align:justify|"Keep at it, Tieu Quach!..."}
{align:justify|Explanation:}
{align:justify|In the Nirvana Sutra the Buddha teaches: "Kashyapa, in this world there are three incurable diseases people often contract: first, slandering the Mahayana sutras; second, committing the five rebellious acts; third, being an icchantika (one who has severed the Buddha seed). These are three extremely grave diseases that only Shravakas and Pratyekabuddhas can treat"...}
{align:justify|Tieu Quach knows his offense but cannot control himself; he must seek a good spiritual friend for help, and once instructed, the causes and effects become clear to him and he resolves to rein in his mind and repent to erase his offenses...}
{align:justify|Most dreadful of all are those teachers calling themselves "masters" who, though wearing Buddhist robes, openly slander the Buddha Dharma from high seats before large audiences. They neither keep the Buddhist rules themselves nor teach their disciples to keep the precepts, but misguide them so that many stray into the deviant... how sad, how lamentable!}
{align:justify|People of the world must penetrate the sutra canon, studying carefully the "Four Clear and Unalterable Instructions on Purity" in the "Shurangama Sutra" to distinguish right from wrong and avoid straying onto mistaken paths...}

{align:justify|Wholesome and evil thoughts alike arise from the mind, for all phenomena are mind-made. In cultivation we must watch and train ourselves in every arising thought, managing the mind well; if right mindfulness stays continuously present, naturally the dependent retribution will follow the primary retribution, and destiny is surely reformed. Buddhism often says: "With one wholesome thought, the palaces of heaven are at once complete; with one evil thought, the punishments of hell stand waiting." If you can advance to thoroughly awaken to the source of mind, dwell where there is no dwelling, and diligently cultivate the six perfections and ten thousand practices, liberation and Buddhahood... can surely be realized.}
{align:justify|We who study Buddhism, once our practice reaches a certain point, will meet all kinds of grinding trials coming at us — and then we need an even mind, patience and vigor to pass the tests...}
{align:justify|Below is an experience from my own life; I hope everyone benefits from reading it.}
{align:justify|"Tieu Van is my cousin, seven years my junior, utterly willful and stubborn. My aunt and uncle dote on him.}
{align:justify|That day I visited my aunt and uncle, but they were out; only Tieu Van was home minding the house. The moment I stepped in, he dragged me online to play computer games. I had no choice but to play with him a while. Since I rarely play games, I lost very quickly, hurting his score and sending him into a rage — he rushed over, shoved me to the floor and scolded:}
{align:justify|"Why do you play so badly?"}
{align:justify|Seeing my brother's aggressive manner, I put on my elder-sister voice and sternly rebuked him:}
{align:justify|"How dare you treat me so fiercely? Normally, out of respect for aunt and uncle, I always yield to you — today I must teach you a lesson!"}
{align:justify|So I grabbed him and spanked him soundly.}
{align:justify|Beaten by me, Tieu Van bawled his lungs out, grabbing and hurling things everywhere as he cried... clocks, tables, cups, toys... he smashed them all. I tried to stop him in vain, and just then my aunt and uncle returned. Seeing the chaotic "battlefield"... my aunt and uncle were displeased and asked in reproachful tones:}
{align:justify|"What's all this?"}
{align:justify|Without waiting for my explanation, Tieu Van bawled at the top of his voice:}
{align:justify|"Sister Hong did it all! She beat me and threw things — she hurt me and smashed all my toys! Boo hoo! Dad! Mom! While you were out she bullied me like this, boo hoo!..."}
{align:justify|Hearing my brother, I stood stunned, frozen, my mouth hanging open in amazement, speechless... with no idea how to explain...}
{align:justify|My aunt and uncle dote so on my little cousin that they believed him at once. They furiously scolded me:}
{align:justify|"How can you be so heartless, behaving so badly? You once lived here a while too — how could you treat your brother so cruelly? You wicked, foul-hearted little thing, get out of here this instant! Out!..."}
{align:justify|I wanted to explain, but the matter seemed beyond repair, so I swallowed my anger and went home, enduring it.}
{align:justify|Back at the dormitory, I cried hard and thought: "Why would aunt and uncle treat me like this? Tieu Van was plainly in the wrong, yet without distinguishing black from white they lashed out at me — why? Why?... Greatly compassionate Avalokiteshvara Bodhisattva, I beg You to support me and dissolve every misunderstanding between us"...}
{align:justify|That night I did not sleep. A week passed; I longed to visit my little cousin and apologize to my aunt and uncle, for they are normally very good to me. So I bought gifts and brought them to their house...}
{align:justify|Stepping inside, the sight before my eyes startled me: my aunt sat on the sofa covering her face and weeping, while my uncle beside her sighed with grief.}
{align:justify|"Aunt, uncle, what's wrong? Where's Tieu Van?..."}
{align:justify|My aunt and uncle pointed to the bedroom. I walked over, and one look at my little cousin gave me a terrible fright: handsome Tieu Van had turned into a dazed idiot. His tongue lolled out of his mouth, twisted to the right, drool dripping down... while his right hand and right cheek twitched without stop.}
{align:justify|"He's been like this for a week!" my uncle told me helplessly:}
{align:justify|"After you left, the next day Tieu Van suddenly convulsed all over. At the hospital the doctor said these are symptoms of cerebral palsy — it can only be treated gradually; there's no better way..."}
{align:justify|"What are we to do? He's our only one..."}
{align:justify|My aunt, sitting beside, choked out her words, her eyes red — she had plainly cried a great deal. I stepped forward to comfort her:}
{align:justify|"Don't worry, there will be a way..."}
{align:justify|Back at the dormitory, I felt this affair was connected with me. Though Tieu Van, my aunt and uncle had shown me nothing but hatred, hurling only harsh words at me... as a Buddhist I must meet them with wholesome thoughts and a compassionate heart. If I sincerely recited sutras dedicated to Tieu Van, perhaps he would gradually recover and the family would be peaceful and harmonious again. So I began reciting the Diamond Sutra, dedicating the merit to Tieu Van.}
{align:justify|A miracle happened: three days later, my aunt joyfully telephoned to report:}
{align:justify|"Since your visit, by the next noon Tieu Van had taken a turn for the better. His mouth and face stopped twitching and he can eat normally. Tieu Van has told us the whole truth — he did wrong and must own up to you! We're so sorry, dear — that day your aunt scolded you unjustly. Forgive your aunt, won't you? Come to dinner tonight!"}
{align:justify|And so all ended well, peaceful and harmonious. This moved me profoundly. If every Buddhist had a fully compassionate heart, then even when others direct evil thoughts at us, we ourselves should not flare with anger, but rely on the power of the Buddha Dharma — repenting, dedicating... If we meet every person and affair with wholesome thoughts, we will surely reap wholesome rewards and harvest unexpectedly fine results!}
{align:justify|The Avatamsaka Sutra says: "If one would know all Buddhas of the three periods, observe the nature of the Dharma-realm: everything is mind-made"...}
{align:justify|Buddhists meeting adverse circumstances and conditions must hold to right mindfulness, meeting every situation with patience and unmoving thusness... Only thus is there a chance of liberation. The Diamond Sutra says: "All dharmas are without self; perfection lies in patience"...}
{align:justify|Hereby I urge people of the world: be careful in speech and conduct, guard the mind and the mouth, and never slander others — for good and evil lie within a single thought, fortune and calamity follow like shadows, and karmic retribution is always real.}

{align:justify|Reading this far, do you wonder why so many foul-mouthed slanderers, guilty of faults like Tieu Van's or worse, suffer nothing? The answer lies in cause and effect and the hidden tangles of people's lives. Let me explain it this way: offending a wicked person or an ordinary person does not bring as heavy a retribution as offending a sage or a saint...}
{align:justify|When I first finished translating this story, I did not believe it, thinking the narrator seemed to be... boasting? But then I reconsidered: "Qua Hong is a Buddhist who does not lie and cultivates most diligently, carefully keeping the precepts... That is why her prayers are so readily answered, and why she receives the empowering support not only of the precept-guarding spirits but even of the Buddhas... and so attracts results like these."}
{align:justify|Reading the sutras and histories, we find the story of an old monk in the Buddha's time who, though already a saint, kept chewing and chomping most unsightly. The cause traced back to distant past lives, when he was a young novice who happened to see an old monk reciting sutras and mocked him, jeering that the master recited just like "a buffalo chewing grass" (never suspecting this master had already realized Arhatship). For offending a saint, that novice spent five hundred lives with his mouth forever munching like a grass-chewing buffalo, until in this final life, though he had already realized Arhatship, he still had to keep repaying the old debt...}
{align:justify|Apart from the workings of the law of cause and effect (revile others and others revile you; harm others and harm returns to you)... groundlessly framing and blaming a sage (or even someone who merely keeps the five precepts strictly) can also summon evil retribution upon oneself — as in the story Qua Hong just told (which proves it)... That is why the Buddha always urges us to speak carefully, ever reminding us: "Disaster dwells on the tip of the tongue..."}

{align:justify|People usually turn to the Buddha Dharma and pray for blessings only when driven to a dead end... If at that moment they meet a wise teacher to guide them, practice repentance correctly, and study Buddhism with sincerity, they can turn peril into safety and come back to life from the jaws of death.}
{align:justify|But among all the bitter retributions to be endured, how many possess such good fortune? Therefore we must always remember impermanence, believe and accept upon hearing, and treasure all the more the strengths of this human life — living in harmony with the current of good, reforming old faults, becoming vegetarian and keeping the precepts, understanding cause and effect, abandoning evil for good, smashing piled-up ignorance, holding to right knowledge and views... only then can we escape teeming calamities and disasters.}
{align:justify|There was a Miss Luu who, though believing in the Buddha for many years, never became vegetarian because her family opposed it, and her faith in Buddhist study was not firm. At the end of 2007, laywoman Luu went for a checkup and unexpectedly discovered intestinal cancer; after re-examination the doctor said:}
{align:justify|"Go home and rest — eat whatever you fancy! With this illness, your life is now counted in days!"}
{align:justify|Laywoman Luu was completely taken aback; normally she had never thought the illness so grave — now it was deemed incurable, end-stage. Impermanence came so fast she was caught off guard, and her heart filled with dejection and despair...}
{align:justify|Just then she remembered: a few days earlier, her aunt and several fellow practitioners had visited and repeatedly urged her to become vegetarian, recite the Buddha's name, recite sutras... and be diligent.}
{align:justify|So she quietly resolved to keep the precepts and become vegetarian, creating no more killing karma. She would truly study and practice the Buddha Dharma and pray for the Buddhas' empowering support to lead her out of this dead end... Once home, laywoman Luu told everyone plainly of her grave condition and firmly declared:}
{align:justify|"I have not long to live. In the past I created such heavy killing karma; starting today, I am determined to become vegetarian and study the practice — please support me..."}
{align:justify|Her husband and children, who had always opposed her vegetarianism, now silently went along...}
{align:justify|Though she had only just begun vegetarianism and practice, the fear of death and the torment of painful illness made her backslide again and again, her spirit teetering on the edge of collapse...}
{align:justify|Just as her practice grew most difficult, her aunt and fellow practitioners extended helping hands: they took her to release life and recited sutras together in dedication to support her... praying that the Buddhas and Bodhisattvas would bless her to a swift recovery. They also urged her to recite the "Sarva-Tathagata-Hridaya-Rahasya-Dhatu-Karanda-Mudra-Dharani Sutra" to firm up her faith and resolve and conquer the sickness-demon.}
{align:justify|A month later, at the hospital for tests, her illness was found stable and improving. This strengthened her faith. When her husband and children learned she was much better, they rejoiced and all became vegetarian with her.}
{align:justify|In the months that followed, laywoman Luu vowed to study and practice diligently as a Buddhist disciple in the true sense. Every day she recited the "Sarva-Tathagata-Hridaya-Rahasya-Dhatu-Karanda-Mudra-Dharani" 108 times. She knelt before the Buddha in sincere recitation, sat in meditation... focused and unrelenting...}
{align:justify|With that, afflictions fell away accordingly — just as the sutra says: "If there are sentient beings with grave offenses receiving retribution — a hundred illnesses assailing them, pain oppressing the body — who recite this dharani twenty-one times, their fateful illnesses and sufferings will vanish in an instant, their lifespan will lengthen, and their merit will be boundless..."}
{align:justify|Laywoman Luu practiced diligently thus for three months, then returned to the hospital for re-examination, and a miracle appeared: the intestinal cancer was gone — the illness completely and thoroughly cured!}
{align:justify|Thanks to following the true Dharma and practicing Buddhism, laywoman Luu came back to life from death's door. Not only did she herself deeply taste the Dharma's benefit, but her whole family was moved and likewise entered the path of vegetarian practice.}
{align:justify|Further explanation: Laywoman Luu had end-stage cancer — why was she healed in only three months? For the following reasons:}
{align:justify|She reformed and cultivated threefold goodness: lifelong vegetarianism — life releases — sutra recitation.}
{align:justify|She vowed to benefit sentient beings, a vow one with the vows of all Buddhas.}
{align:justify|Each day she sincerely recited the "Sarva-Tathagata-Hridaya..." dharani 108 times; this dharani's empowering force is inconceivable.}
{align:justify|The sutra says: "Sincerely vow, recite and hold the divine dharani; every word and sentence emits great radiance reaching the three evil paths, and all suffering dissolves. Sentient beings escape suffering, sprout the Buddha-seed, and are reborn at will in the pure lands of the ten directions"... If everyone practices according to these three, all can transform fixed karma and adversity.}

{align:justify|Hoang Dinh is a girl of tragic fate: just as she came of age, her parents divorced, and she lived with her father and stepmother — a hot-tempered, ferocious woman who scolded and beat her at the slightest provocation, so that little Hoang Dinh's heart was forever clouded over. Unable to bear such a life, she fled her father's home and went to live with her birth mother. Her mother sold fast food — fried chicken and roast duck (creating very heavy killing karma). This led to later calamity.}
{align:justify|The first few years, their shop did booming business. Earning good money, mother and daughter lived very comfortably. Then, after liberation and the reform-era development, the two moved to Qingdao and worked at a Taiwanese enterprise.}
{align:justify|While working in Qingdao, for Hoang Dinh this was a time of pure enjoyment. But "enjoying blessings consumes blessings" — blessed yet ignorant of practicing good, storing merit and creating true benefit for self and others, merely sitting back in idle enjoyment, such blessings run out fast.}
{align:justify|Hoang Dinh worked two hours a day; her remaining free time she spent helping her boss serve tea to guests, chatting, eating rich delicacies, singing karaoke, dancing... The bar lifestyle, glittering with lights, powerfully stirred her heart and seeded the craving to find a man who could provide her a life of full material enjoyment.}
{align:justify|Among the visiting customers, Hoang Dinh met a young man named Ly Hao, who claimed to come from an upper-class family and spoke and carried himself with great style, gradually conquering the heart of the 21-year-old girl. From then on the two grew ever closer. Within half a year, Ly Hao revealed his gambling-addicted nature: he staked all his money on betting, at times borrowing from servants or from the simple-hearted Hoang Dinh.}
{align:justify|One day Ly Hao called to borrow money; at first Hoang Dinh earnestly urged him to quit gambling. But Ly Hao coaxed the naive girl:}
{align:justify|"I'll reform, I promise... Just lend me one last time, please?"}
{align:justify|Kind-hearted Hoang Dinh softened and brought her half-year's savings to his lodgings.}
{align:justify|At the lodgings, Ly Hao sat drinking away his sorrows; seeing her arrive, he was delirious with joy, and in his drunkenness seized her hand. Seeing this, Hoang Dinh hastily held out the money, meaning to urge him to come to his senses and reform — little did she know that Ly Hao, in his drunken state with bestiality unleashed, robbed Hoang Dinh of her chastity.}
{align:justify|A month after leaving Ly Hao, Hoang Dinh discovered she was pregnant; to hide it from her mother and friends, she used a visit to hometown relatives as pretext. Under economic pressure and fear of public opinion, she decided to abort.}
{align:justify|It was agonizing; she gritted her teeth through violent torment, racked for long, drawn-out hours... "Calamity and fortune have no door — people summon them themselves; good and evil retributions follow like shadows."}
{align:justify|Three days after the abortion, Hoang Dinh received terrible news: her mother had been hit by a car, gravely head-wounded, bleeding without stop, her life hanging by a thread. For frail Hoang Dinh this was suffering upon suffering... With friends' help her mother was taken to hospital. But the doctor said:}
{align:justify|"This one can hardly live — family, go home and prepare the funeral!..."}
{align:justify|Hearing of her mother's accident, Hoang Dinh rushed to the hospital; by then her mother had been in surgery over five hours. At that moment she wept without words, grieving endlessly, knowing only self-blame, whispering: "Mom, don't die... it's because I created evil karma that this happened to you... I'm sorry, Mom, boo hoo... What should I do, what can I do?"...}
{align:justify|Suddenly she heard a friend whisper in her ear: "Recite Amitabha Buddha's name as much as you can — His great vow-power is infinitely mighty!"}
{align:justify|Right! Recite the Buddha's name! Heedless of face, Hoang Dinh knelt before the operating room, folded her palms and sincerely recited aloud: Nam mô A Di Đà Phật! Praying for the Buddha's blessing to save her mother...}
{align:justify|Patients and doctors passing by shot her strange looks. Pointing fingers and gossip meant nothing to her, for at that moment her heart held only Amitabha Buddha — she poured her whole heart into reciting for her mother... for this was her only hope.}
{align:justify|The Ksitigarbha Sutra says: "If a dying person's family — even one member — loudly recites the Buddha's name for the patient, then except for the five uninterrupted offenses, all other karma of the dying one can be dissolved"...}
{align:justify|The merit of reciting the Buddha's name is inconceivable; the Buddha Dharma is truly real and never lies — even the doctor could not guarantee her mother would live, yet strangely, she lived...}
{align:justify|After this calamity, mother and daughter together entered the study of Buddhism, diligently listening to Dharma teachings, persevering in vegetarianism and life releases, and often resolving to repent past killing offenses...}
{align:justify|Thanks to both striving in diligent practice, Hoang Dinh's mother recovered very quickly, with results beautifully beyond imagination. Two months later, Hoang Dinh's mother could do everything herself and lived as healthily as an ordinary person.}
{align:justify|Seeing her mother turn peril into safety, Hoang Dinh practiced all the more perseveringly with growing faith; using her free time, she did her utmost to help those around her and shared her own lived experience with listeners, urging them to deeply believe in cause and effect and swiftly abandon evil for good... She helped many families come to the faith, listen to teachings, practice the Buddha Dharma, and leave suffering for joy.}

{align:justify|The Universal Gate Chapter teaches: "If countless hundreds of thousands of kotis of sentient beings, beset by many sufferings, hear the name of Avalokiteshvara Bodhisattva and recite it single-mindedly, He will at once follow that sound and rescue them."}
{align:justify|One day in late July 1998, just as a typhoon was making landfall, at ten that night I had to go to my unit for night duty. So I put on my raincoat and rode out the gate. As usual, before leaving, the moment I stepped out I always recited: "Nam mô Quan Thế Âm Bồ-tát".}
{align:justify|Outside, the gale howled furiously, rain lashed down into my face; the road was empty of people and not a single vehicle in sight. I kept reciting the Buddha's name and rode slowly through the storm — all was normal.}
{align:justify|But as I neared a crossroads, about to turn right... my heart suddenly pounded violently, with stifling breathlessness so that even normal breathing became difficult... This alarmed me greatly: I had never had heart trouble, and a recent health check had shown all well. But... why at this very moment did my heart suddenly fail, leaving me so breathless? I had to stop and rest until my heartbeat steadied. Yet the moment I stopped... strangely, the pain vanished without a trace and my health returned to normal...}
{align:justify|Just then a truck appeared before me at "super-terrifying speed" — it shot — no! — it "flew" past the nose of my bike... (less than a meter from me), for the driver was going far too fast and recklessly, not bothering to watch whether anyone was on the road.}
{align:justify|The driver's horrifying "flying vehicle" act made me suddenly understand: had my heart not seized with choking pain, forcing me to stop and rest... I would have ridden calmly straight ahead and surely been struck head-on by that truck, with consequences too dreadful to imagine! In other words, without the Buddhas' empowering protection, I would surely have been crushed beneath those truck wheels.}
{align:justify|I am ten thousandfold grateful to the greatly compassionate Avalokiteshvara Bodhisattva. Through this I have deepened my faith and now diligently recite His name unceasingly, with full trust in the greatly compassionate vow-power of Avalokiteshvara Bodhisattva.}

{align:justify|In today's society, natural and man-made disasters — earthquakes, tsunamis, floods and fires, plagues, wars, car crashes... — strike constantly. How can we stay far from calamity? In the "Consecration Sutra" the Buddha teaches: If we take refuge in the Triple Gem, thirty-six Dharma-protecting spirit-kings will guard us. Those who receive and hold the five precepts are guarded by five Dharma-protecting spirit-kings per precept — twenty-five spirits in all. These spirit-kings command countless retinues as numerous as the Ganges' sands, all supporting practitioners. The "Sutra Spoken by the Buddha on the Four Heavenly Kings" records the same.}
{align:justify|Aunt Tran is a devout Buddhist, 72 years old this year, rosy and radiant, full of energy — everyone who meets her praises her as young and lovely. No one could guess she is a woman past seventy.}
{align:justify|But some ten years ago, Aunt Tran's spirits were far from what they are now. Then she ached all over, suffered many internal illnesses, and drank medicine year-round for the ailments of aging decline.}
{align:justify|Each hospital visit frightened her — fearing each checkup would uncover yet another illness; inwardly she always feared death, living every day in jittery dread, until one day she went to the temple to bow to the Buddha, picked up a copy of "Karmic Retribution in the Present Life," and upon reading it at home, suddenly awakened: so in this world there is the Buddha Dharma — a fine medicine curing every illness, skillfully healing body and mind while benefiting sentient beings.}
{align:justify|From then on, Aunt Tran began studying Buddhism — becoming vegetarian, repenting karmic obstacles, reciting morning and evening, bowing, printing sutras, releasing life... Year after year thus, her body grew ever healthier. In her second year of Buddhist practice, something unthinkable happened.}
{align:justify|That afternoon she was strolling with her niece in the park. Just reaching a bend, a bicycle suddenly appeared from behind... and rammed straight into Aunt Tran. Strangely, having struck her, the bike rebounded, shot violently yards away and with a "CRASH" — bike and rider together — plunged straight into the lake on the left facing them...}
{align:justify|At this the little niece panicked and hastily asked:}
{align:justify|"Oh no! Auntie, are you all right? That hit so hard!"}
{align:justify|"No, no, I'm fine!" Aunt Tran smiled in reply.}
{align:justify|"How can that be? I clearly saw the bike hit you so hard it bounced off and flew straight into the lake!... Let me take you to the hospital for a check!"}
{align:justify|"It's nothing! No need for that!..."}
{align:justify|Just as aunt and niece were talking, the rider who had fallen into the lake had clambered ashore and came charging over in towering rage... though still far off, he was already pointing at Aunt Tran and shouting:}
{align:justify|"You doddering old hag, you dare ram my... your grandpa's... bike?!"}
{align:justify|"Good heavens! This man talks so outrageously! He plainly rammed my aunt and then 'flew' straight into the lake — everyone here saw it!..."}
{align:justify|The gathering crowd noisily joined in scolding the cyclist; shouted into speechlessness, he slunk off to haul his bike out of the lake...}
{align:justify|Everyone urged Aunt Tran:}
{align:justify|"You must go to the hospital at once — we saw the bike hit you very hard. Get checked for peace of mind, just in case..."}
{align:justify|So Aunt Tran went to the hospital for tests, with this result: apart from slight bruising on her back, everything was normal — not a single problem. The niece beside her found it most strange.}
{align:justify|Afterward Aunt Tran told me:}
{align:justify|"In the instant the bicycle struck, I felt as though an iron plate had just slid across my back to shield me — which is why I felt no pain, before I even knew what had happened..."}
{align:justify|I told her:}
{align:justify|"Thanks to your strict precept-keeping and vigorous Buddhist practice, the Bodhisattva 'Skanda the Dharma Protector' came to dissolve this calamity for you, manifesting an iron plate to block the bicycle and keep you from injury..."}
{align:justify|The awesome power of the Buddha Dharma is truly inconceivable: a strict precept-keeper in peril will have grave retribution lightened and meet peril turned to safety.}
{align:justify|Further explanation:}
{align:justify|Why do some vigorous Buddhist practitioners still meet with accidents? Because before studying Buddhism, in this very life they already created evil karma — killing, stealing, lust and so on... and even evil karma created since beginningless eons has now ripened, so retribution manifests...}
{align:justify|Once studying Buddhism, if you single-mindedly repent, abandon evil for good and never transgress again... If you take precept-keeping as your foundation and practice vigorously, you can dissolve calamities or have grave offenses repaid lightly... When receiving retribution or disaster, understand that you are clearing past-life karma. The Diamond Sutra says: "Good men and women who recite and hold this sutra, if despised by others, have past-life offenses that should have cast them into evil destinies — and thanks to being despised in this life, those past-life offenses are surely extinguished"...}

{align:justify|We Buddhists often recite dharani (the speech and words of Buddhas and Bodhisattvas arising from meditation). Before the Buddha Dharma reached China, the Chinese land already had some worldly spells which, though not ultimate, could still manifest wonders to dissolve calamities — miraculous effects much like the Buddha's dharani. "Dharani" translated into Chinese means "total retention" (holding wholesome dharmas so they are never lost, and barring evil dharmas so they never arise). Dharani are also called secret speech, for neither Brahma gods nor the Two Vehicles can understand them. They are also called true words, meaning: the Thus Come One speaks true words without deceit. Some dharani were spoken by the Buddha emitting light, taught from within the radiance, and so are called "Ming" (Radiance).}
{align:justify|The awesome merit of the Buddha's dharani is inconceivable. In the Shurangama Sutra the Buddha says:}
{align:justify|"Ananda! This is the secret, wondrous composition born of the Buddha-crown radiance where the Buddhas of the ten directions gather. The Thus Come Ones of the ten directions rely on this dharani to attain unsurpassed, perfect, universal wisdom; you learners not yet done with transmigration, resolving with sincere hearts, may attain Arhatship... Without holding this dharani yet sitting on the bodhi seat to distance body and mind from demonic deeds (such a thing cannot be)..."}
{align:justify|The "Cundi Dharani as Spoken by Seven Kotis of Buddhas" also says: "If any monastic or layperson practices this true word, reciting this dharani to a full nine hundred thousand times, then the ten evils, the four grave offenses and the five uninterrupted offenses created over boundless eons... are all extinguished; in rebirth they constantly meet Buddhas and Bodhisattvas, their wealth is abundant, and they often meet the chance to leave home." The Great Compassion Heart Dharani Sutra says:}
{align:justify|"When reciting the dharani, the Buddhas of the ten directions all come to witness; all karmic offenses and obstacles are extinguished — all grave offenses and evil karma such as the ten evils and five rebellious acts, breaking fasts and violating precepts, smashing stupas and ruining monasteries, stealing the sangha's goods, defiling pure conduct... are all extinguished. With one exception: if toward the dharani one gives rise to doubt, even small offenses and light karma cannot be extinguished, let alone grave ones. Yet though grave offenses are not extinguished at once, the seed of Bodhi can still be planted for the future..."}
{align:justify|I first awakened to the power and awesome merit of the Buddha's dharani through my father's illness:}
{align:justify|My father suffered stomach trouble for years, and as he aged the pain grew worse. That day the air was biting cold; after breakfast he drove to work. By noon he felt his stomach burn and wanted to vomit — with one "retch," fresh blood gushed out like a volcano erupting.}
{align:justify|The colleagues around him were scared out of their wits, helpless; one quick-thinking person helped my father up to rest on the sofa, then telephoned me.}
{align:justify|Receiving the news, I rushed my father to the emergency hospital; in my haste I carried only a small booklet containing the Shurangama Mantra, the Great Compassion Mantra and the Ten Small Mantras.}
{align:justify|At the hospital, my father lay in the emergency room, his face deathly white, vomiting blood without stop. My mother sat beside him worrying helplessly, bursting into tears...}
{align:justify|The emergency room looked like a hell on earth, moans, sobs and cries ringing out without pause, every kind of illness gathered there. I went to arrange things and moved my father to a relatively quiet room. The doctor came on rounds and said:}
{align:justify|"This man has vomited far too much blood; if it cannot be controlled, the consequences are hard to foresee! Tonight the family must watch closely — if he vomits blood again, he must at once receive a transfusion..."}
{align:justify|Hearing the doctor, my father with difficulty raised his hand to wave — signaling he did not want a transfusion — and I hastily answered the doctor:}
{align:justify|"Yes, yes! We will watch him — anything happens and we'll contact the doctor..."}
{align:justify|That night I could not sleep a wink, so worried was I about my father's illness. With my whole heart I picked up the dharani booklet and began to recite.}
{align:justify|Shameful to confess, this was my first time reciting dharani alone — before, I had only heard dharani recited on recordings. Even when my pronunciation was off, I believed sincerity surely brings response and the Buddhas' power would bless my father. I never counted how many recitations — I just kept reading on until father and I both felt our whole bodies grow warm and relaxed, an atmosphere of peace enfolding the room.}
{align:justify|The next morning my father had to undergo a gastroscopy; I silently recited the name of Avalokiteshvara Bodhisattva, hoping a miracle would occur. The awesome power of the Buddhas is truly inconceivable: during the examination the doctors gaped in amazement, deeming the illness very grave and hardly believing it could recover so fast. The wound had healed, and the possibility of cancer was ruled out. A week later my father returned for tests, and the doctor's conclusion was: inflammation and ulceration of the stomach...}
{align:justify|While my father was hospitalized I silently recited seven Ksitigarbha Sutras and released life, dedicating it all to him. A week later, my father recovered and left the hospital.}
{align:justify|This deepened my faith all the more — firm in Buddhist study and in the inconceivable power of the Buddha's dharani. I vowed to use all my strength to benefit sentient beings, helping more people know the Buddha Dharma and soon leave suffering for joy.}
{align:justify|Further explanation: All the Buddha's dharani possess inconceivable merit and power; one need only harbor no doubt toward the dharani, and recite with a compassionate, equal, Bodhi mind, a reverent mind, a mind that benefits all beings, a thus-unmoving mind, a lucid and alert mind, a pure and undefiled mind... and it will surely benefit beings, dissolve hatreds, turn evil conditions into wholesome ones, and win great freedom.}
{align:justify|Great Master Yin Guang said: "Reciting dharani prizes sincerity; even without knowing the meaning, if you vigorously pour your whole sincerity into reverent recitation, in time karma will dissolve, wisdom will dawn, and you can fathom the Buddha's intent." Let us practice according to the teachings.}

{align:justify|Ring... ring... ring! The telephone shrilled urgently; young layman Du's voice sounded tense:}
{align:justify|"Lately, sitting in meditation and reciting sutras, for days on end — 24 hours a day — I keep hearing a voice giving its name, and what's more it even knows future events, constantly informing me..."}
{align:justify|"By all means, do not cling to that — do not crave to hear these voices. You must carefully read the 'Fifty Skandha-Demons in the Shurangama Sutra' as the Buddha describes them; moreover you need concentration, right mindfulness, right knowledge and right views. Don't forget the Diamond Sutra says: 'One who sees me by form or seeks me by sound walks the deviant path and cannot behold the Thus Come One.' In sitting meditation, if strange scenes appear and you meet all kinds of states, never crave or cling to them — keep the mind even and thus-unmoving, giving the Fifty Skandha-Demons no opening to enter..."}
{align:justify|"I understand..."}
{align:justify|A month later, at two in the morning, the telephone shrilled again:}
{align:justify|"Sister Qua Hong, it's Du — save me! Voices keep harassing me nonstop. I'm terrified — I may not live to see tomorrow..."}
{align:justify|Hearing this, I understood at once: from craving spiritual powers too hard, layman Du had gone astray. With the situation urgent, over the phone I guided him to recite the Shurangama Mantra and the Great Compassion Mantra word by word, sentence by sentence, to settle his mind first of all.}
{align:justify|In the morning, I gathered Dharma friends of right knowledge and views and visited Du. With everyone joining forces to help, Du temporarily improved. Though layman Du was vegetarian, sat in meditation, recited sutras and held mantras... his mind clung hard and was very narrow.}
{align:justify|Having entered the Buddhist path, he had controlled his habit of self-indulgence, yet his arising thoughts were seldom pure; moreover he had never truly given rise to great repentance, vowing to reform and renew himself. With no real practice and no real attainment, acting always out of self-interest, never reforming his mind-nature but only clinging to forms — and craving spiritual powers far too much — he easily slipped into wrong views, and the skandha-demons seized the opening to crowd in.}
{align:justify|Cultivation that summons demons and ghosts usually starts from craving spiritual powers, craving responses, craving awakening and so on...}
{align:justify|Therefore a Buddhist practitioner must first see the road clearly and possess fully right knowledge and views. First there must be wisdom — illuminating the mind and seeing one's nature. Once you have truly seen your nature, skillfully recognizing the "master of the house," then whatever strange scenes you meet will not delude you, and demons can hardly disturb you. Second, even without working from the foundations, you must at least understand what "studying Buddhism and cultivating" means.}
{align:justify|Though studying and practicing Buddhism daily, the chief purpose is this: you must understand the meaning within the sutras. Once wisdom opens, practice according to what the sutras teach, while constantly nurturing concentration to purify the mind (like drilling wood to make fire, training troops for the battlefield).}
{align:justify|Through sutra recitation and sitting meditation, concentration and wisdom gather force. You must also apply the Buddha Dharma in everyday life. Skillfully wielding the power of concentration and wisdom to reform bad habits — no selfishness, greed or self-seeking — honing virtue to ascend ever higher, toward realms like the Buddhas and Bodhisattvas, turning the ordinary mind into the Buddha mind, everywhere benefiting sentient beings — only this is called "truly studying Buddhism."}
{align:justify|In the Shurangama Sutra the five skandhas (also called the "five aggregates") are: form, feeling, perception, impulses and consciousness; each has ten kinds of demons, together making the "Fifty Skandha-Demons." The Shurangama Sutra says: "Whether demons torment or attainment succeeds depends on the 'five skandhas' in your mind. If the master is deluded, the guests run riot... From this we see that our own inherent Buddha nature and the skandha-demons stand in a 'host-guest' relationship. If the host is deluded, seduction comes easy and the guests have their way. If the host stays ever alert, the guests must withdraw with respect... A true practitioner must never crave spiritual powers, never wish to wildly discuss them, neither clinging nor chasing them away, always holding the Middle Way..."}
{align:justify|Master Hsuan Hua often taught: The Six Penetrations are the manifestation of wisdom (in the practitioner's undefiled cultivation, awakening and realization of fruition) — nothing extraordinary. What matters is whether the practitioner's views are correct. The practitioner must uphold the precepts purely and restrain greed, lest the path go wrong and stray into the deviant.}
{align:justify|Spiritual powers cannot be trained or transmitted. They occur naturally when a practitioner cultivates to a certain level. But spiritual powers cannot end birth and death, nor are they the ultimate goal Buddhism aims at.}
{align:justify|In truth, spiritual powers are simply the pervasion of self-nature (self-nature's own pervasion) — everyone already possesses them fully (both Buddha nature and spiritual powers), only veiled by ignorance and affliction.}
{align:justify|Of course, when the Buddha was in the world he forbade people from displaying their spiritual powers, but he did not forbid the manifesting of spiritual powers. If a Bodhisattva with spiritual powers never manifests them to skillfully teach beings, what use are spiritual powers? The Buddha only feared people would "casually" display spiritual powers or use them to deceive others and lead them into demonic realms (clearly described in the "Fifty Skandha-Demons" chapter of the Shurangama Sutra), which says:}
{align:justify|"If the practitioner does not think himself a realized saint, that is a wholesome state. But if he claims sagehood for himself, he will fall into the deviant."}
{align:justify|The Buddha teaches: always give rise to four minds:}
{align:justify|An equal mind}
{align:justify|A humble and gentle mind}
{align:justify|A never-lazy mind}
{align:justify|A non-poisonous mind}
{align:justify|A Buddhist disciple must take the precepts as teacher, strictly uphold them, deeply believe in cause and effect, draw near to those of right views — good spiritual friends in true practice (skillfully using the practice best suited to oneself to gather the mind and guard mindfulness). In daily life, arouse the Bodhi resolve, walk the Bodhisattva path, and do many things that benefit sentient beings. Patiently plow and sow without craving a quick harvest, always sweeping the mind clean, unceasingly smashing the marks of self and self-clinging... attaining non-seeking and recognizing the original face.}
{align:justify|If Buddhists care only about spiritual powers and neglect nurturing compassion and wisdom, awakening and saving beings... they have mistaken the goal and are not the Buddha's true disciples.}

{align:justify|The "Forest of Gems in the Garden of the Dharma" records: "Karma created may be heavy or light; if light, repentance at once dissolves it, while heavy karma can turn heavy retribution light."}
{align:justify|Every one of us Buddhists created much evil karma before studying Buddhism, planting many evil causes out of ignorance. So upon studying Buddhism — especially when vigorously keeping the precepts in practice — all kinds of adverse conditions appear. The onset of much adversity is not necessarily bad; some of it helps us clear karma: grave offenses lightly repaid!}
{align:justify|The "Biography of Tripitaka Master of Great Compassion Grace Monastery" records: "When Master Xuanzang lay dying of illness, the Dharma-protecting spirit comforted him thus:}
{align:justify|"Master, since beginningless time you have created much evil karma troubling sentient beings; now thanks to a little illness that evil karma is dissolved — you should rejoice"..."}
{align:justify|Before knowing the Buddha Dharma, layman Ho created very grave killing karma. In his days as a head chef, he was a famous butcher, his slaughtering technique swift and deft... Tasks others found hard, in his hands took only a few knife strokes.}
{align:justify|Almost daily, Mr. Ho drank and partied, smoked, drank liquor and ate meat. What is more, he then dismissed Buddhism as superstition! Perhaps thanks to past-life good roots, on a chance occasion he met layman Qua Khanh (author of Karmic Retribution in the Present Life); after hearing Qua Khanh's admonitions and guidance, he at once resolved to quit smoking, cut off liquor and meat entirely, and begin lifelong vegetarianism. Truly "hearing, he at once believed and accepted" — so radical a change that Buddhist friends of many years' practice could only click their tongues in endless praise...}
{align:justify|In October 2007, Mr. Ho went with a friend on pilgrimage to Mount Putuo. The first day, the two sincerely venerated Avalokiteshvara Bodhisattva with one heart. Along the way, every temple they met they bowed to the Buddha — all went smoothly. The second day, that evening at the guesthouse, having bathed and just stepped out of the tub, he carelessly slipped — with a "thud" his right knee buckled to the floor — and he at once asked his friend to wait while he went to hospital. The doctor diagnosed a fractured, crushed right kneecap... so the next day (day three) he was transferred to a Shanghai hospital, where the doctor decided to operate immediately.}
{align:justify|From the fall until surgery, his leg swelled hugely yet he felt no pain at all. He silently trusted deeply: this was surely the Buddhas' empowering protection, letting grave offenses be lightly repaid... so he recited the Buddha's name all the more vigorously without pause.}
{align:justify|That night after surgery, post-operative reaction kept his right knee aching all night. After that the pain never returned and he healed very fast. Several younger patients sharing his room needed all manner of treatment after bone fractures plus five or six months of rest, constantly supplementing with bone-broth soups, believing only meat-eating could nourish them. Yet their wounds to this day have not healed, so they keep groaning before 60-year-old Mr. Ho...}
{align:justify|This fully shows that vegetarianism and Buddhist practice can greatly aid recovery of health.}
{align:justify|Discharged, Mr. Ho practiced all the more diligently; while his aching leg began healing, unable to kneel before the Buddha, he could only sit on his bed repenting his killing karma and all the evil deeds of his chef days, sincerely reciting the Ksitigarbha Sutra dedicated to the beings he had killed... hoping they might soon leave suffering for joy. Two months after surgery, he shattered hospital rules (recovering so well he no longer needed crutches), got out of bed, moved about and walked indoors. Before long, impatient by nature, Brother Ho refused to stay cooped up; he often went out to join life-release activities or meet fellow practitioners...}
{align:justify|At first, uneasy, we all urged him to be careful — to stay home and rest until fully stable before going out. Little did we know: hearty-natured Old Ho not only refused to listen but declared: "Nothing to worry about — my leg has healed! Walking is no trouble at all!"... As he spoke he stuck his leg out for us to see.}
{align:justify|Everyone burst out laughing. Seven months have passed since he broke the bone, yet today his right knee shows no trace of injury. Mr. Ho moves about completely free and easy, just like an ordinary person.}

{align:justify|Chinese medicine holds that illness comes from many causes: the six excesses (wind, cold, summer-heat, dampness, dryness, fire) and the seven emotions (joy, anger, worry, thought, grief, fear, fright)... that is, from overwork, overindulgence, irregular diet, all kinds and manner...}
{align:justify|If the patient's upright qi suffices and deviant qi has not yet invaded, rest or medication alone will bring swift recovery; but if deviant qi has gained the upper hand and upright qi falls short, many methods must assist the upright and expel the deviant. Here, for illnesses drawn forth by karmic retribution, medical treatment may be combined with repentance — for the way to swift recovery from grave illness, Buddhism holds, is sincere repentance above all. "Mohe Zhiguan, fascicle 15" records: "If a karmically ill person inwardly applies contemplative effort and outwardly repents, improvement will follow."}
{align:justify|This self-awakening and self-examination is something every Buddhist must do — just as the Bequeathed Teachings Sutra reminds us: "Always examine yourselves; never commit faults."}
{align:justify|We must constantly examine ourselves, observing each deed of our own making, watching every arising thought; the moment we spot anything wrong or contrary to the Dharma, we must at once repent and reform immediately, giving faults and wrongs no chance to grow!}
{align:justify|Further explanation: Repentance matters greatly, for its power is immense. In the Sutra in Forty-Two Sections, the Buddha says: One who creates many faults yet neither repents nor halts the erring mind will have offenses dogging him like water returning to the sea, gradually growing deep and wide. But one with faults who confesses, owns them, and resolves to abandon evil for good will see offenses dissolve like sweat breaking in illness, gradually regaining health." Understanding cause and effect, honestly owning faults and never repeating them — such offenses can indeed be rescued.}

{align:justify|Below is the story of a Buddhist friend. Let us hear her tell it in her own words:}
{align:justify|September 2006 was the most painful time of my life. After long years of bodily illness, I went to the hospital for tests and learned I had end-stage malignant breast cancer, of the most severe degree.}
{align:justify|Before this brutal truth, I felt utterly hopeless, helpless, furious... my spirit nearly collapsed. A hundred thousand times I asked myself: "Why am I so ill?"... I considered myself kind and honest. In everyone's eyes I was a most virtuous woman, and those fine qualities were always recognized!}
{align:justify|But why, reaching middle age, did I receive a diagnosis like a "death sentence"? Each passing day carried me closer to death. To survive, I had to have surgery. After the operation came the torment of chemotherapy...}
{align:justify|A cancer patient treated with chemotherapy suffers unboundedly — a living hell on earth. Psychologically I could accept it, but my body could hardly bear it, unable to withstand the grinding pain; wanting to recover my health, I strained to battle against fate...}
{align:justify|Above me I had a mother past eighty, below me small children — all needing me. I was duty-bound to care for and shelter them... yet facing my own condition, I truly no longer had the courage to live. I was like a broken car with severed brakes, plunging into a dark abyss, shattered, with nothing left...}
{align:justify|During radiotherapy, lying in the oncology ward, I witnessed every scene, every kind of person: though you once wielded earth-shaking power, though you swam in wealth — once inside here, power and money mean nothing, for they cannot buy life. And life at such moments is like a dewdrop on grass tips — there, yet in an instant dissolving without a trace... Today you see a sick friend; tomorrow you are parted by life and death...}
{align:justify|Undergoing hospital treatment now is like... holding the god of death back... a little longer — but in the end the body still perishes!... I had never thought I would get cancer, had never come close to or observed the suffering sick of the world; the pain of parting the living from the dead is truly hard to tear away...}
{align:justify|I asked the doctor:}
{align:justify|"How much longer can I live?"}
{align:justify|I knew I must die, without a doubt — and so despaired, able to beg help from no one, silently swallowing pain into my heart and sinking further...}
{align:justify|I began to lose sleep, my limbs sweating, my heart panicking. All day my face streamed with tears; I wept without a voice...}
{align:justify|One day in the hospital I suddenly heard Angela Chang's song "Invisible Wings." Its lyrics hold these lines: "I know I have a pair of invisible wings carrying me high, out of despair"...}
{align:justify|Hearing this, the grief I had long suppressed burst forth like a volcano erupting... I broke down weeping — weeping for my wretched fate, weeping that I had not guarded my health, so that now toward my aged mother I was unfilial, toward my young children no loving mother, toward my husband no dutiful wife but a burden, leaving him worried, body and mind worn thin with utter exhaustion... so unkind to my husband!}
{align:justify|I did not know where those "wings carrying me high out of despair" could be. I had nothing to cling to, dazed and panicking... feeling that living was worse than death — yet death? — I could not accept it!}
{align:justify|After half a year of chemotherapy, I received what medicine calls "radiotherapy complete!" The doctor told me to return for regular checks, to catch and handle things in time... I knew I had been placed on the incurable list, with no chance of survival; if anything changed it would only be... a new drug! I would keep taking chemo steadily until I died! My life now depended on chemo. Stop the chemo and life ended too.}
{align:justify|To stay alive, every day before dawn I rose to practice qigong outdoors. Four hours a day. In the sweltering summer, and because of chemo my hair had all fallen out so I wore a wig — the stifling heat poured sweat over me; in the dog-days the scorching air plastered my damp clothes to my body. In the park I felt exactly like a tormented donkey circling round and round... Waves of bitter, resentful, hopeless thoughts surged from the bottom of my heart, aching to the marrow, tears raining down.}
{align:justify|In June 2007, laywoman Kim — a kindred-spirit Dharma friend — entered my life. She is a devout, kind, honest Buddhist who treats people very well. Once she knew my situation, she encouraged and helped me constantly, bringing me many Buddhist books (showing me ways to save my life); among them the two that influenced me most deeply were "The Four Lessons of Liao-Fan" and "Life Means Striving Bit by Bit Yourself"...}
{align:justify|Though I love philosophy, I only liked scholarly books; as for beliefs and faiths, I believed none of it, found it hard to accept... let alone uphold? I even rejected it as foolish, ignorant, superstitious talk!...}
{align:justify|But after reading the stories in those books, I was deeply moved; I, an atheist of no faith, began to believe in the Buddha. Honestly, though, my faith at that time sprang only from the thirst to live.}
{align:justify|Under laywoman Kim's guidance and urging, I began reciting one "Ksitigarbha Sutra" a day — but my mind surged in thought after thought like breakers, so she had me recite the "Diamond Sutra" and took me to release life and repent.}
{align:justify|To be honest, in that phase I threw myself into making merit, thinking "cultivating blessings can prolong life." With that mindset, though I recited the "Diamond Sutra," at times I felt I could neither see through nor let go, and afflictions never ceased.}
{align:justify|In the blink of an eye summer passed and winter came; the cold set in. Hearing that gravely ill fellow patients were dying one after another, or being transferred with illnesses flaring worse in the freezing winter... my heart grew colder still, collapsing to rock bottom... as though sinking to the very floor of the abyss of despair...}
{align:justify|Before the spring of 2008, seeing my desperate state, Sister Kim said:}
{align:justify|"If only you could meet layman Qua Khanh!"}
{align:justify|But where to find him, where to go to meet him? I counted myself thin-blessed and ill-fated, without such luck.}
{align:justify|From that day on, laywoman Kim daily bowed before the Buddha statue in her home (bowing on my behalf), a hundred bows a day, bowing until sweat and tears streamed down her face. Sincerity prevails — it can crack even metal and stone... Thanks to her sincere prayers, wholesome conditions were moved to come...}
{align:justify|I was fortunate to meet Hai Ba (a figure mentioned in Karmic Retribution in the Present Life, volumes 1 and 2), a friend of old layman Qua Khanh; Hai Ba promised to take me to meet layman Qua Khanh on the fifth day of the Lunar New Year.}
{align:justify|At the time Hai Ba's child was ill, but she spared no time to care for the baby, being busy guiding me instead. Hai Ba has big, black, beautifully bright eyes, an air noble as a celestial being, and a gentle, simple manner... I am deeply indebted to her sincere kindness. She is an extremely precious friend of mine (hereby I send her my blessings, praying she be safe and happy all her life!)...}
{align:justify|The moment I met layman Qua Khanh, I was moved by his virtuous cultivation and his noble, upright, kind, humble character, untouched by greed for money or things... He kindly reminded me to always take the precepts as teacher, gently explaining what the Buddha's five precepts are and how to receive and hold the "Ksitigarbha Sutra." He helped me straighten my crooked, mistaken views, and I gained a great deal. Right then I vowed: "From this day on I forever give up meat, become fully and permanently vegetarian, and resolutely create no more killing karma."}
{align:justify|After I returned home, the next day at eight in the evening layman Qua Khanh telephoned and said:}
{align:justify|"Would you like to know what caused your illness?"}
{align:justify|"Yes, I would! Very much!..."}
{align:justify|I answered breathlessly, before he had even finished. I assumed he would surely say "in a past life I created killing karma or some such offense, and so today I suffer evil retribution and must die young like this"...}
{align:justify|My heart pounded in suspense... waiting for his answer. But contrary to my guess, his answer was a complete surprise, utterly beyond imagining!... Over the phone, the professor gently asked:}
{align:justify|"Think carefully: after marrying, did you do anything over the line with someone of the opposite sex?"}
{align:justify|Hearing his question, I nearly toppled over. I fell silent, yet from the bottom of my heart I wanted to shout: "Good heavens! Professor! Don't wrong me! I am a decent girl from a respectable, well-bred family — in my life I have loved only once, with one single love for the man I married, my husband alone! I gave him all my pure feelings... I truly never had any 'one-night' affair, never committed adultery — so how can you say... that my illness comes from such a bizarre cause?"...}
{align:justify|Hearing no answer from me, the professor solemnly affirmed:}
{align:justify|"Your illness truly stems from... sexual misconduct! If you repent earnestly, your illness will heal!"}
{align:justify|The professor patiently waited for my answer. I strained to recall the past... bit by bit. Suddenly I remembered, and said:}
{align:justify|"Ah! After marrying and returning from honeymoon, my office offered to allocate housing. The housing manager asked me to come discuss it..."}
{align:justify|When I arrived, we talked for an hour about nothing but work and studies... Seeing night fall, I meant to go home when suddenly he threw his arms around me, saying he adored me"... We only embraced a moment — absolutely nothing over the line happened..."}
{align:justify|Hearing this, the professor said:}
{align:justify|"That is the very root of your illness. Though in fact nothing happened, in your heart you did not refuse at all — afterward your mind often gave rise to deviant thoughts, harboring... lustful thoughts! The 'Ksitigarbha Sutra' teaches: 'Sentient beings of Jambudvipa — every arising thought creates karma, every arising thought is an offense'..."}
{align:justify|Hanging up the phone, my face was drenched in tears; I wept without a voice. A hundred thousand times I had asked blue heaven why I was so ill — and today the professor had given me the exact answer!}
{align:justify|From that day on, I entered a phase of true Buddhist study never before experienced. From disbelieving the Buddha, to believing for survival and gain — then after meeting layman Qua Khanh, sincerely keeping the precepts and cultivating earnestly. I vow this is the greatest turning point of my life.}
{align:justify|How fortunate I was that, amid confusion and turmoil, I met a wise teacher of deep cultivation and great virtue — especially the books "Karmic Retribution in the Present Life," "Conversations on Compassion" and the "Jeweled Repentance of Nirvana," which like thunderclaps opened my stone-deaf ears. They helped me transform my life and change my unwholesome views of Buddhism. I have read and re-read these books countless times, practicing, reforming and cultivating by them...}
{align:justify|First, toward the evil deeds I had created, I undertook repentance... examining faults across "killing, stealing, lust, lying, drink, greed, anger, delusion, pride, doubt, filiality, propriety, righteousness, trust"... each day I repented one category of offense (reckoning from childhood to the present). I recalled and reviewed every matter great and small; whatever I found wrong or faulty, I repented — and this nurtured a fine habit of daily self-examination and reflection... I reformed with keen anguish, repented sincerely, then followed the guidance in "Conversations on Compassion" and the "Jeweled Repentance of Nirvana" in receiving and holding the "Ksitigarbha Sutra," strictly keeping the five precepts and cutting off meat entirely.}
{align:justify|As a result, I came back to life. What is more, my mind is at peace — able to face brutal reality directly, to calmly accept every oncoming suffering without a word blaming heaven or others. I can calmly face life and death, finding hope from within despair, and even in hell still dream of heaven...}
{align:justify|The Buddha Dharma is the wondrous Dharma — it has fastened wings on me, lifting me high out of the abyss of despair to the land of happiness...}
{align:justify|I thank the Buddhas and Bodhisattvas; I thank all the kind friends who labored heart and strength for me.}
{align:justify|Now, whether retelling or recounting the anguish, it aches like splitting open a grave, reopening a wound just healed. But I hope that sisters with affinity who read this — whether you believe in the Buddha or not (believe or not, as you will) — need only purify your own minds; best of all, give up meat and stay permanently vegetarian; at the very least, be someone who never kills any living being! Never do anything improper or unlawful! This world is truly heaven's vast net — loose, yet letting nothing through. Cause and effect is not for you to toy with, mock or decide... If you err, hurry to repent in time, and never think muddled thoughts or speak and act wrongly... drawing evil retribution upon yourself. For I walked out of a hell on earth, so I know to the bone how bitter, how painful it is!...}
{align:justify|If after reading my story you awaken and gain benefit, then this illness of mine was not suffered in vain...}
{align:justify|I hope I am the last to receive retribution in hell; I hope all the more that hell's gates will close behind my back. May my beloved sisters, though living in this society awash with materialism and desire, always keep for yourselves kind, pure, undefiled hearts... for those are the wings that carry you flying across to happiness, on to heaven...}
{align:justify|***}
{align:justify|Supplementary note: It is two years since I fell ill; every hospital checkup since has come back normal. I thank the Buddhas' empowering protection for granting me results as fine as today's.}
{align:justify|In extreme joy, I have shared my heartfelt gains and the benefits won on the Buddhist path with fellow patients. Those who heard, believed, accepted, became vegetarian, kept the precepts and repented... all saw their illnesses improve beyond imagining. As for patients who mocked and jeered at this, my heart is truly sad and sorry — for they have thrown away their chance at self-rescue... their conditions only worsen, beyond turning...}
{align:justify|I firmly believe that cancer patients need only practice according to the Dharma, and life will surely show miracles. May all the suffering sick under heaven use the Buddha Dharma to rescue themselves from peril.}
{align:justify|With true hearts, sincere hearts, vowing hearts, sow the seeds of good to help people renew their lives and rebuild happiness. I believe: if I can do it, so can you!}
{align:justify|A newly studying disciple: Co Hoa}
{align:justify|Explanation: While vowing to become vegetarian, after months of repentance Co Hoa was able to return to work, and each week she visits the Cancer Hospital, using her own experience as proof to counsel fellow patients, even gifting them the "Ksitigarbha Sutra" and "Karmic Retribution in the Present Life"... To this day a great many cancer patients have turned peril into safety.}
{align:justify|Usually, most patients only want to be cured but refuse to change the causes of their illness. That Co Hoa healed so fast comes precisely from her "practicing according to the teachings." On one hand she relied on the Buddhas' empowering support, unfailingly reciting sutras and the Buddha's name; on the other she sincerely repented, vowing to reform and cultivate good... repenting every past fault from childhood to the present, nurturing a habit of daily vigilance, constantly examining faults and reforming... May all patients under heaven practice likewise, soon dissolving illness, leaving suffering for joy.}

{align:justify|This is the beneficial experience layman Su underwent after studying Buddhism. May everyone increase their faith in Buddhist study through it. Let us hear him tell it:}
{align:justify|In early winter 1998, my wife unexpectedly became pregnant by accident. At the time I was swamped with research work, while my wife's business had just opened — so we made a decision we should not have: my wife went to the hospital for an abortion. Little did we know this rash act would change the second half of our lives. My wife was tormented by a dangerous illness for years, and thanks to it we too awakened and together entered the path of Buddhist study.}
{align:justify|In 2003 my wife found the area near her rectum aching unbearably; the doctor diagnosed "suppurative perianal gland inflammation" and told her she needed surgery — but after surgery the pain stayed exactly the same.}
{align:justify|Later, a specialist at the "Provincial Maternity Hospital" diagnosed "endometriosis." Reading the relevant materials, we learned this illness is extremely grave.}
{align:justify|It inflames the intestinal walls, narrowing and blocking the bowels, causing constipation. Constant, chronic constipation also affects the cycle... during menstruation there can be severe constipation... Moreover, patients constantly suffer discomforts such as the urge to defecate, pain on passing stool, even bloody stools.}
{align:justify|Other symptoms appear, such as bowel obstruction or distension — causing constipation, bloating, bellyache, nausea, discomfort...}
{align:justify|This is the century's incurable disease, whose chief symptoms are inability to conceive and great pain. It is very hard to cure, for after treatment it may relapse at any time — ranked as an "immortal cancer," it torments patients endlessly...}
{align:justify|Every month my wife was racked with deathly pain; the pain arrived on schedule with her cycle, tormenting her for four or five days straight, gravely affecting her health, work and life...}
{align:justify|I suffered along with her. For every time I saw my wife stretched on the bed, her face white, her strength nearly drained by pain, that suffering instantly enveloped my whole body too, leaving my heart gasping as though pressed by stone — unbearably heavy and oppressive.}
{align:justify|The doctor suggested my wife try conceiving, since pregnancy and nursing might ease the illness — but for someone with "endometriosis," normal conception had suddenly become far too arduous. Over a year of effort brought no result, and we had nearly lost all hope.}
{align:justify|In March 2005, my wife developed a left ovarian cyst and had to be hospitalized for surgery, removing the suppurating ovarian region. As for the "suppurative perianal gland inflammation," many nerves and blood vessels run through that area, so surgery there would be very dangerous — the doctor dared not touch it. After the ovary was removed, the doctor again suggested conceiving, saying the critical months of pregnancy were months seven and eight — get past those two months and there would be nothing to fear, "clear skies and calm seas"...}
{align:justify|In August we went to Mount Putuo to venerate Guanyin at Puji Temple and pray for a child. Miraculous to tell, just over a month after returning home from Mount Putuo, my wife had happy news — we wept with joy.}
{align:justify|Then, for the following year, thanks to pregnancy and nursing, my wife enjoyed peaceful days of pain-free life — but "happy scenes seldom last." Just after our daughter was weaned, the very next month my wife was horrified to find the long-quiet pain suddenly striking again... By then we were nearly at a dead end, helpless, grieving without tears...}
{align:justify|Yet at that very moment miraculous fortune smiled and opened a new road for us. We began stepping onto the path "back to our homeland" under the guidance of the Buddhas and Bodhisattvas.}
{align:justify|One autumn night in 2005, Avalokiteshvara Bodhisattva with wondrous awesome power once more bestowed grace upon us. That day, Lufeng Chan Monastery in Shaoxing held the eye-opening ceremony for its Avalokiteshvara statue at exactly one in the morning. Wishing to fulfill my vow for the child She had granted, I climbed the mountain to attend.}
{align:justify|Deep that night, at the appointed hour, Buddhist music began to play and the Bodhisattva's statue glowed pinkish-red all around. I heard people say with feeling:}
{align:justify|"She has come! She has come!..."}
{align:justify|At this the crowd outside the hall buzzed and craned upward; I looked up too... and saw in midair — though it was night — a dazzling shaft of light, the Buddha-light clearly visible, swelling larger, flashing once each second, brilliant yet never dazzling; the radiance in the sky came from the southwest, drifting gradually to above the roof of the Guanyin shrine hall...}
{align:justify|Recalling it later, the Buddha-light shone and flashed more than twenty times, I suppose; I was so excited and amazed my mind went blank and empty... Six or seven hundred people present were likewise rapt and dazed...}
{align:justify|I can affirm that the night sky was extremely clear and bright, without a shred of dark cloud or lightning — this radiance was no electric lamp or man-made light, for its area and brilliance were a hundred times greater than artificial light. Moreover, the light came from high in the sky, and the witnesses each saw different images: some saw lotuses, others pink radiance... every manner...}
{align:justify|From that night, I began to believe that Buddhas and Bodhisattvas are no empty, absurd legend but truly exist and can be seen clearly...}
{align:justify|In 2006 I gradually encountered the Buddha Dharma; the first book to influence me enormously was layman Qua Khanh's "Karmic Retribution in the Present Life," which helped me understand how dreadful cause and effect is — that retribution never errs by a hair.}
{align:justify|I knew my wife's terrible illness came from cause and effect, but newly entered into Buddhism, I did not yet understand its exact cause or how to resolve it.}
{align:justify|Past eight in the evening on November 18, 2006, having just heard a teaching at Xuanzang Temple in Nanjing, I sat in a car covering 400 kilometers home. Having just heard the Dharma, my fervor surged like the tide, and I kept silently asking:}
{align:justify|"Could... I resolutely give up meat and stay permanently vegetarian?"... But the thought had barely formed when I hesitated, remembering all the delicious meats tasted over decades past — beef, chicken, shrimp, fish... every temptation. Having to abandon those beloved dishes made me waver, thinking: "Should I... eat meat a few more years?"...}
{align:justify|Suddenly my mobile phone rang shrilly; my wife's flustered voice reported: our daughter has caught a cold — she can neither drink milk nor sleep, crying and screaming since morning, a dozen hours by now — the grown-ups and the baby alike utterly exhausted...}
{align:justify|Taking the call, my heart burned like fire; I wished for wings to fly home at once. Just as I fretted in turmoil, a thought suddenly struck me: "Doesn't Buddhism say: every sincere plea is answered?"...}
{align:justify|So I folded my palms and sincerely prayed: "May the Buddhas and Bodhisattvas bless my daughter to drink milk and sleep — I vow from this day on to stay permanently vegetarian!"...}
{align:justify|Within ten minutes, my wife called back with the news:}
{align:justify|"Don't worry any more — she's drunk her milk and fallen asleep!"}
{align:justify|At once I was moved to streaming tears, feeling this truly miraculous — the Buddhas and Bodhisattvas so compassionate! They knew full well the unwholesome thoughts in my head and used these conditions to press me to quickly abandon meat for vegetarianism, cut off evil for good, and enter the true Buddhist path... From that day on, I became a strict vegetarian.}
{align:justify|In April 2007, I followed layman Qua Khanh to Shaanxi, my heart full of questions about my wife's illness, and earnestly sought his guidance. He settled his mind a moment and asked me:}
{align:justify|"Has your wife ever had an abortion?"}
{align:justify|When I confirmed it, he said decisively:}
{align:justify|"That is the cause!"}
{align:justify|And he gently instructed me to become vegetarian and recite sutras to deliver the fetus... In May, through him I met laywoman Qua Hong; regarding my wife's condition, she affirmed: "It is surely the abortion that drew such bitter fruit!"...}
{align:justify|She told me to urge my wife to become vegetarian, sincerely repent her created offenses, and recite the "Sutra Spoken by the Buddha on Longevity, Extinguishing Offenses and Protecting Children Dharani" — and without the slightest politeness, she bluntly pinpointed with utter accuracy the killing, stealing, lust and lying... offenses I had committed, leaving my heart violently shaken, remorse and shame intermingled... She showed me the Buddha Dharma's miraculous knowledge and firmed my faith all the more: "if we practice correctly according to the Buddha Dharma, we can cure this incurable disease"...}
{align:justify|In May 2007, under my coaxing both gentle and stern — kindness and authority in full — my wife reluctantly began abandoning meat for vegetarianism. She had always been a gourmet, fond of rich delicacies... but now, facing an unbearable, painful illness, she had to yield a step — though most unwillingly, her heart half-believing, half-doubting...}
{align:justify|Indeed, for an atheist like her who had never touched the Buddha Dharma, making her recite sutras was far harder than making her vegetarian; so no matter how I demanded, she flatly refused to recite sutras or the Buddha's name.}
{align:justify|In July, I resolutely quit my work and career in Beijing and returned to my wife's side. The sutra recitation... I would undertake and complete. I understood my two weighty duties:}

{align:justify|On the 8th of August I began reciting the "Sutra Spoken by the Buddha on Longevity, Extinguishing Offenses and Protecting Children Dharani" and the "Diamond Sutra," one or two recitations a day. Of course, before and after each recitation, I always sincerely spoke to the baby, explaining that the abortion of those days pains us deeply; now I repent my foolish, cruel offense and soothe the infant's resentful, despairing heart... At times grief choked me until I wept without a voice...}
{align:justify|Something miraculous happened: by month's end, when her cycle came, my wife no longer suffered wrenching pain... At the time we put it down to acupuncture and medicine...}
{align:justify|On September 10th my wife stopped acupuncture and all medicine; I kept reciting diligently, and my wife once more came through safe and sound. But we still supposed it might be aftereffects of the one-sided ovary surgery (in 2005), with female hormones low and so on.}
{align:justify|Come November, when my wife went to hospital to test her female hormones, every result was normal. Still uneasy, we went to ask laywoman Qua Hong. She confirmed:}
{align:justify|"The baby has truly gone — and left with a very happy heart..."}
{align:justify|Only then were we convinced. During this time something amusing happened. By early spring 2008, my wife had been healed six months and had nearly forgotten the tormenting pain... Especially facing the New Year feast tables, table after table laden with fish and flesh, piled with rich delicacies, her gourmet nature was all the more provoked; she smacked her lips hungrily and several times sighed to me plaintively:}
{align:justify|"Honey, staying vegetarian all my life like this — I can't bear it! Come this April it will be a full year vegetarian; then I'll eat meat again..."}
{align:justify|She had barely declared this when that very first lunar month the dreadful illness suddenly reappeared, seemingly flaring from ambush and throwing my wife into panic and fear...}
{align:justify|Luckily, that day we were traveling with laywoman Qua Hong to Shanghai, Nanjing and Hangzhou, and with several laypeople to release life at West Lake (all connected with this life-release trip was aired by Zhejiang Television and reported in the Qianjiang newspaper on December 25th). At lunch in a vegetarian restaurant, I asked Qua Hong (seated at the same table):}
{align:justify|"My wife hurts again — has the baby come back?"}
{align:justify|Qua Hong answered:}
{align:justify|"No! Your wife spoke falsely — SHE BROKE FAITH AND HER VOW!..."}
{align:justify|My wife sat beside us pale-faced, so frightened she no longer dared breathe a word about "eating meat again"... Naturally the next month, thanks to my wife reining in before the abyss (halting in time before peril), the cruel illness no longer recurred to rage. From then on my wife reformed for good, never daring to speak or even dream of eating meat, and she has stayed well to this very day.}
{align:justify|Once more the law of cause and effect "taught" us a lesson: we have learned "three feet above our heads there are spirits," understanding that every gesture and word, each arising thought of ours... all lie within the spirits' sight, all governed and driven by the law of cause and effect.}
{align:justify|Nearly two years have passed; my wife is free of the dread illness, and constipation and every ailment great and small have gradually dissolved. My doctor friends say:}
{align:justify|"Usually this disease only worsens — your wife healed with no surgery and no medicine. That must be called a great miracle!"}
{align:justify|We thank the Buddha Dharma; we thank the wise teachers who patiently guided us to smash delusion and abandon evil. We thank our child for understanding, forgiving, and letting go of hatred to leave. Above all we thank the baby for using this means to save its foolish parents, making us halt before the abyss, swim against the current, and leave the land of delusion.}
{align:justify|Further explanation: The miracle of "coming back to life from death" arises from an awakened, reforming mind. One who does not wish to die must first know how to "die while still alive" — meaning all bad habits must die! (Greed, anger, delusion, pride, doubt, wrong views... and whatever runs counter to the true Dharma must die clean away)... Moreover, one must sincerely repent, reform faults, nurture virtue... cultivate properly according to moral discipline, meditation and wisdom — then life will change. Once the Buddha Dharma is understood, even miracles are perfectly ordinary!}

{align:justify|I first met Diem Tuyet at a "Water and Land Dharma Assembly" held at the temple. She stood behind me then; with the crowd pressing, I was jostled into Diem Tuyet. Later, learning she had been pregnant for months, I found her a seat to rest. After the ceremony, Diem Tuyet trusted me utterly and told me, grief-stricken, about her family.}
{align:justify|Diem Tuyet is 26, married over two years. Newly wed, her husband Mr. Truong was attentive and loving, fussing over her in every way — Diem Tuyet was overjoyed, weaving bright dreams of the future. But alas, "happiness seldom lasts" — before long, their cozy home was shattered by the arrival of a third person, Miss Diep.}
{align:justify|At first Mr. Truong only flirted secretly by phone... though she chanced upon Miss Diep's messages, Diem Tuyet naively suspected nothing. But then she saw her husband lingering out after work, not even coming home nights — Diem Tuyet grew uneasy inside, yet still insisted to herself that "perhaps my husband is just too busy to care for me," not daring to think the worst, for by then she was pregnant.}
{align:justify|One winter day, she accidentally discovered her husband's improper liaison with Miss Diep, and all Diem Tuyet's hopes crumbled on the spot...}
{align:justify|The truth was harsher still: her husband openly admitted his love for the Diep woman, saying they already lived together! Before her husband's betrayal, Diem Tuyet was crushed with disappointment, her heart torn apart... furious to the extreme... and so a quarrel erupted — though neither yet mentioned divorce. Tormented a hundred ways, longing for peace and release, Diem Tuyet turned to the Buddha Dharma and diligently attended temple ceremonies. She hoped the Buddhas' empowering support would ease her pain... Seeing Diem Tuyet, pregnant, sobbing out her story without a voice, my heart filled with pity and sympathy... I took her cold hands, comforting her without cease... In truth, this suffering sprang from a past-life story of her own making:}
{align:justify|"In the 1930s, among Shanghai's pleasure-loving wealthy aristocrats, there was a famous young businessman named Ky. At an evening banquet, Ky met the virtuous, noble wife of a tycoon named Tran. Though Ky already had wife and children, Mrs. Tran's beauty set his heart trembling with love; and so Ky schemed to conquer the beauty at all costs...}
{align:justify|And so every few days he visited the Tran home, bearing costly jewelry... At last, young Mrs. Tran was 'won over' and fell into Ky's arms. When Mr. Tran learned of it, heaven and earth crumbled beneath him; for the clan's honor he swallowed his rage, enduring, gulping down all bitterness... From then on, Mr. Tran knew only wine to drown his sorrows, drunk all day, abandoning himself to ruin — and so the family fortune swiftly crumbled to decay.}
{align:justify|As for Ky and Mrs. Tran, their tempers clashed and they quarreled constantly; in the end they too parted. Both families fell into 'wife parted, children scattered,' ending in tragedy"...}
{align:justify|The dashing playboy Ky of that past life is none other than Diem Tuyet today. Mrs. Tran has been reborn as Mr. Truong (Diem Tuyet's present husband). As for Mr. Tran, the heartbroken, betrayed husband, he is now Miss Diep (appearing as the third person), intruding to shatter Diem Tuyet's home (returning to her foe the pain of having a spouse stolen in that distant life)...}
{align:justify|In this life, the relationship (run by the law of karmic debt and repayment) has brought the three together again — only the genders have changed. But through evil karma created long ago, those involved must taste anew the bitterness of this marital tragedy!}
{align:justify|I told Diem Tuyet:}
{align:justify|"Do not give rise to hatred toward your husband any more, for in the past you stole another's wife — and so in this life you taste abandonment, robbed... in turn!"}
{align:justify|Having entered Buddhist study, you must deeply believe in cause and effect; repent the sinful karma you created in past lives, recite 49 "Ksitigarbha Sutras" dedicated to your husband and Miss Diep to atone for ancient faults... If you sincerely repent, recite sutras, become vegetarian, release life and so on... your future life will be happy.}
{align:justify|Diem Tuyet, eyes full of tears, smiled and nodded.}
{align:justify|Later, meeting Diem Tuyet again, I hardly recognized her — she looked years younger, her face rosy, her smile radiant. It was easy to see she had found liberated peace through the Buddha Dharma!}

{align:justify|Hieu My is a beautiful, wealthy, successful businesswoman. She and her husband An Vi lived happily with two children — a boy and a girl, bright and obedient... Everyone praised them as an ideal couple. But Hieu My's very beauty, success and charm led her into error, wrecking her own home with her own hands.}
{align:justify|On one business trip, Hieu My met a young man named Dien; the two fell in love "at first sight," feeling wonderfully in tune at heart — their love deepening daily. They drowned in love's net, loving each other madly, to death and back...}
{align:justify|To possess Hieu My forever, Mr. Dien took the initiative to seek out An Vi, announcing their intimate relationship and proposing An Vi divorce his wife.}
{align:justify|An Vi came home and in fury beat Hieu My before their two children. Beaten, Hieu My burned with rage and insisted on divorce, but An Vi refused. Through friends' introduction, Hieu My came to me, her face haggard and pale, weeping out what had happened...}
{align:justify|Hearing her out, I said:}
{align:justify|"Right now you cannot divorce, for this bond between you and Mr. Dien is sexual misconduct! It is a sinful love — pursue it further and suffering will only multiply! For Mr. Dien already has a wife, a peaceful family, and a daughter besides..."}
{align:justify|Pausing a moment, I went on:}
{align:justify|"You and Mr. Dien, over a moment's emotional stirring, demand to throw away everything to unite — then the retribution you both must bear will surely be tragic!..."}
{align:justify|Seeing Hieu My's doubting eyes, I warned:}
{align:justify|"Even united now, before long you two will have to part. For you have not ended killing karma, still eat meat, and now add the grave offense of sexual misconduct!... If you do not repent, you will contract a dangerous gynecological disease and die before forty..."}
{align:justify|Hieu My heard this in alarm and hastily asked:}
{align:justify|"Lately my lower belly aches dully and unpleasantly — will I really get a gynecological illness? I'm 36 this year — how can I escape this calamity?..."}
{align:justify|"Return to your husband, devote yourself fully to your duties as mother and wife, care for husband and children attentively, and cut off Mr. Dien completely — no more entanglement! From today you must recite 49 'Ksitigarbha Sutras,' become vegetarian, release life, strictly keep the five precepts, diligently study Buddhism and cultivate..."}
{align:justify|Hearing this, Hieu My nodded and said:}
{align:justify|"I'll go home and do as you teach."}
{align:justify|Half a month later, Hieu My — face rosy and fresh — came to visit us... The moment we met, she took my hand and said:}
{align:justify|"I've cut him off — I no longer see Mr. Dien..."}
{align:justify|"Remember to persevere in reciting and dedicating, cut off evil and practice good, keep the precepts in cultivation — count it as renewing your whole life from here..."}
{align:justify|Two months later, Hieu My's life had turned for the better. Daily she recited sutras, repented past errors, and quietly practiced good and stored virtue. What is more, her whole family had begun vegetarianism and Buddhist study; family life grew happy and warm... and so the children thrive, healthy and advancing.}
{align:justify|As for Mr. Dien, at first unwilling to give up, refusing to leave Hieu My... I had to coax, explain, and weigh the pros and cons every way before things were finally settled.}
{align:justify|Relating this story, I hope every home on earth lives in harmony and joy. May all husbands and wives stay faithful to one another in mutual love. Please, never stray into affairs over a moment's stirring. The retribution for sexual misconduct is dreadful — besides ending "person lost, home wrecked"... it summons tragic retribution in hell. The moment you give rise to adulterous thoughts or nurse deviant intentions, you shatter your own present blessings — wealth, fortune and power all sliding!... Everyone thinks only killing shortens life, but in truth sexual misconduct likewise draws dangerous illnesses, scattered wealth, lost blessings and shortened life!... Dead, one still enters hell to suffer.}
{align:justify|May everyone strictly keep the precepts and never let outer scenes seduce them... If you have strayed into sexual misconduct, resolve here and now to quit, earnestly reform past faults, vow repentance, and never repeat it!... Only thus can you turn your destiny beautiful. Translator's note:}
{align:justify|Hieu My committed adultery and was warned by Qua Hong that persisting would bring no happiness with her lover — plus an incurable gynecological disease and early death!}
{align:justify|No doubt some readers will ask: "We see many adulterers in this world who leave to build new lovebirds' nests with no retribution at all?..."}
{align:justify|We may explain it thus: evildoers not yet repaid at once still have abundant blessings. Once blessings wane, retribution strikes... Besides, you only watch from outside — never shadowing them (nor living it as the insider, feeling the miseries the 'adulterer' must wretchedly taste). Follow them long enough and you will see most cases unfold exactly as Qua Hong warned...}
{align:justify|Moreover, in Diem Tuyet's story, Miss Diep — though the third person — appears to collect a debt and take revenge... (returning the sufferings the other once sowed for her in a past life). For the one at fault here is Diem Tuyet (so in this life she may not complain) — on the contrary, she must create much blessed good to dedicate to Miss Diep, to redeem ancient faults...}
{align:justify|Hence those tangled, unjust love affairs — "betrayed yet seeing no payback, home wrecked yet seeing no repayment"... stem from hidden knots we do not yet understand; the law of cause and effect never errs. As for Hieu My's case — creating offenses out of nowhere — she will reap evil retribution exactly as Qua Hong warned...}
{align:justify|Most people err from not understanding the law of cause and effect; understanding it, they would never willingly create offenses... For compare: chasing a moment's feeling buys little joy, while the suffering to be repaid... is endless. On the scales, the wise will know to choose what truly benefits them...}
{align:justify|People often say the Buddha Dharma is world-transcending, nothing to do with this world — yet the Buddha Dharma "matches" this world beautifully. Let me tell you a true story I personally witnessed:}
{align:justify|I know Nha Lan, about 26, very beautiful, a film-school graduate; not long into her career she married a handsome man of matching family and retired, living ordinarily like every other home. But Nha Lan and her husband are both talented and beautiful — and talent and beauty in this world are ever the target of pursuit and rivalry"...}
{align:justify|And so Nha Lan's marriage stood on the brink of collapse — for each had admirers, and if husband and wife alike were unfaithful, parting would be natural.}
{align:justify|Once Nha Lan telephoned me, sobbing:}
{align:justify|"Auntie, I don't want to live any more — I think I'll die!"}
{align:justify|Hearing the child's lament, I understood at once: in life people usually grieve over "career, fame, feelings"... falling short of wishes. Nha Lan had everything, so I guessed only love could sadden her so...}
{align:justify|Surely her husband's many admirers had made her miserable. So I said:}
{align:justify|"Check whether in life you ever made anyone suffer?"}
{align:justify|"Yes — a great many, very many."}
{align:justify|"Then count it as repaying what you owe! Tasting fresh pain to sympathize with those you once made suffer..."}
{align:justify|At the time I was very busy — two sudden, important calls at once — so I had no time to say much to Nha Lan, and sad as she was, she did not want to talk much either... Later she told me that, just then, wanting suicide, my words "Count it as repaying what you owe" woke her up and resolved her to overcome the pain.}
{align:justify|Afterward an eminent monk guided the young couple; before the Buddha's image they vowed repentance for all past faults, pledged lifelong fidelity, strictly kept the five precepts, and practiced the Buddha Dharma...}
{align:justify|And yet, ever since that vow, husband and wife have both lived happily; keeping the Buddha's precepts, outdoors they spare no glance for outsiders, living utterly faithful, trusting each other, never lying... Today Nha Lan lives most peacefully; she once confided in me:}
{align:justify|I dream of no riches, fame or anything else — living contentedly like this, happy... is enough. Daily examining my own faults to never err, the more we practice the Buddha Dharma the happier we are. And so husband and wife trust each other absolutely... speak ill of me and he defends me at once; speak ill of him and I defend him!... for each knows the other keeps the precepts and dares create no offenses... No one fears affairs any more... no one bothers watching anyone... for the Buddha's precepts already guard us! We just live on like this, just keep storing virtue and blessings, planting seeds of peace from this life into lives to come...}
{align:justify|Hearing the child made me glad. Who says the Buddha Dharma brings this world no happiness!}

{align:justify|Medicine has now proven that sexual misconduct leads to many diseases — syphilis, gonorrhea, AIDS and so on... while the Buddha Dharma, viewing it from the cycle of the six realms, warns us that sexual misconduct draws evil retribution not only in this life but stretching into many lives to come...}
{align:justify|At a gathering of fellow practitioners, a young couple brought a little girl of about two, begging everyone for help. The baby's parents wept as they told of their daughter's misfortune...}
{align:justify|The little girl, only two years old, suffered an incurable malignant cancer — worse still, the tumor grew very large right at her vagina. Her parents felt "struck by lightning," unable to fathom why — the baby only two, why such a grave illness? More dreadful still, the doctor said the only treatment was surgery, with a very low chance of success; without surgery the disease would flare and endanger her life. And so the young couple were heartsick, exhausting every idea with no good answer.}
{align:justify|When the illness struck, the little girl suffered terribly — "begging life, denied; begging death, refused" — leaving relatives heartsick, all drowned in boundless pain.}
{align:justify|My fellow lay practitioners and I deeply sympathized with the baby, all the more convinced this was grave karmic retribution manifesting. I observed a while and discovered the cause:}
{align:justify|"Though this little girl is only two, in a past life she was a man who committed very grave sexual misconduct. Not only did he wreck others' families, he even raped and brutally murdered girls of good families, causing 'person lost, home wrecked' tragedies. The souls of his wrongfully killed victims, seething with hatred, vowed to haunt him for revenge — making the little girl suffer malignant cancer in this very life, tormented to the extreme."}
{align:justify|I solemnly told the little girl's parents:}
{align:justify|"You two must help your baby repent — recite 49 'Ksitigarbha Sutras' dedicated to those creditors."}
{align:justify|Before I had finished, with a sudden "thud" the little girl knelt before the Buddha statue, tears of remorse streaming down, her gaze full of pleading for us to save her (though she could barely speak). Everyone was amazed; her parents burst into tears. I pityingly lifted the skinny baby, looked into her dim, pleading eyes and said:}
{align:justify|"You must repent before the Buddha with your mother — whatever your mother reads, you read after her — and vow never again to harm any person or creature..."}
{align:justify|And turning to the baby's parents, I instructed:}
{align:justify|"You two must recite sutras, become vegetarian, and release much life for your baby, gathering every bit of good... dedicating the merit to the baby's karmic creditors, begging their forgiveness, letting go of hatred..."}
{align:justify|After returning home, all three knelt before the Buddha in sincere repentance; the parents did as I instructed, sincerely reciting sutras dedicated to those the child had harmed in the past... A miracle appeared: the next day the baby's pain eased greatly. Seeing this, the whole family grew more diligent in vegetarianism, precept-keeping, sutra recitation and Buddha-recitation... And so the baby's tumor began to shrink. Neighbors around, seeing the miracle upon the baby, likewise gathered to study Buddhism and practice.}
{align:justify|May their whole family soon leave suffering for joy and live healthy and happy. If only they stay vegetarian, recite sutras, diligently repent and reform, this little girl will surely recover.}
{align:justify|Finally, I urge those who indulge lust to take this as a mirror — never lose yourselves in sensuality, for it harms self and others alike. In this life it weakens the body and shortens life; in lives to come it brings every evil illness, even rebirth in hells or as animals... enduring full suffering... A human body is hard to obtain — treasure it!}

{align:justify|In early spring, on the train home for a visit, I met a young couple taking their three-year-old daughter to see a doctor. Talking with them, I learned the child's illness dated from birth and was very hard to cure.}
{align:justify|The child's father lifted her back for me to see — truly startling: the little girl's face is refined and lovely, yet her back bristles with burn blisters great and small, high and low... scattered in chaos (like someone gravely scalded by boiling water). Listening to the couple's anguished account... a moving scene appeared before me:}
{align:justify|"A gray-black wildcat often came to a certain farming couple's house to steal food... One day the husband bought ten ducks to raise in the garden. But each passing day two ducks went missing. On the third day he secretly lay in wait and found the culprit was indeed the wildcat. So husband and wife set a trap and caught it; they locked it in an iron cage, then jabbed and stabbed it cruelly with sharp sticks... beating while cursing:}
{align:justify|'You beast, daring to steal our ducks! You'll die no peaceful death!'"}
{align:justify|Seeing the wounded cat still struggling with all its might, they turned brutal — boiled water and poured it over its back... The wildcat screamed in agony until it died, and only then did husband and wife stop.}
{align:justify|At the time the wife was pregnant... the wildcat's consciousness, full of hatred, at once seized the womb (contending for the place — entering the fetus); as a result it was born as this family's child — the very little girl before my eyes..."}
{align:justify|I asked the couple:}
{align:justify|"Did you once scald a wildcat to death?"}
{align:justify|Hearing this, the husband paled, startled and amazed, answering tensely:}
{align:justify|"'Master,' how could you know that three years ago we killed a wildcat?"}
{align:justify|"Your daughter's illness is karmic illness — only Buddhist practice can help your whole family..."}
{align:justify|"Yes, Master, we all believe in the Buddha — every year we visit the temple to burn incense, bow to the Buddha and pray for peace..."}
{align:justify|"True Buddhist study is not about lighting incense with eyes closed, praying for peace. That is seeking the Dharma outside the mind! You must shed your hot tempers and unwholesome traits! After returning home, you must repent the killing karma created in the past, become vegetarian, recite 100 'Ksitigarbha Sutras,' and devote yourselves to life releases dedicated to your daughter. Because you two created killing karma and forged evil ties, you have such a child..." Husband and wife, frightened yet glad, looked remorseful. Right then they vowed to become vegetarian for the baby, recite and dedicate sutras, and practice well at home as instructed...}
{align:justify|Further explanation: We humans crown ourselves the most spiritual of all beings, yet out of selfish desire we slaughter living beings without a shred of pity. Master Hsuan Hua said: never, ever forge enmity with beings — otherwise, when retribution strikes, our suffering will be unbearable...}
{align:justify|If we believe in the Buddha, we must deeply believe in cause and effect and strictly keep the precepts. "Cutting off evil helps us shut the gates to hell. Cultivating good opens heaven's gates for us. Studying Buddhism means practicing — stepping onto the bright, radiant road out of the three realms"...}

{align:justify|Tiet Thanh and Su Dinh are a deeply loving couple. He is handsome and successful in his career; she is beautiful, noble and graceful... in everyone's eyes they are a golden boy and jade girl.}
{align:justify|Yet this couple, envied and admired by all, often sigh and mope. They have two daughters: the youngest, Bao Chau — obtained through "praying for a child" — is doted on by the whole family...}
{align:justify|Bao Chau is refined and lovely, very well-behaved with outsiders. But alone with family she always turns hot-tempered and furious, often screaming and hitting relatives on sight. For his daughter's sake, Tiet Thanh entered Buddhist study, often reciting sutras and releasing life... But Bao Chau improved not a whit — worse, she often fell sick with fevers... Strangely, whenever Tiet Thanh was away, these abnormal states all vanished or turned for the better.}
{align:justify|Worried sick over his darling daughter, Tiet Thanh spent countless money on Bao Chau; the enormous expenses plus his fretting wore down this successful man until life itself felt too bitter... At the end of his rope, Tiet Thanh came to me for help, lamenting:}
{align:justify|"I can't hold on any more — Bao Chau wears my heart and mind to exhaustion! Could I have owed her in a past life? If I truly owe her... how must I repay?"}
{align:justify|Hearing his gloomy account, I said:}
{align:justify|"Children are reborn to us through evil ties or wholesome ties... That Bao Chau torments her parents a hundredfold like this is truly no wholesome tie! In fact this evil tie was forged in 1930 on the Shanghai Bund, as follows:}
{align:justify|"At the police station (of the old Shanghai Bund), there were Chief Hu and his subordinate Tieu Quach — close friends trusting each other utterly. At the time a kidnapping case (a sensation in its day) occurred: a notorious underworld boss, Mr. X, seized a local rich man over a personal grudge. The rich man's family, beside themselves with worry, sought out Chief Hu to report the case and begged him to rescue their kin...}
{align:justify|But greedy-natured Chief Hu seized the chance to force the rich man's family to pay an enormous sum before he would act. Then he sent Tieu Quach to investigate...}
{align:justify|Though Tieu Quach found the case's mastermind, the boss X bribed old Hu and Tieu Quach with more money than the rich man — so the two policemen colluded to shield and free the criminal, offering the victim's family only token comfort... In the end the rich man died a tragic, wronged death (bullied and driven to death)... Chief Hu of that past life is Tiet Thanh today, while his trusted subordinate Tieu Quach is now his beloved wife Su Dinh.}
{align:justify|Because the rich man died nursing hatred, he vowed revenge and has now been reborn as the darling child Bao Chau. As for boss X, his crimes brimming over, to this day he still suffers torture in hell"...}
{align:justify|I told Tiet Thanh:}
{align:justify|"Though you have now begun Buddhist study and practice, your repentance is still too weak. That rich man was groundlessly murdered — how could he let go so easily?..."}
{align:justify|Therefore you two must strictly keep the precepts — become vegetarian and hold the five precepts: no killing, no stealing, no sexual misconduct, no lying, no drinking... always practice the ten good deeds and recite the "Ksitigarbha Sutra" dedicated to your daughter.}
{align:justify|You two must atone with persevering resolve, cultivating vigorously without laziness. Whatever great hardships you meet, understand them as trials... think 'I am repaying debts to dissolve karma'... If you strive to practice thus for some five or six years, you will surely harvest fine results.}
{align:justify|Half a year later, Tiet Thanh telephoned, his voice full of moved joy. He reported his daughter had truly turned for the better, while his beloved wife Su Dinh — once very frail — had gradually recovered her health... Through this, Tiet Thanh vowed to practice well and become a Buddhist family...}

{align:justify|Through boundless eons we have forged enmity with countless beings through the afflictions of greed, anger and delusion. If we do not soon release our sorrows, follow the Buddha Dharma, and leave the six realms, we will keep settling debts with one another in birth and death forever... When will such tit-for-tat ever end?}
{align:justify|Than Khoa Truong is a veteran cadre of the police force, conducting himself most strictly, never the least bit careless. Solving major cases several times, he earned great merits. Yet this illustrious career brought him no joy, for family troubles towered high and teeming... tormenting him without cease.}
{align:justify|Mr. Than has two sons. The elder, sober, is fine — but drunk, he beats people and raises pandemonium at home until neither chickens nor dogs rest. The younger constantly wheedles money from his parents (sometimes one or two thousand at a time), pouring it all into betting; losing at gambling, he comes home to beg more... This misery leaves the old couple washing their faces with tears daily. Mr. Than's heartache is beyond words — living worse than death...}
{align:justify|For consolation, they adopted a daughter, hoping for a little warmth in old age. The couple pondered endlessly, never understanding why they bore only grossly unfilial sons, always bringing their parents grief. Sitting across from the tear-drenched old couple, I keenly felt that "good and evil retribution truly follows like a shadow"... The seeds of fateful enmity they planted in the past are now flowering and fruiting, bringing unspeakable pain...}
{align:justify|"In ancient times long ago, there was a man poor as a church mouse, helpless and shelterless, lying sick by the roadside moaning, with no one to help — left to wait for death... A woman happened to pass by; seeing the poor man, she gave rise to pity and lent him half her fortune (her own savings). Borrowing the money, the poor man was overwhelmed with gratitude and swore: 'When rich one day, I will surely repay my benefactor generously and do all I can to repay this life-saving debt'..."}
{align:justify|With this capital the poor man earned a living, married, had children, and grew gradually comfortable — but forgot his woman benefactor entirely. As for the benefactress, having paid out to help the pauper, back home her family scolded and doubted her in every way; all her life she lived in gloomy misery... (That woman benefactor is the younger noble son today).}
{align:justify|After growing rich, the pauper one day went shopping with his wife; reaching a riverbank, they saw an old man slip and fall into the water... Far from rescuing him, husband and wife stood gawking, letting the old man drown — then seized his luggage for their own... (That drowned old man of old is their elder son today).}
{align:justify|The pauper couple of those days are the Than couple today. For past borrowing, seizing and stealing... in this life they must repay manyfold. Their two boys are creditors come to collect!"...}
{align:justify|Finishing, I warned:}
{align:justify|"If you two do not repent, practice, and perform many great meritorious deeds, in the future these two sons will torment you to death!"}
{align:justify|Old Than, learning the cause, was ten-thousandfold alarmed and at once vowed to repent diligently and enter Buddhist cultivation. He hopes the Buddhas' empowering support will dissolve all his fateful ties, that they may live peacefully in old age.}
{align:justify|Understanding cause and effect, we should never act carelessly. We must always warn ourselves, living purely and spotlessly every hour of every moment, never trusting to luck. For karmic retribution never errs.}

{align:justify|Mrs. Truong is the general director of a grand enterprise managing over ten thousand workers. This glorious success has drawn everyone's admiration. Yet at home, though husband and wife live warmly and happily, they are helpless before their precious son Cuong.}
{align:justify|Cuong is very bright — studious and quiet since childhood. In his parents' hearts he is a good boy, a priceless treasure. But ever since he entered middle school, he became addicted to online games, and many unwholesome things followed. Day and night Cuong lives online gaming, so his studies collapsed... Parents, teachers, friends — all advice and teaching slid off him like water off a duck's back. Cuong paid not the slightest heed, growing ever more depraved until he even beat his father and cursed his mother...}
{align:justify|One day Mrs. Truong earnestly urged her son to stop gaming; Cuong at once flared up — not only speaking rudely but striking his father and injuring his right eye. Furious, Mrs. Truong left home with her husband to stay temporarily with friends.}
{align:justify|When I met Mrs. Truong, her face was haggard, her eyes red; she lamented sorrowfully:}
{align:justify|"What on earth turned our Cuong into such a wretch? Grown-ups kindly, well-meaningly advise him, bring him tasty nutritious food... yet he won't even accept it — and even raised his hand against his father..."}
{align:justify|Pausing a moment, she went on:}
{align:justify|"He games so madly he's dropped out — over half a year now! His teacher often comes to ask after him, even bringing police to counsel him — all useless. Oh dear! Boo hoo..."}
{align:justify|Seeing Mrs. Truong in hopeless pain, I sympathized deeply and consoled her, explaining:}
{align:justify|"Having to embrace such pain and be seared by affliction today all stems from your own past-life evil causes!"}
{align:justify|"Mrs. Truong and her husband were husband and wife in a past life too — only the positions have switched. Mrs. Truong then bore a male body: a famous landlord. Rich, but of the 'rich yet heartless' kind — presuming on wealth, he despised others.}
{align:justify|At the time their house had a boy servant of about twelve. His parents, owing debts they could never repay, had no choice but to send their son to the house as a bond-servant. The couple, annoyed with the boy's parents, used him to vent their anger — often beating and cursing him, treating him terribly...}
{align:justify|One day the boy accidentally smashed a precious vase, enraging the rich couple; they beat him, bound his hands and feet, and dumped him in the fields.}
{align:justify|Luckily a kind passerby arrived in time to save the boy while he still breathed faintly, preserving his life. From then on the boy nursed seeds of hatred, swearing: 'If ever the chance comes, I will surely take revenge and reclaim the blood debt'..."}
{align:justify|In this life, conditions gathered: the boy once scorned and brutally humiliated has now been reborn as the Truong couple's darling son. He has come to collect a debt! Before long, this husband and wife will be angered to death by him"...}
{align:justify|Understanding these karmic twists, Mrs. Truong burst into heartrending sobs; her husband beside her hastily comforted her:}
{align:justify|"Don't cry, dear, don't cry! No wonder Cuong, from age twelve on, began failing at school, gaming obsessively online, even beating his father and cursing his mother... So in the past we created evil, treating him heartlessly. Then what should we do now?"}
{align:justify|"The best way: starting now, you two must become vegetarian, sincerely repent before the Buddha the evil deeds of old, and kneel to recite 49 'Ksitigarbha Sutras' dedicated to Cuong — then perhaps he can forgive you, the binding karmic net can be severed, and all will benefit..."}
{align:justify|"Yes — once home we will practice at once."}
{align:justify|Three months later, Mrs. Truong telephoned, saying with feeling:}
{align:justify|"Thank you beyond measure — the Buddha Dharma is truly miraculous! After we became vegetarian, repented, recited and dedicated for Cuong... now he has started school again, treats us obediently, and has changed so much — some days he even cooks for us himself... Truly, dissolving fateful hatreds works best and most effectively only through the Buddha Dharma!..."}
{align:justify|Further explanation: Stories spelling out evil ties between parents and children are told to warn everyone. Most often lament: "How hard it is to raise children"... (For in past lives most of them were victims, coming in this life as children to take revenge)... As parents, deeply believe in cause and effect — do not blindly beat your children, but repent what you did and created in past lives (even since beginningless eons). For your children, become vegetarian, recite and dedicate sutras... praying for the Buddhas' embracing protection to dissolve... turning evil ties between parents and children into wholesome ones.}
{align:justify|Next, teach your children to honor and support their parents and fulfill the human way. In time, children will naturally change greatly for the better.}
{align:justify|To turn children good, parents must first reform themselves — they themselves must cut off evil and cultivate good, draw near the Buddha Dharma, recite and dedicate blessings to their children...}
{align:justify|Only thus will the Buddhas' empowering support be drawn and merit multiply. Then, though an unfilial child lives at your side, if blessed conditions let you draw near the Buddha Dharma, that unfilial child is the golden key helping parents open happiness's door (thanks to Buddhist study and practice... benefiting both sides) — the whole family sharing the Dharma's joy, turning affliction into Bodhi.}
{align:justify|As for children — meeting unkind parents, understand the causes are just the same...}

{align:justify|Layman Tinh Hang grew up in a poor family, tasting every hardship. Grown, with life as he wished, he used it to create much evil karma, and life relapsed into every misfortune... Not until he met the Buddha Dharma, heard sutras and practiced, strictly kept the precepts, cut off evil and cultivated good, did he find happiness — remaking his life, living a second time...}
{align:justify|Let us hear him pour out words sincere from the bottom of his heart:}
{align:justify|I was born in a remote small town of Yangzhou, Jiangsu province. As a child my family was rather poor. From my earliest memories, I recall my father was a commune cadre — free-spirited, quick-tongued, blunt, with many drinking buddies, so guests were always coming. My father was a hard-drinking cadre; when I entered middle school, owing to internal office conflicts, he was expelled and stripped of office. This affected and changed our whole family's fate.}
{align:justify|His official career ruined, my father sighed day and night, as though become another man entirely, and my mother became his outlet... To win back lost face, ignoring my mother's pleading advice, my father borrowed from relatives and opened a coal kiln. Before long the kiln failed and closed. My father not only earned not a cent but plunged our family into utter destitution. For the whole family had to shoulder an enormous debt (fifteen thousand yuan) — in (1981) those days a terrifyingly huge figure.}
{align:justify|Our house simply could not repay; my mother grew ever more haggard and sad watching her husband's business fail, facing the family's abject poverty, with no hope of ever rising... In despair, several times my mother wanted to kill herself to end the painful life... But looking back at her children, still so young, she swallowed tears and forced herself to live, unable to abandon us...}
{align:justify|Then the four of us — mother and children — held one another weeping, knowing no road to take, no way to live. Whenever spring approached, every house bustled preparing for the New Year — only ours stood desolate, doors shut tight, while outside creditors shouted threats and the family suffered insults of every kind... ever deepening our taste of the world's cold human ways... My father, too, turned silent and brooding, grieving without end.}
{align:justify|Then my kind mother led my second brother and me from house to house begging understanding, weeping as she said: "The money we owe you we will surely repay — I stake my life as guarantee." In the cold wind, my mother's face streamed with tears, while my brother and I ached with choked pain. I was only seventeen then. The whole family's life rested upon my mother's frail, thin shoulders.}
{align:justify|Later the nation reformed and opened its policies; I too joined the workforce, and our whole family united in hard labor — at last repaying all debts with a little to spare. Life began to rise; I became a cadre in an office — and the dissolute life began...}
{align:justify|For I thought: "Poor as a child, now life has improved — time to enjoy and play." So I threw myself into feasting and debauchery, drinking and carousing daily till dead drunk, picking up every vice: smoking, drinking, gambling, killing, lust... I even ate creatures alive — no evil left undone, countless depravities...}
{align:justify|And so my official path turned rough (from debauchery and heaps of evil karma) — not only was I demoted, but at home all was strife, husband and wife quarreling noisily, the children's studies collapsing utterly.}
{align:justify|Stripped of office, I moved to Wuwei and opened a small shop to make a living. Sun rising, sun setting, day after day, my life stayed as before, nothing improving... I began sighing, recognizing my state exactly like my father's of old.}
{align:justify|One day, downstairs, we chanced upon a layman (now my senior fellow) who told me Buddhist stories and gifted me "The Merciful Boat of the Sea of Awakening" and a "Sutra Spoken by the Buddha on Immeasurable Life." The moment I got home I eagerly opened them; the two books seized my whole attention like "magnets drawing iron" — I read straight through, and by then dawn had come.}
{align:justify|From then on I loved Buddhist tales, never letting them leave my hands... striving to practice accordingly. I silently vowed: "The Buddha Dharma is hard to hear, yet I have heard it — in this life I vow to reform until birth and death are ended." From then on I followed my senior fellow into Buddhist study.}
{align:justify|Seeing me improve day by day, my wife and children admired and gladly joined the "ranks" of practice — our house luckily became a Buddhist family. My wife and I vowed together:}
{align:justify|"From now on we give up meat forever — lifelong vegetarianism — and I further resolved to quit smoking..."}
{align:justify|Strangely, before I had quit and relapsed many times, craving all the more with each attempt. But this time I could quit with a remarkably light, relaxed heart, thoroughly and decisively... This filled me with joy and feeling: truly the compassionate Buddhas and Bodhisattvas abandon no sentient being! Now, daily I persevere in reciting sutras, bowing to the Buddha, reciting the Buddha's name, copying sutras...}
{align:justify|Each lunar first and fifteenth I guide my family to release life and do many good deeds. At the same time I measure each of my deeds against the Buddha's sutras, examining myself.}
{align:justify|Well aware of my heavy habitual nature, many times before the Buddha I shed tears, sincerely repenting, urgently praying the compassionate Buddhas and Bodhisattvas bless me with added strength to reform successfully, helping me always spot faults in time and resolutely quit, never repeating...}
{align:justify|Since studying Buddhism, through years of precept-keeping and good deeds, my fate has turned for the better — many clients, hearing my name, admire and seek me for business, cooperating to this day. Now I have even opened a branch; business thrives.}
{align:justify|Since becoming vegetarian, my blood pressure runs no longer high, insomnia is gone. The mild facial paralysis has gradually healed. The whole family's health has greatly improved — even my aged parents far away in Yangzhou are hale. Since studying Buddhism, ever more people around me love and respect me.}
{align:justify|Each passing day my heart is very much at ease, working all day without fatigue. Life stays well-regulated: early to bed, early to rise. Daily, happiest is standing before the Buddha shrine, reciting sutras and bowing — Dharma-joy brimming... The Buddha Dharma truly grows more delightful the deeper one understands.}
{align:justify|My son has changed enormously too — once introverted and withdrawn, now he diligently recites the "Ksitigarbha Sutra," never missing a day. Cheerful, open, forgiving, our work too "succeeds as wished." My wife cultivates diligently as well; our whole house is bathed in the Buddha Dharma, living most harmoniously and joyfully, sharing happiness and ease.}
{align:justify|The Buddha Dharma is truly wondrous beyond conceiving — a treasure creating miraculous happiness on earth. I take my own experience as proof to share and tell you: avoid evil, practice good, constantly examine faults to "wholesomize" body and mind, carefully guard body, speech and mind in every deed, vow to strictly keep the precepts... never wantonly indulge and fall into evil's net and evil karma...}
{align:justify|I hope my true story encourages and greatly helps those with affinity. May all friends, in these remaining days of life, resolve to diligently study Buddhism and practice — saving self and others. May all escape affliction and pain, cross the great sea of birth and death, and reach the pure shore of awakening...}
{align:justify|- Tinh Hang -}
{align:justify|Buddhism grants us a beautiful education, helping humanity transform its own destiny most effectively. If everyone can practice according to the Dharma, each will find happiness in this human life.}

{align:justify|My Dinh is 30 this year, with neither work nor life going smoothly. Years ago, greedy for high profits, she quit a fine job she had, poured in all her savings (plus borrowing another hundred thousand) into a private trading company. Little did she know it was a scam: at first they used bait tactics (letting investors taste several thousand in sweet profits up front), then directors and staff alike absconded with the money and vanished. So My Dinh not only lost all her savings but fell deep in debt. Besides, five years ago she dated a boyfriend who deceived her so badly she had to abort; their love collapsed and ended in parting.}
{align:justify|To this day My Dinh has found no suitable lover; relatives' and friends' warm introductions all come to nothing. One day My Dinh's mother My Linh suddenly came to me, weeping as she told:}
{align:justify|"Qua Hong, I know you are writing 'Karmic Retribution in the Present Life' — please put my story in! My offenses are grave. My daughter's work goes poorly, her marriage unfulfilled, meeting every obstacle and misfortune... all my fault! Because this mother did so many, far too many bad things... my daughter is dragged down! Boo hoo!"}
{align:justify|I hastily handed her tissues to dry her tears. My Linh, grieving beyond the will to live, began confessing and repenting the unwholesome faults she had created in the past:}
{align:justify|"Beautiful My Linh, pampered since childhood, cradled by her parents like a thin-shelled egg. The family rich and grand, she got whatever she wished. A luxurious life of always-first privilege nurtured bad habits in My Linh. At school she disrespected teachers, yet was idolized in class as 'queen' — even teachers yielded to her two or three parts.}
{align:justify|After graduation and work, all went smoothly; though she worked far away at a mining enterprise in the great Northwest, the women cadres there endured no hardship — My Linh lived utterly free and easy, always enjoying every priority from schooling to work (when a research institute or company hired only two out of ten thousand competing applicants, My Linh was always hired first).}
{align:justify|Before forty, one may call it My Linh's golden age (her luckiest, most fulfilled years) — yet these were only blessings she enjoyed from past-life accumulation... But enjoying blessings is precisely consuming blessings, for My Linh knew no Buddha Dharma, understood no Way — so amid comfort she grew ever more deluded, creating skyfuls of evil.}
{align:justify|At My Linh's enterprise there were a great many men. With her lovely beauty drawing hosts of ardent suitors, she stood out among them like the radiant moon amid a thousand stars, dimming every other girl...}
{align:justify|At first she was a pure, simple girl — but with looks, power and money, plus swelling inner lust, My Linh could not resist money's and fame's allure, and she went astray, ever more wrongly. To gain her ends, she would readily sleep with male leaders — winning both the post she wanted and higher income.}
{align:justify|Through this dissolute life she aborted eight times. These very wrongdoings and deviant intentions swiftly drained her blessings dry, and evil retribution seized its chance to ripen in the present:}
{align:justify|First her family fell into discord; married, husband and wife quarreled and brawled without cease. Her husband, constantly swallowing rage, vented all frustration upon their daughter (My Dinh). So from childhood My Dinh was often beaten by her father, never knowing his care or love; and with a dissolute mother for bad example, grown-up My Dinh dated carelessly, was sweet-talked and deceived into losing her virtue and aborting.}
{align:justify|As for My Linh, evil retribution began ripening too: she contracted many illnesses — appendicitis operated on four times yet never cured, with grave aftereffects causing unbearable pain. Her legs developed bone spurs; one careless step meant stabbing pain. Gynecological illness weighed heavier still — each cycle racked her into fainting.}
{align:justify|What is more, work no longer goes smoothly: laden with every illness, leaders began avoiding My Linh; tormenting sickness forced her to stop working, then lingering illness kept her home resting, unable to work at all.}
{align:justify|These retributions are only flower-retributions; in future she must still fall into the three evil paths and suffer boundless pain — exactly as the 'Ksitigarbha Sutra' describes: 'If any man or woman practices no good but does evil — even disbelieving cause and effect, committing sexual misconduct, lying, divisive and harsh speech, slandering Buddhist sutras — such karma-makers must surely fall into evil destinies'..."}
{align:justify|My Linh wept bitterly as she repented and told her faults...}
{align:justify|Now, having studied Buddhism, she knows all that pain is self-made, self-borne. Plant evil causes, reap evil fruits. Karmic retribution follows like a shadow...}
{align:justify|Now daily before the Buddha My Linh sincerely repents her foolish, deluded offenses, the many evils created from craving gain and lust. Each day My Linh kneels to recite the 'Ksitigarbha Sutra,' diligently repents, praying for the Buddhas' empowering support to soon turn her sorry state. She also wishes to use her own experience to warn others.}
{align:justify|'A human body is hard to obtain, the Buddha Dharma hardest to meet — treasure them, diligently study Buddhism, actively practice good; for once blessings run out, calamity surely comes'..."}
{align:justify|I told My Linh:}
{align:justify|"'Causes and effects each maker bears — what I do, no other can shoulder'... That parents' deeds can shape their children's prospects... or as people say: ancestors store virtue, leaving blessings for descendants; ancestors do evil, bequeathing calamity to posterity'... Explained in Buddhism, this is 'shared karma draws together' — meaning those young ones with 'similarly created karma'... are reborn to repay together, exactly as the saying goes: 'like tones resonate, like qi seeks like!'..."}
{align:justify|Therefore parents must model themselves, practice good and store blessings — above, honoring elders with filiality; below, raising children well — never forgetting the teachings of Buddhas, Bodhisattvas and eminent monks; in life living uprightly: honest, faithful, vowing, practicing. Only then can families prosper and children flourish..."}
{align:justify|At this My Linh vowed:}
{align:justify|"In this life I will surely practice diligently, benefit sentient beings, and cultivate to end birth and death, leave the three realms, be reborn in Ultimate Bliss, and accomplish Buddhahood." Two months later I heard My Dinh had found stable work, My Linh's bodily illnesses had improved and no longer needed medicine. The family too grew gradually harmonious. I believe that under the Buddhas' empowering support plus the resolve to reform and renew, My Linh and her whole family will surely grow ever more wholesome and happy.}

{align:justify|Only amid adversity do people profoundly taste the pain of "birth, aging, sickness, death" and thirst to escape, longing to leave suffering for joy. Thanks to this, many enter Buddhist study. So those trials and grindings (including relatives and kin) are in fact our great wise teachers — we should meet them with grateful, sincere hearts.}
{align:justify|Tieu Hong has a kind mother who cares for her with exceptional, meticulous devotion... (yet owing to killing-karma obstacles), to this day the mother hovers halfway between believing in the Buddha and not.}
{align:justify|But at times the honey of binding attachment brings many troubles... Tieu Hong's mother often wishes her to be "like a little bird forever nestling against her"... though mother and daughter live together, if her daughter comes home just a little late, Tieu Hong's mobile phone rings nonstop, her mother's voice pouring out reminders urging her to "come home early as usual" — even when Tieu Hong is at work, her mother still calls to check; even when she reads at home, her mother watches and asks: "What are you reading now?"}
{align:justify|Whatever she wears, eats or does... her mother always inspects minutely. Tieu Hong knows her mother loves her and is very good to her, and filial feeling rises in her heart. But Tieu Hong is 32 this year, and such "clutching, clinging" love from her mother leaves her pressured, unfree, past bearing...}
{align:justify|One evening, Tieu Hong met with several fellow practitioners and planned to return a little late, having told her mother beforehand. But her mother still fretted, calling again and again to interrogate:}
{align:justify|"Why aren't you home so late? What meeting is it anyway? Where? With whom?..."}
{align:justify|Tieu Hong patiently answered; in the end, before the meeting finished she had to arrange to leave — yet her mother stayed displeased, calling endlessly. Tieu Hong had to endure and soothe... Only thus did the agitation in her mother's heart subside...}
{align:justify|Tieu Hong asked me in great puzzlement:}
{align:justify|"Why does my mother care for me so excessively?"}
{align:justify|I answered:}
{align:justify|"Conditions arise, conditions cease — all have causes and effects. This mother's gravely clinging love for her child springs from a distant cause in ancient India:}
{align:justify|"In those days, in southern India, there dwelt a noble prince. That day, as always, the prince rode a white horse, holding a golden cage in which a most lovely little bird was kept; the bird's board and lodging were supremely fine, tasty food daily, the cage even decked with lovely fresh flowers — yet the bird forever missed the outside world, thirsting to spread free wings... But its master doted too much and decreed its fate: 'for life it must stay caged.'"}
{align:justify|That ancient bird is Tieu Hong's mother today. And Tieu Hong herself was that prince of old."}
{align:justify|Because in that past life she acted with binding attachment, in this life she receives exactly the same retribution — watched, tended and clutched by her extremely caring mother, though past thirty yet still unfree. The white horse is Tieu Hong's father. As Tieu Hong confirms: as a child her father always gladly let her ride piggyback on his back and belly, feeling most happy...}
{align:justify|Having heard my explanation of the roots, Tieu Hong understood clearly: honoring parents means — besides caring for their livelihood and complying (without betraying the law of cause and effect) — guiding parents to the Buddha Dharma, deep faith in cause and effect, right faith in the Way, cutting off evil and practicing good, entering the liberating Buddhist path. That is true great filiality.}
{align:justify|Further explanation: In the Northern Song, Ouyang Xiu wrote a poem 'The Hwamei Bird': A hundred thousand trilling notes. Now low, now soaring past a thousand hills and streams. How mournful its voice inside the cage. Never as joyful as roaming high and free. Why does the caged bird's song sound less sweet than in the forest trees? Because the bird has lost freedom, so its song is gloomy, joyless.}
{align:justify|”Such causes bring such fruits”: the little bird of past lives has now not only been born human in a woman's body but has become the mother of the prince who once lovingly doted on it, forever caging it. A mother's love for her child is only natural — yet a daughter past thirty is bound by that attachment without a shred of freedom.}
{align:justify|Craving love is the root of birth and death: at first the prince gave rise to craving love and used it to bind, never permitting the little bird freedom... and so in this life, the little bird likewise uses craving love, possessiveness... to treat the prince exactly the same.}
{align:justify|In the Sutra in Forty-Two Sections, the Buddha teaches:}
{align:justify|From love arises worry,}
{align:justify|From love arises fear,}
{align:justify|If parted from craving love,}
{align:justify|What worry, what fear...}
{align:justify|The Sutra of Perfect Enlightenment says: "Know that love is the root of samsaric birth and death."}
{align:justify|We see how, life after life, beings bind one another without cease — all for craving love and attachment. Practice is like walking on thin ice: carefully uphold the precepts, stay far from craving love and clinging — only then liberation.}

{align:justify|Through boundless eons we have transmigrated through the six paths,forming hatreds with beings through the three poisons. The "Liang Huang Repentance" says: "All sentient beings have hostile adversaries. Why so? Because without hostile opposition there would be no evil destinies. Now that evil destinies never cease and the three evil paths only grow, know that 'hostile opposition... never ends.'"}
{align:justify|The sutra says: "All sentient beings possess mind; this mind can become Buddha — yet beings sink deluded in birth and death, unable to awaken, only from craving the world, refusing to escape, thus founding the root of pain and nursing hatred — and so they cycle through the three realms, traveling the six paths, shedding bodies and taking bodies without rest.}
{align:justify|Why so? All sentient beings, since beginninglessness, continuously discriminate in delusion; ignorance ever rising, drowned in the sea of love, giving rise to upside-down thoughts, practicing the ten evil deeds — practicing the ten evils themselves (teaching others to practice evil, praising evil dharmas, praising evil-doers). Thus with body, speech and mind, in a single thought-moment, eighty-four thousand gates of defilement open wide; the offenses of a day, a month, a year, over many eons... total into boundless, endless offenses. And so foes seek revenge on one another without end. To dissolve hatred, one must deeply believe in cause and effect, repent karmic obstacles, and broadly cultivate merit. Below is a true story of fateful ties between mother-in-law and daughter-in-law:}
{align:justify|Truong Lan and her mother-in-law got along fairly well at first. Then she married in and lived under the same roof. After a year, her mother-in-law often scolded her over trifles. Her husband, ever filial, always kept silent when his mother flared — leaving Truong Lan heartsick and gloomy.}
{align:justify|Retired at home, her mother-in-law shopped and cooked — that is, she managed every household affair single-handedly. These past days her health has been poor, so she ordered Truong Lan to cook. Truong Lan obediently complied.}
{align:justify|Daily she rose early and slept late: cooking, kitchen work... completing housework well. Today, Truong Lan happened to oversleep a little, and her mother-in-law had already finished breakfast. Before Truong Lan could open her mouth to explain, the old woman lashed out:}
{align:justify|"The toilet bowl must be scrubbed every day — one day unscrubbed and it's filthy and stinking! Such simple cooking, yet you work in fits and starts — what will you ever amount to, huh?"}
{align:justify|Her father-in-law stepped forward to mediate, which only further provoked his wife into a rage. Before the old woman's furious tirade, Truong Lan dared not utter a word. She swallowed the injustice — yet she too had to go out to work, most strenuous. Normally she treated both parents-in-law with utter deference, minding her manners in everything, striving to fulfill filial duty. Little did she expect that oversleeping once would draw such unreasonable, raging abuse...}
{align:justify|Truong Lan ate while wiping tears... finished the dishes, apologized to her mother-in-law, then went out to confide in a fellow practitioner, weeping out the injustice in her heart...}
{align:justify|Hearing Truong Lan's tearful account, I said:}
{align:justify|"Oh dear, stop crying — what happened to you today comes from past-life friction and misunderstanding between you and your mother-in-law,forming today's hard-to-untie enmity!"}
{align:justify|"In the Tang dynasty, Truong Lan was an imperial concubine deeply favored by the emperor. Once, while the concubine and emperor enjoyed song and dance, a general — defeated on campaign (and slandered by villains) — was summoned to audience by the emperor.}
{align:justify|Just then the concubine beside him whispered a few words into the emperor's ear; no one below heard clearly — but the general, seeing this, grew most displeased, misunderstanding that the concubine was badmouthing and secretly harming him!}
{align:justify|In fact the concubine only begged His Majesty to pardon him — but the emperor had already decided: the general must be punished as a warning to all! And so the general was jailed and stripped of rank. Not only did he suffer cruel torture, but a dozen-odd family members suffered along with him.}
{align:justify|And so his heart's hatred poured wholly onto the concubine; before and after he always believed she had whispered behind his back to ruin him. 'And the general's misunderstandings, the concubine's wrongs'... all sprang from a still remoter past — that is, many lives earlier the concubine had likewise wrongly suspected and misunderstood the general... And so tit-for-tat karma requiting wrong with wrong without cease, until in this life they meet and live together..."}
{align:justify|That ancient general is none other than Truong Lan's mother-in-law, and between mother-in-law and daughter-in-law the unloosened knot plays on"...}
{align:justify|Truong Lan recalled and told:}
{align:justify|"Ah — no wonder when a schoolmate once visited, the first meeting, my mother-in-law asked: 'Has Truong Lan badmouthed me?'..."}
{align:justify|Learning of this, Truong Lan felt most indignant. Afterward, her mother-in-law often suspected her of backbiting, worsening to the point of pointing in her face and cursing...}
{align:justify|Now Truong Lan understands it is all retribution — hidden evil ties of her own past making. And so she began reforming, turning to examine her own faults, repenting karmic obstacles, resolving to become vegetarian and recite 49 "Ksitigarbha Sutras" dedicated to her mother-in-law, hoping to dissolve the hatred between them. From then on she strictly kept the five precepts and cultivated diligently.}
{align:justify|Three months later, Truong Lan's bond with her mother-in-law had improved wonderfully. Today her parents-in-law love her like their own daughter. The whole family lives happily in harmony.}

{align:justify|"Ring! Ring! Ring!"... at midnight the telephone shrilled, a flustered voice sounding:}
{align:justify|"Qua Hong, I have an urgent question for you."}
{align:justify|"Aunt Trieu? Don't panic, take your time..."}
{align:justify|"My daughter went to hospital for tests today and learned she has malignant lung cancer, already spread everywhere. The doctor says there's no hope left. Please help us. My daughter is 33 this year — I truly don't know what to do!"}
{align:justify|"This girl so young — why such a monstrous illness?"...}
{align:justify|Before my eyes a vivid scene suddenly appeared, rooted in World War II:}
{align:justify|"In a German concentration camp, a crowd of naked Jewish people, group after group, were herded into dark, filthy rooms pumped full of poison gas; these wretched Jews, once inside, were soon choked by the gas and died en masse." The German officer driving the victims into the gas chambers is Aunt Trieu's daughter today.}
{align:justify|This is karmic retribution: in a past life she created evil karma, making pitiable beings' lungs breathe poison gas to a choking death... and so in this life the fruit ripened, giving her an incurable malignant lung cancer, threatened by death...}
{align:justify|I solemnly told Aunt Trieu the whole story and said:}
{align:justify|"To save your daughter, now only one way remains: she must before the Buddha greatly repent with a sincere heart, become vegetarian... Daily she must recite the 'Ksitigarbha Sutra' dedicated to the beings she harmed, praying they soon leave the bitter sea, be reborn in wholesome realms, and leave suffering for joy."}
{align:justify|Besides, your daughter must strictly keep the precepts, persevere in good, devote herself to giving, life releases, sutra printing... creating every merit to dedicate to them. Remind your daughter: 'Only patient, enduring practice offers a chance to live!'."}

{align:justify|One day, layman Lo and Tieu Man finished a vegetarian meal and stepped out of the shop when suddenly, not far off, they saw a whitish lump in a puddle, its shape unclear. They stepped closer to look... and jumped with fright. It turned out to be a pigeon's corpse — but so tragically dead it raised goosebumps. Apart from a bit of backbone and its intact wings, nothing remained at all. The two laypeople's hearts ached with pity; they wrapped the body in paper, recited the rebirth dharani, and buried it in a nearby flowerbed. Later at a Dharma assembly, layman Lo chanced to mention this; everyone said:}
{align:justify|"This must be karmic retribution..."}
{align:justify|Right — I suddenly saw clearly: drawing such a tragic death came from evil causes it planted in past lives, during World War II:}
{align:justify|"There was a German soldier tasked with burying the murdered Jewish victims' bodies. Among them one corpse still breathed faintly, its two arms jutting out stiffly; the soldier, annoyed at the sight, devised a cruel means: pouring gasoline on kindling to set the body ablaze — in no time the corpse burned away, leaving only two arms and the skull bones, a gruesome sight.}
{align:justify|Because that German soldier then planted evil causes, after death he fell into evil destinies; in this life he was born a pigeon. While foraging for food, a wildcat seized and ate him, leaving only the wings. This wildcat is the very Jew whose corpse was burned in the past."}
{align:justify|Cause and effect is real and never lies. Hereby I sincerely urge readers never to create killing karma; keep kind hearts, pity suffering beings, soon become vegetarian and study Buddhism, to swiftly leave suffering for joy.}
{align:justify|Further explanation: Heaven's net is vast — loose, yet hard to escape. Good and evil are repaid in the end. Do no evil because it is small; neglect no good because it is small.}
{align:justify|If in this world many believed in the Buddha, studied Buddhism, and practiced according to the teachings, humanity would suffer far fewer calamities and pains.}

{align:justify|In December 2004, the Indian Ocean was struck by an earthquake giving rise to an enormous tsunami; eight Southeast Asian nations counted the dead and missing... some two hundred thousand, over ten million homeless, grievous losses...}
{align:justify|Watching television, seeing corpses piled on the beaches, families in hopeless grief — a scene of "nation ruined, homes lost" — wrung my heart beyond words. Beholding this, unable to hold back sorrow, I vowed to recite the "Diamond Sutra" dedicated to those who died in the tsunami. I solemnly recited one "Diamond Sutra," and when I reached the dedication:}
{align:justify|"May the merit of reciting this 'Diamond Sutra' be dedicated to all who suffered in the Indian Ocean tsunami — may you hear the Buddha Dharma, give rise to great repentance, repent the evil karma created in the past, and soon be reborn in wholesome destinies, leaving suffering for joy."}
{align:justify|Suddenly, about two meters left of my shoulder, a crowd appeared standing in a long, orderly line, then one by one stepped forward (passing from left to right), reverently bowing before the "Diamond Sutra" volume, then vanishing. This lasted several minutes (at the time I thought it might be hallucination).}
{align:justify|I thought to myself: "If that line bowing to the 'Diamond Sutra' were indeed those lost in the tsunami, please appear once more." The moment this thought formed, outside the windowpanes (left side of the room) there clearly appeared hands of every kind and shape — fingers wrinkled as though long soaked in water, fingernails plainly visible, drop after drop of water falling in turn from between the fingers — as though they had risen from the sea and were clawing at the glass.}
{align:justify|It happened so suddenly, in the dead of night, that my heart felt a touch of fear. Yet that night the sky was perfectly clear, no rain — yet on the windowpanes appeared those dripping-wet hands, beyond all my expectation. This convinced me that in the sea a great many must have benefited from hearing the sutra Dharma. So at once I held right mindfulness, single-mindedly recited the sutra and chanted "Nam mô A Di Đà Phật!"... and all around gradually returned to stillness as before.}
{align:justify|Afterward I reflected: "The departed appearing thus also tell us: they truly exist and truly need the Buddha Dharma's help." May this world hold many precept-keeping Buddhists, all hearts turned to good, all deeds self-restrained — no fighting, no greed, no seeking, no self-seeking, no lying... and natural and man-made disasters will greatly lessen.}
{align:justify|Further explanation: The "Ksitigarbha Sutra" teaches the way of deliverance: "Before the images of Buddhas and Bodhisattvas, sincerely recite this sutra, or invite others to recite... from three to seven times. Then kin in evil destinies, hearing the full number of recitations, will be liberated..."}
{align:justify|Reciting the "Diamond Sutra" to deliver the departed works just the same. The "Collected Writings on Responses to the Book of Rewards and Punishments" records: Qi Jiguang, a Ming-dynasty general, daily recited the "Diamond Sutra" — he once recited it to deliver a deceased subordinate. As a result, the subordinate was reborn in a wholesome realm and even thanked him in a dream. Today disasters strike the world unceasingly; the spirits of compatriots killed in calamities suffer and long for our deliverance...}
{align:justify|Fellow Buddhist practitioners, please compassionately recite and dedicate sutras for these sufferers, joining strength to lend them a hand: "May they soon escape the bitter sea." Cultivating with such a heart brings attainment — for that is the human heart, the wholesome heart, the Buddha heart.}

{align:justify|Today, on advertising posters, newspapers, magazines, websites, even phones... beautiful women are made to dress revealingly to sell products, never knowing this is a mistaken act harming self and others.}
{align:justify|Working daily, I often ride the subway. That day, waiting for the train, I saw a lingerie poster picturing a scantily clad foreign woman. A young man not yet twenty stood glued to the picture, eyes fixed — the poster's provocative dress stirring restless thoughts in his mind...}
{align:justify|This is truly worrying: boys who will one day be the nation's pillars, at school age, ought to absorb wholesome principles... needing training and forging in correct views of life and the world. If pollutant images like these are displayed everywhere, constantly striking their eyes, their minds are easily dragged onto mistaken paths, leading to acts harming self and others — a great pity...}
{align:justify|I looked again at that ad: the girl in the picture lovely and alluring — yet dressed so revealingly she provokes lust in beholders. Then what retribution will she face in future? The thought had barely formed when my mind lit up, and beneath that huge poster a screen suddenly appeared:}
{align:justify|"There is an iron bed, flames blazing beneath; this advertising girl lies upon it, her back scorched and burned... Beside her stand two tall figures in black holding sharp knives, slashing hard into her belly... and now intestines, excrement, urine, blood and pus gush out mingled..." Seeing this, I could not suppress horror, thinking: "Is this not the punishment 'men embrace copper pillars, women lie on iron beds'? Committing lustful, pornographic, arousing offenses means suffering retribution in hell!"}
{align:justify|At the same time, I sensed that this lingerie model, though now young and beautiful, already suffers many illnesses — including gonorrhea, a dangerous, hard-to-cure gynecological disease... Then a confirming sentence rang out in my mind: "This woman, having created lustful offenses, will surely fall into hell — and the photographer plus the crew designing and printing those arousing pictures will all receive dreadful retribution. They must wait until the very last person on earth — who, seeing those pictures, gives rise to lust and falls into evil destinies, then escapes the three evil paths, rises in cultivation until no lustful thought remains — only then can this girl and all connected with shooting and producing those pictures escape hell's suffering and leave the evil paths..." "Of ten thousand evils, lust stands first." Truly terrifying! Fortune and calamity have no door — people summon them. Good and evil retributions follow like shadows; know that karmic retribution is dreadful indeed.}
{align:justify|I warn people of the world: whether women or men, never dress revealingly for advertising self-display, flaunting the body, peddling sexy photos, acting in arousing films and so on... If you do not wish to create hellish causes, your dress and bearing must be noble, proper and dignified; do not act carelessly and loosely. Do not chase trends, craving a little praise while the retribution falls on you alone.}
{align:justify|If you love dressing revealingly to seduce the appreciative gazes of the opposite sex, even stirring them to lustful thoughts, the retribution this evil karma creates is dreadful! Sometimes one small carelessness, one moment's error and ignorance, becomes eternal regret. Please remember carefully and stay ever vigilant, lest you harm yourselves...}
{align:justify|Monastics especially must engrave the Buddha's words: "Stay far from arousing scenes! Eyes see not, ears hear not, mouth speaks no deviant words..."}
{align:justify|One who keeps the no-lust precept enjoys five merits, praised by the Buddhas past, present and future. The lust-free ever dwell in the Buddha's dignity, their bodies fragrant as the Buddha's.}
{align:justify|Monastics must keep eyes, ears, nose, tongue and mind pure... Eyes behold no lustful forms, ears hear no love-ditties, the tongue discusses no worldly matters and praises no lustful affairs; the mouth never sings of lustful love and contact-pleasures, never speaks words feeding the evil karma of deluded love. The mind dwells in no frenzied sensations. Intent stays ever pure, never thinking of or recalling lust, never practicing deviant conduct oneself.}
{align:justify|Studying the Buddha's conduct, ever dwelling in the Buddha-mind thus is dwelling in thusness — winning five merits, the body ever pure as the lotus, chaste and clean. Hence the no-lust precept is supremely pure and complete with merit and unsurpassed benefit... It is the cause of liberation — no praise could ever exhaust it. Lust is the heaviest binding fetter, the fundamental grave offense. The Shurangama Sutra says: "Unless the lustful mind is cut off, the dust cannot be escaped."}
{align:justify|As for laypeople, lay Buddhists should wear proper, loose-fitting clothes and never dress too revealingly. Especially toward precept-keeping monastics in cultivation, you must never disturb them. Buddhism always reminds us: "Better stir a thousand rivers than stir a practitioner's mind." Deliberately making a precept-keeper's mind stir with affliction means offending against their pure precepts — an enormous fault. Please understand clearly to guard yourselves (especially women visiting temples should dress modestly and solemnly).}

{align:justify|At first, reading this story, I found it truly hard to believe!... Later, chancing upon confessional sharings by other men and beauties (please see the next story: "A MODEL'S WORDS OF REPENTANCE" in Part 2), I began reconsidering and reflecting on what laywoman Qua Hong says.}
{align:justify|Qua Hong lives in mainland China, while those others — in Taiwan and elsewhere — never knew one another, yet what they describe resembles one another; the difference is they personally lived through those scenes rather than merely seeing them as Qua Hong did...}
{align:justify|And so, hesitant though I was, I translated this story. Readers are free to believe or not and to choose the way of life that benefits them. Once, reading the papers, I saw a report: a gang of youths aged 15 to 17 gang-raped a little girl. Caught, the weeping children confessed it was all from watching phone sex films that stirred deviant thoughts. So harmful images truly cause boundless suffering!}
{align:justify|I mused: "If publicizing sex films and discs, arousing, depraved materials and so on... is so enormously sinful, then officials who collect, seize and destroy such things must likewise... earn boundless merit!"...}
{align:justify|Part 2}

{align:justify|I am a girl in her twenties; an abnormal childhood family gave me a corrupted character. As a child I envied children loved by their parents, with good friends. Though these are ordinary things everyone has, to me they were impossibly distant, forever out of reach.}
{align:justify|My parents are people of money and social standing, yet they never cared for me. As a child I was often criticized and mocked. I never felt I did anything wrong — only that I studied poorly (despite trying), my nature being weak. From my earliest memories I have felt only unjust treatment!}
{align:justify|There is something else: with parents paying no heed, in childhood, visiting a friend's house once, I was raped. This pain left an indelible mark on me. Growing older, I was often harassed. From sixth to ninth grade, classmates mocked me, boys teased and pestered me. I was then a naive, honest schoolgirl, weak-natured, dressed in unfashionable old clothes — yet somehow always teased? I cried constantly. For I utterly could not explain, never knowing what wrong I had done that the world treated me so. I only wanted to be an ordinary person!}
{align:justify|Tenth grade was truly my nightmare. Earlier mockery had all been small stuff. In high school I became the outlet for classmates' every hatred. I grew ever more dejected and timid. My hair was always tangled like a crow's nest. Clothes sloppy, face always tear-streaked. Classmates could even spit before me without restraint, loudly jeering and humiliating me to the utmost... Teachers not only ignored it but looked most delighted. I don't know how I endured it. (Even now, looking back, I admire myself — for living through such surroundings and rising from them.)}
{align:justify|At last I despaired completely and believed no more in "kindness" or "justice"! I concluded: this is a world where "the strong bully the weak!" — meaning "no matter how much you yield... they won't spare you"... Nursing nearly ten years of hatred and resentment, I rose up: from then on everything I did was double-crossing, betrayal, deceit... so long as I won fairness back for myself! I would gain the upper hand — be... stronger than the world!}
{align:justify|To me, "this world is too dark!" Entering university, my school was a place of unwholesome values (worshipping money above all); most people admired and welcomed girls of chaotic love lives, changing boyfriends like shirts. I dare say very few schoolgirls stayed chaste. Next to my school stretched a row of hotels and inns. Come Sundays or holidays they filled to bursting — a great many students from famous universities came prowling for fun (many other universities stood near mine).}
{align:justify|From here I began to molt, becoming someone else entirely. I always dressed in fashion, constantly changing styles and boyfriends. I neither liked nor loved any of them — some sickened me at a glance... In truth I only used them to take revenge on life; I wanted all men to serve me. At school I still consolidated my standing. I always thought: "I'm long since 'unclean' anyway — advancing by my looks may be my path!" I grew arrogant, proud of my beauty, and accepted invitations to model for modeling companies.}
{align:justify|Being a model, a beauty... countless fellows swarmed around, but I cheated them all for money or turned them into suppliers of what I needed — hence my great arrogance. What is more, I cheated my family of money: getting along badly with dormitory roommates, I wanted to rent outside, and on pretext of good grades cheated my family of nearly ten thousand. Though I defrauded my own kin, I felt not a shred of shame — thinking "they never cared for me, so this retribution serves them!" Though the dormitory girls were fierce-tempered, who told me to quarrel with them? Had I early learned to deal with people tactfully and gently, perhaps we'd never have quarreled so loudly.}
{align:justify|During this time I met an extremely kind young man who sacrificed and was good to me; though at school I already had many boyfriends providing for me, I still strung him along to cheat him of money. I always found reasons to squeeze him — food money, clothing money... always lamenting and playing poor to wring his pity and cash, while I had money of my own from many providers — yet I squeezed him further, only to salt it away against hard times. Thus he spent nearly ten thousand on me. He never truly knew me; so much I hid from him.}
{align:justify|But at last I could not bear my conscience's rebuke, so I confessed part of the truth. I said: "I stayed with you only to use you — never expecting you to be such an honest, kind man; you showed me the warmth of love! So I don't want to cheat you any more." When I behaved honestly and confessed all, the result was: he cut me off and left me utterly.}
{align:justify|The night before I was to shoot a lingerie ad for a company the next morning, I dreamed I went to hell — a gloomy, dreadful place, fires burning everywhere, people moaning and wailing all around. Some unknown guide led me on and on... touring around hell. My heart felt unbearably pressured and afraid.}
{align:justify|Though I woke most uneasy, I could not resist fame's and money's pull, and accepted the ad shoot (though the company was really "selling dog meat under a sheep's head") — arriving, I learned it was not lingerie as proposed, but nude photos.}
{align:justify|Only after a while did I learn: not only nude shots, but some sex-film makers, seizing loopholes in the law, took the chance to trade in sex. At first I refused to shoot anything excessive — but how could I outplay those experienced directors? Here there are always "unspoken rules": to advance, to stand out, you must "go to bed"... At first they pose as legitimate companies, then step by step lure the girls into depravity, until the girls become their money-making tools...}
{align:justify|The director promised I would earn a great deal each month. (Thinking back now, I deeply regret ruining my whole life for money.) I consoled myself: "Earn a pile, open a shop to live on, then quit this trade"... I found a great many in my trade thought the same — yet none ever got their wish. I switched many companies, always finding people untrustworthy; some companies constantly used the "unspoken rules."}
{align:justify|Of course I had to sleep with many people, many times — famed as a beauty, yet living wholly as a temporary mistress. At my school a great many girls served as married men's mistresses, and people rather respected them. So I lived likewise.}
{align:justify|Entering society, living and working, meeting endless fakery and fraud, I realized I was wrong: having done nothing good, I met only rotten, cheating scoundrels...}
{align:justify|Once I drowned in hatred and knew only money... so each step of my advancement walked toward the abyss. (I destroyed my life's ultimate happiness with my own hands.) Now, fallen and declining, I know no man can look at one such as me — unloved by parents, without close friends... Someone once loved me most sincerely, but I destroyed it all...}
{align:justify|I fear that if my arousing ad films and sex photos spread and cause a noisy scandal... I will never be hired again.}
{align:justify|Then my future is ruined entirely: fortune gone, self gone. But regret is useless! Before, I thought such photos won respect and stopped bullying — never knowing I was abandoning root for branches, trading my whole life for a few years of fleeting, illusory glory!}
{align:justify|Afterward I dreamed successive hell-related dreams — all during the time I shot ads and released sex films and photos.}
{align:justify|Only after working and facing a sordid reputation did I discover: "Being a good person, living contentedly within one's lot, is the happiest and most easeful — the heart always relaxed, fearing neither hardship nor lack"...}
{align:justify|Since filming, nude shoots, sex ads and many evil deeds, I have known not one joyful day, not one good night's sleep. My heart always weighs heavy as though pressed by stone, at times feeling torn and cut — aching, anguished, panicking, uneasy, my heart pounding in fright, nights unable to sleep.}
{align:justify|Like me now, my spirit under enormous pressure — though I earn much money, I spend it all. For unclean money comes fast and goes fast!}
{align:justify|For craving money and fame I went astray and ruined my whole life! Some may say: "As supermodels, some girls earn piles of money, lucky stars, much happiness"...}
{align:justify|Then let me tell you truly: this trade must sacrifice a great many beauties to "produce" one lucky-star model — and her "rosy fate" lasts none too long... Many girls may fancy themselves beautiful enough to model famously and live in every pleasure... Then let me explain: I have met models lovelier than stars; I know many film-school graduates — yet why is their star not red? Not for missing chances, nor poor networking, nor weak studies or temperament...}
{align:justify|Many are lovely as descended fairies, yet no matter how they strain every nerve and chance — studying acting, arts... (so many students adore this field). But once in show business, whatever they do, their star never turns red. Do you know why? Not bad luck, but destiny!}
{align:justify|My friend, those popular stars of fame and power — whatever they do, their star stays red; though they shoot, pose and act in sex or erotic films... destiny stays rosy! (But that is because in past lives they stored virtue, and so in this life win such chances and enjoy such blessings. Lovely looks alone never suffice.) Do not rely on beauty, pouring all your strength into it, imagining... one step to the clouds! (Countless abyssal traps wait, beyond your reckoning!)...}
{align:justify|Yet these "red-star" celebrities will destroy themselves too, if they agree to shoot sex photos or act in filthy erotic films (once they do, they have destroyed all their own blessings!)... One may also affirm: if they shot no sex, acted in no porn, indulged in no sexual license — their star would stay very red indeed, their fame enduring!}
{align:justify|Therefore I urge all beauties who dream of modeling, singing, acting: a thousand times, ten thousand times, never step into the sex ring — nude shoots, pornographic films — and above all never indulge sexually!... Never, ever trust in your own beauty. Even riding high, doing such things guarantees no "clean" retreat. Though you merely sing arousing songs or shoot sex photos... it still counts as "walking the road dragging everyone down into 'sinking in darkness'" — after death you will descend to hell to suffer and atone for who knows how many years... for I once dreamed hell's scenes, so I know this well.}
{align:justify|Moreover, however beautiful you are, never take any job involving shooting, acting, nude ads, or singing and dancing in bars to arouse lust...}
{align:justify|Human desire is often uncontrollable; just as you seek debut and advancement through (sex- and lust-arousing) photo and film work, another vicious, fallen circumstance may already lie in wait, leaving you no escape, no rescue. (Refuse your partners' demands and you will never get a chance to rise — so better, from the very start, stop and refuse to do it!)}
{align:justify|Moreover, I urge modeling dreamers: ten thousand times, never casually find companies online, or accept lingerie, sex or nude shoots... never trust those promising to crown you, make you famous, earn you fortunes... If you agree, "unspoken rules" will force you into men's beds — and things will be no better! Never believe everything comes as easily as you wish!}
{align:justify|Many may say: "I'm only a second-tier model, that's why I get no big break"... but do you truly know the hidden twists and heartbreaking perils of this trade?}
{align:justify|I was once a famous model, often walking fashion shows, with many chances to release albums and publish wonderful photo collections — yet this is a fame-road of tears you cannot fathom... In our China today a great many film graduates find work very hard. They hustle and run about for ages, begging countless connections, even sleeping with many directors... (of course, many rise from mud unstained — I don't say all performing arts are evil) — yet most such friends get only silent, nameless roles...}
{align:justify|My friend, without blessings, background or good connections... yet vying with others to enter this dark fame-and-profit arena... you are destroying everything in your hands, burying your own happiness. For even with background and connections... even a rising red-star celebrity — so what? Can you stay red forever? Will admirers chase you in old age? Shooting and acting in sex — will you not descend to hell? Before releasing such arousing films, at least you could still live well by your own effort, with no need to earn by making depraved films and discs. For the guilt (the harm those corrupt discs cause) falls on you — not to mention spiraling into chaos... Papers report beautiful models taken abroad and forced into prostitution and drugs (search online and you'll see it all)...}
{align:justify|I know modeling companies very well: basically all supply models for prostitution — of course beforehand they say nothing, only showing}
{align:justify|an outward face of staging "bodily beauty" or "artistic bodies" and so on... while inside, many models are secretly filmed in sex movies. When such photo albums prove popular, the shooters post them online to trade with demanding kingpins. China has no personal privacy law: once your "sizzling" pictures hit the internet, you'll never know who spread them — you have no idea how many pornographic websites China has, all formed from this very source.}
{align:justify|Moreover, if other directors secretly sell your pictures to porn sites, do you think they will tell you? There — wanting big money, you unwittingly send yourself to hell. For only when your pornographic films and photos vanish from this world can you exit hell. Does that mean waiting until the world ends (and the pictures perish) before you can leave hell?... Is such money worth it?}
{align:justify|Many who hear my account may disbelieve and pity me. But recalling in detail my past deeds, I understand it is all retribution.}
{align:justify|I grew up in humiliation — spurned by kin, isolated by friends, bullied and scorned — because I once planted these very causes. Let me tell you.}
{align:justify|I was once a famous model with power and money in hand; trusting in beauty, I wantonly hurt others and toyed with feelings. For I cheated the love of so many men, leaving them weeping in bitter pain — some driven mad by me...}
{align:justify|I chose advancement by looks, indulging sexually with many men — and now my looks have degraded; I age fast and ugly. From excessive lust, harming my spirit and primal force. Now I'm skin and bones like a stick — climbing one flight of stairs leaves me breathless; my memory is terrible, unable to study or do anything more...}
{align:justify|Once my skin glowed smooth and rosy — now wrinkled; friends seeing me say I look older than some old auntie!}
{align:justify|My skin now cracks and bleeds. Washing hands, walking... all hurt. In winter, washing my hands in cold water makes me want to cry — ceaseless cutting pain, like knives... Now, though I apply anti-crack cream, the wrinkles remain. I think my spiritual primal force is too gravely damaged. That I live at all is a miracle.}
{align:justify|Moreover, indulging sexually with many men has eroded my uterus; doctors once warned me to screen for uterine cancer. My lower belly often feels unwell. Once, just after taking birth-control pills, I felt cutting pain lasting all night, leaving me weeping endlessly.}
{align:justify|From chaotic promiscuity, once some male students tricked and kidnapped me to a remote place and gang-raped me in turn — though I had done them no wrong.}
{align:justify|Having tasted suffering to the dregs, with nothing left in hand, I always asked heaven, asked the Buddha and Bodhisattvas... longing to know my own causes and effects. Perhaps thanks to my sincere prayers, at the end of 2012 I dreamed a past-life dream:}
{align:justify|"I was a young man standing with an older woman (like my mistress) beneath a building. My mistress held an account book; together we collected debts. I looked clever, sly, cunning and capable... I served my mistress most pleasingly, always ready with words she loved to hear — though they harmed others to profit us!}
{align:justify|From inside a shabby, squalid room rang a girl's moaning curses: 'Though I die I'll never forgive you!' The words brimmed with hatred — sounding like ghosts from long-unrisen depths — chilling even hardened, experienced me to the marrow. This resentful aura seemed to take shape, bearing dark, gloomy black vapors, piercing and blanketing all space, exhaling sky-piercing hatred. Yet then, to flatter my mistress, I still put on a harsh, domineering face and said:}
{align:justify|'Who told you not to pay your debts...'"}
{align:justify|Roughly, this girl had not paid some room-rent, so my mistress and I came demanding it and punished her by heartlessly standing by, watching her gang-raped — while I coolly spat venomous, mocking words...}
{align:justify|Awake, the girl's cold, resentful aura still seemed to hang around me, her wretched, hate-filled moans still ringing in my ears. I suddenly understood why as a child I was raped, later gang-raped, and further fell into the dusty wind.}
{align:justify|Long had I guessed that in a past life I must have committed some offense to draw this life's tragic fate. But I never dared believe I had raped women in a past life — for however sinister and wolf-hearted, I would never do such things!}
{align:justify|Had I not been violated as a child, perhaps this life I might have lived normally like everyone else — perhaps never willingly fallen, never created hellish offenses...}
{align:justify|And so I conjectured: perhaps in a past life I ran a brothel, a pimp? Harmed many women, and so in this life must taste rape, living in humiliation? But the dream I just dreamed (perhaps thanks to my unceasing repentance, questioning blue heaven and the Bodhisattvas)... Then suddenly I understood clearly: all this life's humiliation comes from my past-life coldly standing by as that girl was violated, spitting venomous mockery — when plainly I could have saved her, yet did not! Not only did I fail to rescue the victim, I fawned over my mistress, speaking only harsh, selfish words to profit myself.}
{align:justify|And so in this life, when I was raped and told my kin, I met their cold indifference — never a word of pity, only blame: 'Why did you go sticking your head there?'..."}
{align:justify|Though I knew my parents could surely never weep with pity like normal parents, their words and kinfolk's cold treatment chilled me ten thousandfold.}
{align:justify|I always ask myself: just what did that past-life girl do to take revenge on me?...}
{align:justify|I too behaved badly, joining crowds to fawn, laughing at isolated friends. And I keenly taste the retribution of being mocked, isolated, scorned and shunned by those around me.}
{align:justify|I once mocked and betrayed friends — fearing isolated friends would drag me down, I joined the crowd in casting them out, even badmouthing them...}
{align:justify|In university I loved mocking people, even drawing serial "erotic satire" cartoons to jeer for fun... Yet what I drew, the victims never did — all my victims, fabricated... I liked giving people ugly nicknames and writing satirical poems mocking them, always showing off how clever, witty and talented I was...}
{align:justify|And so the retribution is: I know only false, faithless friends, always betrayed.}
{align:justify|As a child I killed a great deal, specializing in torturing small creatures into cripples, thinking it nothing. Though some insects my family ordered me to kill, still I did it — how can I escape karmic retribution?}
{align:justify|I loved eating live fish, flesh and fresh seafood — hardly able to be vegetarian! From ignorant senselessness I offended a great deal. Creatures dead by my hand beyond counting — how can I ever repay this debt of wrongs?...}
{align:justify|Perhaps drawn by karma, or my own past-life evil karma hard to reform — in foolish days, knowing no Buddha Dharma, I created sky-high evil. And so I often dream of countless insects, even dream of becoming the creatures I killed and ate, stewed and cooked... Without these nightmares terrorizing me to heart-shaking fear, how could I remember so clearly!}
{align:justify|I tell you my life story to prove: dissolute living, lust, evildoing and so on... destroy all one's own goodness, making everything go wrong, luck rotten, relations bad, meeting only wicked friends, family in constant strife, career never succeeding...}
{align:justify|A great many things I drew by my own fall — for I cheated a great many schoolboys of money, and so when I finished shooting ad albums, companies and clients cheated me in turn!}
{align:justify|Afterward I dared not speak of love — for one such as me can hardly meet good people. 'Wrong done, dare to bear the fruit.' And the fruit I must repay is this: I have lost all happiness of an ordinary human — no one can love me, and living is very hard. Because I earned unclean money, I destroyed my own blessings entirely. So everywhere I seek work, nothing goes as wished; now even being an ordinary person is hard.}
{align:justify|Now, seeing my friends live happily, I can only stand watching with streaming tears. I have no right to wish for anything. All comes from my towering dreams and fierce greed: craving power and position, I went so astray into retribution that now even being ordinary is denied me. Had I not lived badly at first, perhaps I would have known simple happiness...}
{align:justify|Now my health is ruined, memory much faded, looks turned ugly, prospects a blurry blank — always fearing my past evils exposed. Beside me no true friends, no loving kin, no one to pity me! Fallen this far, stripped of every finest thing — what could be more wretched? I constantly wish to end my filthy life — not only others, even I myself despise me!}
{align:justify|Ask yourself: living in this world with no one sincerely loving you, everyone around — knowing the truth about you — disgusted and scornful, then though you seize great successes, what meaning does life hold? I see that worldly ties are only four: owing debts, collecting debts, repaying kindness, requiting wrongs...}
{align:justify|Knowing my offenses deep and grave, I borrow this essay as repentance, hoping to lighten grave offenses, hoping the Buddhas, Bodhisattvas and all people will bless me, keeping me from hell.}
{align:justify|I destroyed myself with evil karma — may all take me as a mirror and never live as I did. Whatever the reason, ten thousand times over, never act in sex, never sell your image, body and soul! Hardships will pass — only practice good and destiny can change. Now, though each month I earn only a few hundred, even borrowing to live on, my heart is calm and bright, no longer rebuked by conscience. If you ask what still troubles me now, it is my past evils — all branded deep in mind, keeping me from ever holding my head up as a person! I hope reading my story wakes you to live wholesomely.}
{align:justify|One who repents}

{align:justify|In fifth grade, I lived near the Tamsui River in Taipei. We were three schoolmates — two friends, one surnamed Trinh, one surnamed Tran. After school we'd go to the Tamsui River to catch crabs; caught, we'd snap off their claws and legs for fun, gleefully... Playing thus, in the end we'd stone the crabs to death. That day the game ended; the three of us, done with mischief, headed home.}
{align:justify|My two friends walked on the asphalt road, while I stepped inside (on the grassy verge), hurrying along when suddenly I spotted a pretty strange rope ahead and rushed to pick it up... it turned out to be a SNAKE! Terrifying! I hurled the snake away in horror and jumped clear; the snake didn't bite but hurried off. Yet I flared with anger and picked up a stone to hurl at the snake...}
{align:justify|My friend surnamed Tran standing by reminded me:}
{align:justify|"Meeting a snake without killing it, tonight it'll come find you!"}
{align:justify|"Then you two help me kill it!"}
{align:justify|My friend said:}
{align:justify|"To kill a snake, aim at the seventh segment..."}
{align:justify|I took that for its heart... stoned the snake to pulp until dead, then swaggered home. You think that's the end? So did I — but it wasn't over!}
{align:justify|Friends, when our grandparents die, why must we worship them? Because they have future lives. You hope they are delivered, not made ghosts. We all believe the dead still have future lives.}
{align:justify|Friends, a rabbit, an ant, a snake — dead, do they have future lives? They do! Surely they do, one hundred percent! But in those days we knew none of this, so after I stoned the snake dead, I proudly, gleefully walked home.}
{align:justify|Half a year later, I entered sixth grade of middle school. I have two elder brothers, the eldest two years older, the second one year older. Once I quarreled with my eldest over a toy; I don't remember what insult I hurled, but he flew into a rage (we brothers had never quarreled or fought) — he threw me against the wall and beat me wildly, savagely punching my chest until his own hand split open, bone jutting out — only then did he come to and scream miserably at the sight of his own split bones. My whole body ached unbearably. That night home, under mother's questioning, neither dared tell the truth; both said "we tripped... and hit the wall." He went to hospital, I went to hospital...}
{align:justify|From that day, in my very first year of middle school the pain began — until what age? I tell you: I hurt until age thirty-five!}
{align:justify|All that time, the moment I flared with anger or resentment (including scoldings from my parents), pain struck at once. Each attack pained my brother to watch, guiltily... So he seldom spoke with me (feeling deeply at fault). Yet I never understood this pain's cause — not until a month before I left home did its source become clear...}
{align:justify|I have a friend with yin-yang eyes — seeing both realms of living and dead... he is no Buddhist, yet gifted like that. Others see gods; he sees ghosts — quite unique.}
{align:justify|Once, walking beside him, I told him I was about to leave home and said:}
{align:justify|"Later I won't hang out with you any more — my head's about to be shaved."}
{align:justify|My friend, psychic, seeing what fleshly eyes cannot... told me:}
{align:justify|"Goodness! You often ache in the heart, around the chest — badly, don't you?"}
{align:justify|"Yes, I do!"}
{align:justify|He said:}
{align:justify|"I see a vicious snake biting and tearing there. That is your pain's cause!"}
{align:justify|I said:}
{align:justify|"You're talking nonsense! What snake bites me? This is internal injury from my eldest brother beating me as a child!"}
{align:justify|He smiled and said:}
{align:justify|"This you don't know!"}
{align:justify|I thought: "Not everything he says is true?"... yet strangely, the moment he finished, my heart began to ache. I was most amazed. Normally some reason had to trigger my pain. But now, the instant he finished speaking, I felt pain!}
{align:justify|He asked:}
{align:justify|"In life, have you ever killed a snake?"}
{align:justify|"Yes, I have!" — I had barely answered when my heart stabbed with pain...}
{align:justify|He explained:}
{align:justify|"This is easy to understand. In fact, after you killed it, that snake fell into the ghost realm (still wearing a snake's form)."}
{align:justify|My chest at once ached horribly (hearing his explanation, at last I understood). That snake, groundlessly stoned dead by me, was at once reborn into the ghost realm. (Had it been reborn a snake, at most meeting me it would strike once; reborn a dog, meeting me it would bite —forging evil ties together.) But this snake, reborn into the ghost realm, could take revenge immediately. How did it take revenge?}
{align:justify|That is, in my first middle-school year, as I told — over some trifling nothing, my brother beat me black and blue (as my friend explained: the snake-ghost had lodged in my eldest brother's right hand) — for at first I myself had used my right hand to stone the snake dead; however many blows I struck, in revenge the snake borrowed (my eldest brother's right hand), poured "hatred-force" into it, and "served" me the beating back. It made my eldest punch my chest until his hand split (he himself never knowing), and so I suffered internal injury.}
{align:justify|Why I ached twenty-odd years — at last fully understood. Understanding, I at once knelt; my friend beside me also helped me arrange the snake's deliverance — to no effect. So I knelt again, contemplating the snake as before me, bowed my head to it, and spoke remorseful apology thus:}
{align:justify|"My friend, I never knew you suffered so, never knew that killing you made you suffer so; I too now ache with grief for my foolish childhood fault... that harmed you! At this moment, I bow my head in sincere apology before you; however many stones I threw at you in those days, so many prostrations I now offer..."}
{align:justify|I knocked my head until it swelled and bled. Having kowtowed my apology, I vowed:}
{align:justify|"From this day on I will never again kill any living being; from this day on I will never again flare with anger. (I was then about to leave home, so I vowed thus.)"}
{align:justify|At the time I knew only the Heart Sutra by heart, and dedicated it. Done, I stood up — and suddenly my whole chest and heart turned cool and easeful, pain gone, utterly comfortable... From that day to this, I have never suffered that torment again...}
{align:justify|Friends, to me: one fault seeming so trifling — who knew its impact so grave, racking my body with pain for decades, all for killing one snake. Is it dreadful? Most dreadful! So please be careful — never create killing karma.}
{align:justify|Let me tell one more story:}

{align:justify|I have a friend who, six months of every year, suffers breathlessness; hospitals, Chinese and Western cures all tried, none helped.}
{align:justify|Once, mid-conversation, he choked and gasped and asked me:}
{align:justify|"Is there any cure? This must be my karmic obstacle."}
{align:justify|I said: "If karmic obstacle, still its cause must be found. There is surely a cause. Think hard back: in this life have you killed any creatures, and how many?"}
{align:justify|He said:}
{align:justify|"Yes — back home in the countryside, rats swarmed. My parents trapped a great many. Knowing I loved drowning rats, they had me carry whole cagefuls to the river; I choked the rats breathless and drowned them all... and if any rat still lived, I refused to lift the cage... Perhaps that is why retribution strikes me thus!" My friend daily keeps choking and gasping... half-dead. Friends, do you find it dreadful?}
{align:justify|I tell this story not to frighten you, but to make you understand clearly: never kill, and never earn a living by killing trades. Please guard yourselves well!}
{align:justify|Related by Master Hai Tao}

{align:justify|From age thirty-three I began expounding sutras; now seventy-two, I have taught the Dharma for over forty years. Yet today many slander me, and I gladly accept it all. Why? Because in this very life, before knowing the Buddha Dharma, I myself created very grave speech karma!}
{align:justify|Before knowing the Way, I often slandered the Buddha Dharma, even brazenly declaring: "BUDDHISM IS SUPERSTITION!" At the time I believed only in Catholicism and Islam, having spent two years among Christians and one year among Muslims — my impressions of these two faiths were excellent; they had things I admired.}
{align:justify|As for Buddhism, I had never had the chance to encounter it nor ever heard anyone teach me the Buddha Dharma — so what did I know! A mere bystander, utterly blind about the Buddha Dharma, I scorned "Buddhism as superstition" — and having so classified it, of course I had to smash superstition... and so I created most grievous evil karma.}
{align:justify|In Buddhist terms, I offended among the five rebellious acts (shedding the Buddha's blood) — an offense consigning one to the Uninterrupted Hell. As a foolish, senseless child, during the anti-Japanese war years when schools were few, we studied inside temples (for the government confiscated temples for schools — main halls for assembly, side rooms for classrooms).}
{align:justify|So people gathered all the Buddha and Bodhisattva statues... into one room used as a storehouse, sealing the outside with paper — well meant enough. But would we children let it rest? Hearing of many strange statues inside, we longed to sneak in and play with them...}
{align:justify|So we broke the window, climbed in, and made toys of the Buddha statues — playing till bored, then tossing them away, smashing them all... Out of curiosity I even split open a wooden Buddha statue to see... what might be inside? Committing these offenses, we were then too young to know... Nor did teachers ever warn us... And frankly, had they advised, we might not have listened — for I so loved creating the karma of slandering the Buddha Dharma...}
{align:justify|Only after studying Buddhism did I understand: those faults meant falling into the Uninterrupted Hell. Today, having expounded sutras for over forty years, I am still slandered and insulted... I gladly receive slander's fruit... well deserved! Reflecting, I created grave offenses yet receive light retribution...}
{align:justify|Moreover, as a schoolboy I was most unruly and stubborn... Though teachers loved me dearly, disciplining me was hard. My memory was outstanding; I learned fast and retained long... Lessons I needed to read but once to memorize — so no need to strive at all; I coasted through school, studying-cum-playing, learning only to cope with exams...}
{align:justify|Ten minutes before an exam, one quick review guaranteed a pass. Besides, in exams I aimed for sixty points only — one point more, no thanks! I craved no fame or gain, yet always handed in my paper first. Teachers grading them shook their heads... seeing I could advance well, easily score far higher — but why wouldn't I?... Because I loved play. Studying just needed steady promotion each year — purpose served! Teachers and family demanded little, so pleasing them came easy.}
{align:justify|Like everyone, I liked doing what I pleased. I read a great deal, far beyond my schoolmates — hence my extremely rich knowledge. From third grade I began reading China's old novels, stopping in my first year of middle school — for nothing remained to read. Fine novels I had nearly all read four or five times — the four great classics: "Journey to the West, Romance of the Three Kingdoms, Water Margin, Dream of the Red Chamber"... each I read five or six times. Thick as they are, I devoured them fast — under a week per set. Classics exhausted, in sixth grade I read nothing more. I lived most freely — tales by day, operas by night. My school days were delightful; teachers and the principal all knew my name. For I always argued with them:}
{align:justify|"Schools today are merely places to hunt diplomas and degrees — yet most of what is learned proves useless. Even learning these things, how much use will I ever make of them?"}
{align:justify|Principal and teachers, hearing this, shook their heads, speechless. I tell old tales so you see my "record" — and how much karma I created. But I had one shred of good roots: I never harmed people, never hurt society — yet I harmed... animals!}
{align:justify|Young and ignorant of the Buddha Dharma, I loved eating meat, offending far more gravely than others. For during the anti-Japanese war I often hunted; after reading the Ksitigarbha Sutra, I feared — truly feared — and began lifelong vegetarianism, daring eat meat no more, knowing my created offenses most grave.}
{align:justify|As a child knowing nothing of sin or merit, my father loved hunting, so I often followed him up the mountains; daily we brought home shot game. I hunted three years — starting at sixteen until nineteen's end. So I shot superbly — no aiming needed, yet hundred-percent hits. Daily I spent at least twenty bullets; when not hunting, I practiced shooting...}
{align:justify|My father was a soldier; during the anti-Japanese war he managed weaponry (arms, guns, explosives...) — so our house held many guns, giving us our chances to create karma...}
{align:justify|Daily I practiced — what child dislikes playing with guns? Better still, playing with real ones. My crack shooting came from daily practice — hence "review the civil, drill the martial," no? Without drilling, nothing; drilling makes mastery! Through long training my shooting grew outstanding — and so the evil karma I made grew most grave, though then I still knew nothing. Later, studying Buddhism, reading the Ksitigarbha Sutra, my hair stood on end... For with my own eyes I watched my father die exactly as the "Ksitigarbha Sutra" warns! The "Ksitigarbha Sutra," upper scroll, records: "Meeting those who hunt and kill wantonly, He teaches: they will suffer the retribution of madness and loss of life"...}
{align:justify|In olden times people used bows, arrows or nets... killing few beasts; even net-fishing casts limited hauls... But in our day hunting, we used guns and explosives... acting most cruelly: after hurling explosives, thousands upon tens of thousands of dead fish floated belly-up — killed by blast and by shock... how grave do you call this offense? Beast-hunting kills still count fewer than dynamiting fish... We created far too much killing karma, boundless evil. Killing's retribution is short life — and so my father died at only forty-five.}
{align:justify|Reading that Buddhist passage recalled my father's retribution — exactly so. In his forty-fifth year, my father "went mad and lost his life" — I witnessed with my own eyes the retribution he repaid in this very life!}
{align:justify|When my father fell mad, though his body wasted to bone, his strength was immense — several men could not restrain him. Meeting water he plunged headlong in; meeting mountains he dashed headlong up — this is hunting's retribution.}
{align:justify|Finishing the "Ksitigarbha Sutra," I awakened and resolved to become vegetarian, knowing my killing offenses most grave — having witnessed it myself... feeling it profoundly... from then on I strove to cut off evil and cultivate good.}
{align:justify|Later, studying Buddhism, famous fortune-tellers all read my lifespan as not past forty-five — I fully believed. So I studied Buddhism all the more diligently, my lifespan being fixed at forty-five.}
{align:justify|Sure enough, at forty-five I fell gravely ill. I saw no doctor, took no medicine... thinking: "My life is ending — doctors can cure illness, not lifespan." So for a whole month I single-mindedly recited the Buddha's name, seeking rebirth in the Pure Land; after a month's recitation the illness vanished of itself.}
{align:justify|In truth, I only turned my resolve, vowing to dedicate what little wisdom, ability, body and mind I have wholly to the Buddhas and Bodhisattvas — to work for the Buddhas and Bodhisattvas, no longer living for myself. Buddhism often says "returning on the strength of vows" — one may say I turned karma into vows; once I "changed" my heart, the effect proved supreme...}
{align:justify|I am one of no blessings; money, too, I have little. To redeem past killing, whatever offerings I receive I spend on life releases, hospitals (medicine money for the poor), aiding the sick and suffering, and printing sutras to give away. Later, one year at the Renwang Dharma assembly in Taiwan, I met the "Living Buddha" Cam Chu (an old friend of mine); he looked most delighted and said:}
{align:justify|"These years your sutra-expounding merit is vast. You had no blessings and were fated for short life — yet now you not only have blessings but long life as well... all thanks to propagating the Dharma and benefiting beings, so good fortune manifests..."}
{align:justify|Truly I never sought long life, understanding: "Once awakened, live no longer for self — all for the Buddha Dharma, for sentient beings." Hence the response.}
{align:justify|For myself, I cling to nothing in this world. This body remaining... is beings' blessing — for when beings need me, when the Dharma needs me, I stay a few more days; when beings need me no more, when the Buddha Dharma needs me no more, I leave at once, clinging to nothing. This is my present state and attitude; I most joyfully look forward to soon departing to attend the Buddha.}
{align:justify|I tell of my karma-making and retribution so you see I too was bad, most evil. Had I never practiced Buddhism, I would surely suffer in hell. I have proven it to you. I — from one of many faults and heaps of evil karma — could reform and turn good... so can you. What matters is only whether you will.}
{align:justify|Related by Great Master Chin Kung}

{align:justify|Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva's merit is true and never false. Let me offer my own family's story as proof: last April my father had heart surgery. Afterward he partly recovered, but last winter his condition turned worrying, the heart illness gradually worsening — even turning over or dressing... the smallest tasks needed others' help; he also grew dizzy and blurry-eyed, his body gravely afflicted. Nights he slept little, often sleepless and plagued by nightmares, feeling his whole body aching.}
{align:justify|My wife and I live apart from my parents, so daily care of my father fell wholly on my mother; these past months she has grown much thinner. Wracked with pain, my father's spirits sank into extreme pessimism; he often spoke gloomy words that frightened the family. Hospital visits helped nothing — the doctor, at a loss, prescribed only tonics and said:}
{align:justify|"No better way — keep him going day by day!"}
{align:justify|Then our whole family despaired; my father even began instructing my mother about his funeral. Trained in medicine myself, witnessing my father's state, I knew medicine could no longer resolve it. So I thought of the Buddha Dharma. (Shamefully, thanks to having read many books by Master Nan Huaijin, I suddenly remembered.)}
{align:justify|So I began reciting the Cundi Mantra dedicated to my father, also urging and teaching him to recite it — but for one so dizzy and blurry-eyed, it was hard to take in and felt far too long. Though he strained to memorize, progress crawled. Hearing me say: "This mantra needs two to four hundred thousand recitations to work"... my father's face fell disappointed — learning by rote so slowly, in what year and month could he ever finish? So I thought of another way: I taught my father to recite Amitabha Buddha! But all his life my father had held hardly any faith, so Buddhist practice this way felt equally strange. When I came home to press him, he would recite a line or two — the moment I left, he forgot everything... truly worrying!}
{align:justify|At my wits' end, I suddenly recalled Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva's great vows and fetched two "Ksitigarbha Sutras" — one for my father, one to read myself. (Shame on me again: I had long heard Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva's name, but opening the sutra to read — this was the first time.)}
{align:justify|I began reciting, silently contemplating, dedicating the merit to my father and to his creditors of many lives and eons. But my office workload was heavy, and home I still had to tend a boy past one year old — so evenings left me exhausted; after two days my perseverance ran out, while the Cundi Mantra (perhaps from too few recitations) drew only a small response — my father's illness improved somewhat, now worse, now better, unclear...}
{align:justify|My father — first, from dizziness and blurred eyes; second, from finding the scriptures obscure — grew bored and listless, managing a dozen-odd pages before he could turn no more...}
{align:justify|Last Friday I visited my father; having not slept the night before, his health was terrible; grasping my hand, he lamented:}
{align:justify|"I think I'm done for — can't sleep, illness worsening, dizzier than ever. I won't pull through..."}
{align:justify|Mother and I coaxed and comforted every way, yet could give him no faith or hope in life. Suddenly I remembered my father had complained the "Ksitigarbha Sutra" was beyond him, hence unrecitable.}
{align:justify|So I explained the sutra's meaning to him. (Merciful Buddha, I had only read a little more than my father — but luckily, having read Master Nan Huaijin's writings, I possessed a crude grasp of the Buddha Dharma.) So I read the sutra aloud to him, explaining as I went, sharing my limited understanding of the Buddhist sutras with my father. Perhaps father-son closeness eases understanding — my father found it "went in one ear" and grasped a little; I sensed he had provisionally understood this "Ksitigarbha Sutra" and given rise to right faith.}
{align:justify|Just the first chapter took me until noon to expound... By then my throat was hoarse and dry, while my father listened until... sleepy! He skipped lunch and went straight to sleep — over two hours, most peacefully, without a snore. Waking, my father even asked me:}
{align:justify|"Did I just sleep? Why does it feel like I lay down and woke at once?"}
{align:justify|Mother and I both laughed. Hearing his question gladdened us — months had passed without such a thing. Awake, my father looked cheery; he too felt his illness much lighter. Then he did something astonishing the whole family: while we went on napping, he went downstairs alone for a haircut. My mother first went looking and discovered it — seeing him standing at the gate chatting happily with someone, his face beaming, utterly unlike his former pessimism...}
{align:justify|Just reading the first chapter of the "Ksitigarbha Sutra" — and such merit? Honestly, I was amazed at Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva's unfathomable marvels! Then, too, tangles at the factory were settled with others' help, cheering my father further.}
{align:justify|From that day, my father daily, diligently and willingly recited the "Ksitigarbha Sutra" — never mind understanding or not, he just kept reciting. He even had me teach him the Cundi Mantra, holding the text, patiently memorizing word by word. Now he sleeps soundly, seldom dreaming, his spirits no longer gloomy. These changes move me beyond measure.}
{align:justify|"Homage to Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva — thank You for delivering the creditors who once tormented my father, granting him peace. Thank the Buddhas' power for compassionately blessing and guarding my father's restored health."}
{align:justify|To thank the Bodhisattva, one must learn from His vows; the best way to emulate Him is to reform the mind and recite the "Ksitigarbha Sutra," dedicating it to kin and to all beings... praying their evil karma dissolves and they come to know the Buddha Dharma...}
{align:justify|Liem-Minh}

{align:justify|In 2004 I was 32 years old. Before relating my own story, before the thousand Buddhas and Bodhisattvas of the ten directions... and all sentient beings, I sincerely repent.}
{align:justify|I now live in Meizhou city and once studied at United Middle School. Then I cared nothing for study — always ganging up, brawling and stirring trouble in society without cease. Teachers' and kinfolk's advice never reformed me; in the end I dropped out and hired myself out. I did every job in society, but worked fitfully — on and off — living day to day, never shedding bad habits, still running with so-called sworn brothers to steal come harvests and catch dogs, raising rowdy trouble and a most harmful influence...}
{align:justify|In those days I craved money and things, so I constantly cheated and stole; by nature cruel, loving to kill creatures, plus a hot temper and foul mouth cursing without stop — I steeped myself in the worst habits, worse still ganging with villains so evil tendencies grew ever deeper — and so I was constantly hauled into police stations; east, west, south and north posts all over Meizhou town — everywhere locked up for breaking the law.}
{align:justify|Relating this much, I silently repent to all beings I ever deliberately or unwittingly harmed, vowing never to repeat old faults... The bitter fruits my evil causes summon, I accept; may the Triple Gem of the ten directions and Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva bless all to liberation and swift supreme Bodhi...}
{align:justify|Now, recalling the evils I did (later becoming calamities) — wrecking both my work and health, meeting constant mishaps — all retribution! For "plant melons, reap melons; plant beans, reap beans"... Bitter fruits of my own making I myself must shoulder; none can bear them for me. Below I recount my Buddhist affinity:}
{align:justify|In autumn 2000, I witnessed a horror scene remembered all my life — through a hundred thousand kotis of eons hard to forget! Now I relate it so all may stay alert.}
{align:justify|Then I was unmarried. One night, very late, I went to bed and soon dozed into dreams. Dreaming, I saw myself with two friends walking into a gloomy, dreadful land. Suddenly a great flood struck; fleeing too late, the deluge swept us away... all three struggling in it, "crying to heaven and earth resounding" — all in vain.}
{align:justify|As in films we watch, in a blink the dream cut to another scene. I saw myself lying on a narrow little bed, feeling most uncomfortable; I struggled to break free — yet the more I struggled the more it hurt, aching till I moaned without cease. Suddenly I saw around me, boundless in every direction, countless single beds bearing countless people, moaning and writhing in unbearable pain — truly dreadful...}
{align:justify|In a blink, yet another scene: I and countless people penned in a wall-less-topped high city, monster-like black jailers guarding the walls, their looks most dreadful, wielding every weapon — clubs, spears, tridents... escorting, torturing, beating people.}
{align:justify|Then I ached so I wished not to live. Thinking: "Escape this place fast." So I strained with all my might to climb the wall and break out fleeing. Around me many fellow torture-mates... broke out like me. All fell into chaos; the monsters shouted and blustered, swinging clubs, maces and tridents in pursuit... I ran for my life... at last scaling the wall-top, looking beyond saw below a bottomless-seeming abyss; soon the monsters caught up, nimbly seizing me...}
{align:justify|I flung myself and... jumped! Feeling soul-scattered, I plunged straight into that bottomless abyss; at last my feet touched ground... I scrambled up and fled on, behind me monsters raising weapons and bellowing:}
{align:justify|"Khau Kien Hao! Stop! Seize Khau Kien Hao..."}
{align:justify|Chased too hard, I tired to exhaustion, limbs trembling, terrified and panicking utterly — when suddenly my back stabbed with piercing pain: a monster had hurled a trident pinning my back, still bellowing:}
{align:justify|"Seize him! Seize him!"}
{align:justify|I sprawled on the ground; monsters bound me tight and planted a great placard on my back lettered: KHAU KIEN HAO SHALL DIE! Then they tied me to a post; around me I saw a row of people suffering likewise. Next a monster raised an iron bucket before me and shouted:}
{align:justify|"KHAU KIEN HAO SHALL BE PUNISHED!"}
{align:justify|And tilting the bucket, poured it over my legs...}
{align:justify|Goodness, I saw flowing from the bucket some most strange liquid, bubbling and hissing, white smoke billowing — I felt horrible pain (as though splashed with acid or boiling copper). Then suddenly I thought: "Ah, I'll pay it no mind — I'll recite the 'Six-Syllable Great Bright Mantra.'"}
{align:justify|So I hastily recited without stop: "Án ma ni bát di hồng"... "Án ma ni bát di hồng"... the moment I recited, I knew nothing more of the terrors before me; the faster and louder I recited — for my lower body had dissolved away... nearly to the chest.}
{align:justify|Just then before me rang an (Great Bright Mantra recitation) most loud; turning, I saw ahead more than five children in lovely clothes, hand in hand in a row, bodies glowing light toward me, together reading the "Six-Syllable Great Bright Mantra" — deep, majestic, most beautiful to hear (by their looks, surely come to aid me?).}
{align:justify|"Án ma ni bát di hồng! Án ma ni bát di hồng! Án ma ni bát di hồng!..."}
{align:justify|My chest was gone too, then my neck — only a lump of white vapor left, bubbling and hissing. Nearly to the head... I desperately strained to recite, but voiceless — only moans: "Án ma ni bát di hồng"...}
{align:justify|Suddenly an "ÁN MA NI BÁT DI HỒNG!" extraordinarily mighty, resounding, solemn... reached my ears; before my eyes appeared a dazzling radiance: a shaven-headed figure in white robes, palms joined, hovering above... my bed's head. He showed half His body, leaning slightly toward me, utterly solemn in bearing. (Telling is slow, but then all was lightning-fast.) The mighty voice still rang loud; then I felt from the head downward... (the dissolved body restored whole) and my whole body unspeakably light and easeful. Vigorous life-force, full of energy, covered me entirely.}
{align:justify|I woke to find myself sitting on the bed and reached to switch on the lamp. Light. Then I was profoundly shaken — yet felt none of a nightmare's fear and panic, only gratitude, moved thankfulness... for living again, reborn from death! Trembling all over with feeling, the white-robed one had vanished utterly, nowhere to be seen... yet intuition told me plainly: thanks to that white-robed one, that "shaven head"... I was saved! He possessed most wondrous awesome power, granting me new life...}
{align:justify|I forced myself calm and steady, then straightened my clothes and, facing where the white-robed monk had just appeared, knelt and bowed three times. (Then my bed's head pointed west.)}
{align:justify|Told this far, surely some will ask:}
{align:justify|"How did you know the mantra? Did you normally worship the Buddha? Weren't you wholly evil? How could you believe in the Buddha and recite mantras right in dreams?"}
{align:justify|Well, this needs explaining: truly I share an extremely deep affinity with the "Six-Syllable Great Bright Mantra." Ten years ago, chancing upon a chanting recording of it, I at once loved it dearly — and so all these years, meaning to or not, I have recited and hummed it throughout.}
{align:justify|Though wholly ignorant of the Buddha Dharma, I sensed this mantra worked most well — proof being times working away from home when strange, acute, grave illnesses struck (like a heart gripped as though pricked by countless needles, or whole-body cramps and shivering)... single-mindedly reciting it freed me from pain. So normally, day or night, I always recite it, year-round unbroken. (Those who know the Way surely grasp this mantra's divine power better than I.)}
{align:justify|Back to the tale: afterward, I longed to learn which Bodhisattva that dream-appearing, miracle-working white-robed one was. Avalokiteshvara Bodhisattva? No — I had seen Her image. A Buddha? Likewise unlike!? I pondered endlessly without answer, then asked my family — but all mocked and laughed at me. They said sickness gave me nightmares and ignored my words; mention it further and they called me mad!}
{align:justify|Then I ached sorely, believed by none. Silently I searched, thinking: "The Bodhisattva appeared in the west"... and westward lies Buddha Light Temple... Just then the neighborhood called for aid to the under-construction Buddha Light Temple; I had passed it before, so I decided to visit, hoping for answers...}
{align:justify|So that day a friend and I went to Buddha Light Temple, just as the masters ascended the hall to recite. I thought: "I must ascend and recite with them"... That afternoon, after offering incense, the monks began reciting a very thick sutra titled "Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva's Fundamental Vows Sutra" — I recited along.}
{align:justify|Never having recited it before, I stumbled through with difficulty... yet I thought to persevere; my mouth went dry, hardly able to recite... I could only follow the text silently... and I got the shock of my life: the scenes the sutra describes matched exactly the dream-scenes I had seen.}
{align:justify|"In hell there are beds, filling everywhere; one offender sees his own body filling a bed; a thousand myriad offenders each see their own bodies filling beds."}
{align:justify|Heavens! Reciting this far, I shook uncontrollably with excitement; I sensed what I had labored to seek nearing fruition. Suddenly I recalled that day's extraordinarily scorching air — yet my whole body broke in cold sweat, for the scripture's description matched my dream-scenes too closely!}
{align:justify|Perhaps the volume was too thick (or for other causes unknown to me), for after half an hour the masters declared a rest. My heart panicked and lost, knowing not what to do — so I stepped out and loitered before the temple gate. Near the gate a monk chatted with several pilgrims; mustering all my courage, I approached and asked:}
{align:justify|"Venerable Master, once I dreamed a nightmare whose scenes match exactly the hell the sutra describes — yet afterward a monk appeared and saved me; then I recited the 'Six-Syllable Great Bright Mantra,' and that monk recited it too — are such things real?..."}
{align:justify|The monk looked at me and said:}
{align:justify|"This is no joking matter! You must not speak of it lightly!"}
{align:justify|I hastily asked:}
{align:justify|"Master, which Bodhisattva was the monk who saved me?"}
{align:justify|"When the time comes, you will understand yourself!..."}
{align:justify|That was all the monk answered. Then drums sounded within; I heard people say: "Sutra time."}
{align:justify|I felt most bewildered, but the monk had already gone inside, hearing no more of my questions. Owing to deep karmic obstacles, that day I gained few answers — only learning through the "Ksitigarbha Sutra" that the sutra's hell-scenes matched my dream-scenes exactly. I knew nothing of the "Ksitigarbha Sutra," nor who Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva was. But I guessed "perhaps Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva saved me" — though exactly how remained unclear.}
{align:justify|After a while, layman Hong telephoned me, saying:}
{align:justify|"Hao, I have a 'Ksitigarbha Sutra' — would you like to come see it?"}
{align:justify|I hurried to his place. He handed me an exquisitely printed "Ksitigarbha Sutra" and said:}
{align:justify|"Inside is a beautiful picture of Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva!"}
{align:justify|I opened it and looked — "Ah! This is him, it's him!"... Goodness! The monk of my dream... his looks exactly like this... "Oh!... Nam mô Địa Tạng Bồ-tát!" I was so moved I reverently raised the book above my head and sincerely recited Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva's name.}
{align:justify|I said to layman Hong:}
{align:justify|"May I take it home to read?"}
{align:justify|Layman Hong agreed.}
{align:justify|After bidding layman Hong farewell, home again, I opened the book and recited as in the temple. From then on, Brother Hong and I often visited Buddha Light Temple together to obtain sutras to read. There many laypeople guided and instructed me further, and I threw myself into Buddhist study.}
{align:justify|Layman Hong, two years my senior, and I both deeply realized: only studying the Dharma and practicing the Buddha's teaching is life's best road. We threw ourselves into Buddhist activities — diligently reciting sutras, releasing life... nurturing good roots, dissolving karmic obstacles, practicing hard.}
{align:justify|Layman Hong had planted deep good roots in past lives. At first he said: "To study Buddhism, first take refuge in the Triple Gem and build a sound foundation of faith"... he invited me to take refuge together, but I refused — so he took refuge first.}
{align:justify|Under Brother Hong's direction, plus my own unceasing study, faith in the Buddha Dharma swelled; I resolved to take refuge.}
{align:justify|Sun and moon shuttle like looms, time flies like arrows; human life is brief yet full of pain, life impermanent... The great Buddha has shown us a radiant path of practice. This is the road of happiness, beings' greatest fortune. We must treasure it and never lazily strive. Thought after thought in practice, to accomplish unsurpassed wisdom and repay the Buddhas' kindness. Before laying down my pen, I quote the Buddha's words entrusting the salvation of humans and gods... in the Ksitigarbha Sutra:}
{align:justify|..."Sentient beings are by nature unfixed, habituated to evil... Though they give rise to wholesome thoughts, in an instant they backslide — and meeting evil conditions, evil thoughts swiftly multiply. Therefore I must divide into hundreds of thousands of kotis of bodies, teaching beings according to their natures.}
{align:justify|O Ksitigarbha! Now I earnestly entrust the hosts of gods and humans to you. In future ages, if any gods, humans, good men or good women plant even a little good root within the Buddha Dharma — no more than a hair, a dust-mote, a grain of sand, a drop of water... you should support them with your powers of the Way, helping them gradually cultivate the Unsurpassed Path, never letting them backslide.}
{align:justify|If gods or humans must receive retribution according to karma and fall into evil destinies; just arriving at the threshold, if these beings can recite the name of one Buddha, one Bodhisattva, or recite a line or verse of Mahayana scripture — then use your divine powers and skillful means to rescue them, manifesting boundless bodies where they are, shattering hell for them, granting them wondrous, supreme joy."}
{align:justify|Reading this passage, let us calm our minds and reflect together — then we will understand why I was saved, and grasp Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva's awesome vow-power. He is most compassionate, of great divine power and inconceivable eloquence! The Bodhisattva stays ever beside us, mindful of us, never once forgetting His vows.}
{align:justify|Had I in this life not sincerely taken refuge in the Triple Gem and in Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva, resolved to reform and walk the Unsurpassed Path... then by the evil karma I created, once this human body ended, I would surely fall into the Uninterrupted Hell for ten thousand eons, hard to escape — for my offenses deserve such retribution!}
{align:justify|These last months of the year, I joyfully study the Way with many laypeople. Thanks to Dharma friends' teaching, practicing together with the lay community, I inwardly sense the group's guarding support extraordinarily strong and wholesome — ever moved, quietly glad Meizhou town has such a fine Way-place. Under the wise guidance of Abbot Minh Hue, he teaches the four assemblies to strive without laziness. People stream from everywhere to visit the temple, venerate the solemn Thousand-Buddha Stupa, listen to the great master's guidance, receive the right Dharma, purify hearts, wash away dust, and reveal Bodhi.}
{align:justify|From a fierce, vulgar unbeliever, thanks to the Buddha Dharma I have transformed and changed my whole life toward the good.}
{align:justify|May all who read my story gain much benefit.}
{align:justify|Khau Kien Hao}
{align:justify|May this merit,}
{align:justify|Adorn the Buddhas' Pure Lands,}
{align:justify|Above, repay the four heavy kindnesses.}
{align:justify|Below, rescue beings' sufferings.}
{align:justify|May all who read and see...}
{align:justify|Give rise to the Bodhi resolve,}
{align:justify|This one last body ended,}
{align:justify|Together born in the land of happiness.}
{align:justify|ENDNOTES}
{align:justify|The Four Clear Decisions on Purity.}
{align:justify|Beset by calamities, trapped and distressed, suffering.}
{align:justify|Lieu means "to understand, end, resolve, settle, repay in full"... therefore I keep it untranslated, letting readers interpret it better themselves.}
{align:justify|It means the whole family cultivates together — morning and evening practice, lifelong vegetarianism, following the Buddha's conduct, strictly keeping the five precepts, guarding body, speech and mind... developing wisdom and compassion, benefiting self and others, according to the Buddha Dharma.}
{align:justify|THREE PURE MEATS: three pure meats are meats meeting three conditions:}
{align:justify|Eyes did not see the killing.}
{align:justify|Ears did not hear the killing.}
{align:justify|It was not killed for one's own eating.}
